शिवसूत्राणि — THE ŚIVA SŪTRAS
Vasugupta’s Guide to Realising You’re Literally Śiva, You Absolute Donut

BOOK I — ŚĀMBHAVOPĀYA
The Way of Śiva: Your Consciousness Has Been Running the Entire Universe and Somehow You Didn’t Notice.

Right. We’re back in Kashmir.

Ninth century, give or take. The Himalayas. Snow-covered mountains. Ancient temples. A man named Vasugupta is about to receive what tradition describes as a revelation from Śiva himself.

According to one famous account, Śiva appears to Vasugupta in a dream and directs him towards a rock bearing a secret teaching.

And what’s this incredible revelation?

Seventy-seven tiny statements.

That’s it.

Twenty-two in the first section. Ten in the second. Forty-five in the third.

No elaborate mythology. No 600-page introduction. Just the metaphysical equivalent of a bloke walking into your house, informing you that you’ve misunderstood the entire nature of reality, and disappearing.

These are the Śiva Sūtras.

And unlike the Tantrasāra, which was written by Abhinavagupta as an extensive systematic exposition, this thing is absolutely compressed.

A single word can contain an entire metaphysics.

Fortunately, Abhinavagupta’s disciple Kṣemarāja wrote the Śivasūtravimarśinī, which helps unpack what on earth Vasugupta is on about.

We’re going through all 77 in their traditional order, with the authentic Sanskrit, accessible glosses, and an original, mildly unhinged narration grounded primarily in Kṣemarāja’s interpretation. The jokes are ours; the sūtras are real. Source Library +2

1.1 — The Biggest Plot Twist in Human History
चैतन्यमात्मा ॥१॥

Caitanyam ātmā.

Literal: Consciousness is the Self.

Ladies and gentlemen.

Vasugupta has entered the building.

And he has decided to open with a statement so outrageous that the rest of Indian philosophy is going to need a cigarette.

You are consciousness.

Not you possess consciousness.

Not your brain produces a lovely little subjective experience and that’s what you fundamentally are.

No.

The thing that you actually are — ātman — is caitanya.

Let’s unpack this carefully.

Caitanya comes from the Sanskrit family of cit, awareness or consciousness. But Kṣemarāja is not talking about ordinary, fluctuating mental awareness.

He’s talking about consciousness as fundamentally free, self-revealing, and endowed with unlimited powers of knowing and acting.

That last part is crucial.

In Trika, Śiva is not merely a cosmic spectator.

He’s not sitting in some metaphysical armchair thinking, “Lovely universe, shame I can’t do anything about it.”

He’s prakāśa, the luminous manifestation of reality, inseparable from vimarśa, its reflexive awareness of itself.

And that self-awareness is inseparable from svātantrya — absolute freedom.

So what does Vasugupta actually mean?

Your deepest nature is not a little individual observer trapped inside a body looking out at a separate universe.

Your deepest nature is the very conscious reality through which that entire distinction between observer and observed becomes manifest.

The universe isn’t an alien object standing opposite Śiva.

It’s Śiva expressing his own nature.

And you, apparently, have spent your entire life under the impression that you’re just some bloke with a calendar and a digestive system.

An extraordinary administrative error.

The philosophical point: In Trika metaphysics, individual consciousness is not ultimately separate from universal consciousness. Recognition, pratyabhijñā, means realising an identity that was never actually absent.

And that’s sūtra one.

He’s barely started.

1.2 — Congratulations, Your Knowledge Is the Problem
ज्ञानं बन्धः ॥२॥

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Literal: Knowledge is bondage.

Oh, brilliant.

We’ve gone from “you are infinite consciousness” to “knowledge is your prison”.

Thanks, Vasugupta. Shall I cancel university?

Not quite.

Here jñāna means limited, differentiated knowing — knowledge structured around the fundamental division:

I am here. That thing is over there.

You see a tree.

There is you, the perceiver, and the tree, the perceived.

You immediately organise reality into categories.

Me. Tree. Other people. My possessions. My memories. My terribly important opinions.

Now ordinary differentiated knowledge is useful. It stops you walking into trees.

But the problem begins when this limited standpoint is mistaken for your complete identity.

Kṣemarāja associates this with āṇavamala, the primordial contraction in which universal consciousness experiences itself as a limited individual.

Mala means impurity, but don’t imagine literal dirt covering a perfectly clean soul.

Think of it as a fundamental limitation of awareness.

The infinite experiences itself as finite.

The complete experiences itself as incomplete.

The universally free experiences itself as somebody who desperately needs everything to work out a particular way.

And here’s the extraordinary move.

From the Trika perspective, bondage doesn’t require a second power to capture Śiva.

Śiva’s own freedom makes contraction possible.

The prisoner, the prison, and the authority permitting the imprisonment are not ultimately three independent entities.

It’s Śiva.

Doing the entire performance himself.

The philosophical point: Bondage is not knowledge as such, but the limiting mode of knowing which obscures one’s undivided nature. Kṣemarāja also connects bondage to the failure to recognise nondual awareness.

1.3 — The Universe Has Three Different Ways to Ruin Your Day
योनिवर्गः कलाशरीरम् ॥३॥

Yonivargaḥ kalāśarīram.

Literal components: The source and its multiplicity; that whose nature is limited activity.

Vasugupta now expands the explanation of bondage.

The sūtra is extremely compact. Kṣemarāja reads it in connection with the previous statement, “Knowledge is bondage,” and elaborates the remaining forms of impurity.

Welcome to the three malas.

Āṇavamala — The impurity of smallness.

The fundamental feeling of incompleteness.

“I am only this.”

“I am not enough.”

“I am this little subject rather than the limitless whole.”

The infinite has apparently decided to identify as a fraction of itself.

Māyīyamala — The impurity of differentiation.

Now consciousness doesn’t merely experience itself as limited.

It experiences reality as divided into separate objects.

This is mine.

That’s yours.

That’s pleasant.

That’s disgusting.

That person disagrees with me and must therefore be metaphysically defective.

The world appears as a multiplicity of independently existing things.

Kārmamala — The impurity of limited agency and action.

Now the individual acts within the limitations it experiences, generating further karmic involvement.

“I did this.”

“I must obtain that.”

“I have to correct what happened yesterday.”

And the entire machinery of conditioned experience keeps operating.

Notice the elegance.

First, I am limited.

Then, the world is separate from me.

Then, I am a limited agent who must act within this separate world.

And suddenly you’ve got the entire human predicament.

The technical details of yoni, varga, and kalāśarīra belong to Trika’s theory of manifestation and its limited powers of agency, not merely a psychological metaphor. Kṣemarāja explicitly connects the sūtra with māyīyamala and kārmamala. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

1.4 — Your Alphabet Is Secretly Running the Show
ज्ञानाधिष्ठानं मातृका ॥४॥

Jñānādhiṣṭhānaṃ mātṛkā.

Literal: Mātṛkā is the foundation of knowledge.

Now things get properly tantric.

Mātṛkā means the little mother, or mother principle.

Here she is the power associated with the Sanskrit phonemes — the letters from a through kṣa — as the basis of differentiated cognition.

This isn’t just a bizarre claim that the alphabet invented your suffering.

The idea is much more sophisticated.

Language articulates experience.

Experience becomes conceptualised.

Conceptualisation helps establish distinctions.

Those distinctions become entangled with identity, memory, desire, aversion and the sense of being an individual subject surrounded by other things.

You perceive an event.

Then language arrives.

“This is terrible.”

“That person disrespected me.”

“I always fail.”

And an entire little world forms around the verbal construction.

You’ve transformed a passing occurrence into a personal cosmological crisis.

All before breakfast.

For Kṣemarāja, however, Mātṛkā is not intrinsically evil.

She is Śakti.

The very same power that generates limitation can, when recognised, become a means of liberation.

This is enormously important for understanding Trika’s fascination with Sanskrit phonemes, mantra, and the metaphysics of speech.

Speech isn’t just a tool for describing an independently established universe.

Within this tradition, it belongs to the very process by which consciousness manifests differentiated reality.

The mother who makes the prison is also the mother who holds the key.

Rather inconvenient for the prison authorities, who are also Śiva.

1.5 — The Sudden Cosmic “OH, FOR GOD’S SAKE”
उद्यमो भैरवः ॥५॥

Udyamo bhairavaḥ.

Literal: The arising, or upsurge, is Bhairava.

This is one of the most beautiful sūtras in the entire work.

Udyama comes from the sense of rising up, emerging, surging forth.

But Kṣemarāja interprets this as the sudden upsurge of supreme awareness.

Not laboriously manufacturing a spiritual experience.

Not sitting there aggressively trying to become God.

Not spending sixteen hours thinking, “Am I enlightened yet? How about now? Now?”

It’s the immediate arising of recognition.

An opening in which universal consciousness becomes apparent.

And that, says Vasugupta, is Bhairava.

Notice how radically different this is from a purely progressive model of spiritual accomplishment.

There may be preparation. There may be discipline. There may be years of practice.

But the recognition itself isn’t produced by assembling Śiva out of spare parts.

Śiva was never missing.

The limitation was in the mode of recognition.

Imagine somebody desperately searching their house for their spectacles.

They search every room.

Check every drawer.

Accuse the neighbours.

Begin questioning whether spectacles exist.

Then suddenly realise they’re wearing them.

“OH, YOU ABSOLUTE IDIOT.”

That’s not enlightenment, obviously.

But it illustrates the structure of recognition.

Nothing new had to be created.

The truth of the situation simply became apparent.

And in Trika, that flash is not merely a clever intellectual conclusion.

It’s the living manifestation of Bhairava.

1.6 — The Universe Gets Swallowed, but Don’t Worry About the Furniture
शक्तिचक्रसन्धाने विश्वसंहारः ॥६॥

Śakticakrasandhāne viśvasaṃhāraḥ.

Literal: Through union with the circle of powers, the universe is withdrawn.

Right.

Now we’re dissolving universes.

Six statements in.

Excellent pace.

Śakti is power.

Cakra is a wheel, circle, or collective.

Sandhāna means joining, connecting, or bringing into union.

Viśvasaṃhāra is the withdrawal or dissolution of the universe.

But we’re not saying the advanced yogi sits in his bedroom and Jupiter physically disappears.

Rather, Kṣemarāja is explaining the withdrawal of differentiated manifestation into its conscious source.

Consider all the powers constituting your experience.

Seeing.

Hearing.

Thinking.

Remembering.

Imagining.

Desiring.

Each appears as a distinct activity.

Ordinarily, attention follows these activities outward, towards their objects.

But what happens when consciousness recognises the shared reality of these powers?

The apparent separateness of their manifestations is reabsorbed into the unity from which they arise.

The universe is no longer experienced as something independent of the conscious reality manifesting it.

This is saṃhāra — withdrawal.

And it’s inseparable from manifestation.

Śiva expresses the universe through Śakti.

Śiva withdraws the universe through Śakti.

It’s not a one-way escape from a terrible material world.

It’s a dynamic movement of manifestation and reabsorption within consciousness itself.

Like a wave discovering that it was never separate from the ocean.

Except the ocean is conscious, infinitely free, and has been pretending to be every single wave.

Which is quite the hobby.

1.7 — Turns Out You’re Supposed to Be Awake While You’re Asleep
जाग्रत्स्वप्नसुषुप्तभेदे तुर्याभोगसम्भवः ॥७॥

Jāgratsvapnasuṣuptabhede turyābhogasambhavaḥ.

Literal: In the distinctions of waking, dreaming and deep sleep, the experience of the fourth is possible.

Now Vasugupta introduces a magnificent idea.

The ordinary person experiences three principal states.

Jāgrat — waking.

Svapna — dreaming.

Suṣupti — deep sleep.

You wake up.

There’s a world.

You go to sleep.

There’s another world.

You fall into dreamless sleep.

Apparently, there’s no world at all.

Then you wake up again and go, “Bloody hell, where have eight hours gone?”

But across these changing conditions, the tradition introduces a fourth principle.

Turya.

Literally, “the fourth”.

A beautifully understated name for something of such metaphysical significance.

Didn’t call it the Great Golden Celestial Palace of Eternal Transcendent Consciousness.

Just “number four”.

Turya is the underlying nondual consciousness that can be recognised throughout waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

And this is where the teaching becomes extraordinarily subtle.

Because turya is not simply another mental state to be entered after the other three.

It is the conscious reality that remains fundamental regardless of their changing contents.

In waking, experiences appear through sensory cognition.

In dreaming, experiences appear through mental constructions.

In deep sleep, differentiated cognition is unmanifest.

But from the standpoint of Trika, the ultimate reality of consciousness is not exhausted by any of these states.

The accomplished yogi recognises that reality through their transformations.

Imagine three films being projected onto a screen.

An action film.

A romantic comedy.

And an entirely black film in which nothing happens.

The images differ.

But the screen is not destroyed whenever the film changes.

Now take that metaphor carefully: unlike an ordinary screen, Śiva is not an inert support. Śiva is self-aware and free.

The point is the continuity of the underlying reality, not an assertion that your personal waking mind remains fully alert throughout every period of sleep.

The remarkable claim: Liberation does not require fleeing from ordinary conscious life. The same fundamental reality can be recognised through every mode of experience.

1.8 — The Waking World Is Just One Department
ज्ञानं जाग्रत् ॥८॥

Jñānaṃ jāgrat.

Literal: Knowledge is waking.

Specifically, the relevant form of jñāna is cognition of the externally presented world.

You open your eyes.

There’s a cup.

There’s a table.

There’s a gecko on the wall looking suspiciously like he owns the property.

Everything appears organised into objects apprehended through the senses.

This is waking consciousness.

But do not confuse the waking state’s relative clarity with ultimate awakening.

You can be wide awake physically and completely unaware of your own fundamental nature.

You’ve got your eyes open.

You’ve got eighteen tabs open.

You’re analysing interest rates and arguing about whether somebody has used a Sanskrit compound correctly.

And the whole time, the consciousness in which all these things are appearing remains unrecognised.

You’re very busy knowing things.

But you’ve forgotten the knower.

1.9 — Dreams Are Your Mind Making Stuff Up
स्वप्नो विकल्पाः ॥९॥

Svapno vikalpāḥ.

Literal: Dreaming consists of conceptual constructions.

Vikalpa is a hugely important word.

It means conceptual construction, differentiation, imagination, or determinate mental representation, depending on context.

Your mind has an extraordinary ability.

It can construct an entire experienced world without corresponding external sensory objects.

You fall asleep.

Suddenly you’re late for a mathematics exam.

You’re naked.

Your childhood friend has become the prime minister of Bulgaria.

And none of this seems particularly unusual.

The mind is constructing appearances.

But Trika’s observation extends beyond sleeping dreams.

Much of ordinary waking experience is also shaped by conceptual constructions.

You remember an argument.

You imagine tomorrow.

You rehearse a conversation that hasn’t happened.

You construct an entire future in which someone rejects you.

Then you get upset about it.

The other person hasn’t even entered the imaginary conversation yet.

You’ve invented them, invented their words, invented your response, and somehow come out of the encounter emotionally exhausted.

Congratulations.

You’ve been defeated by your own screenplay.

The crucial distinction is that Vasugupta is classifying modes of experience, not claiming that waking sensory perception and dreams are empirically identical.

But the same manifesting consciousness is involved in both.

1.10 — Deep Sleep: No Thoughts, Still Not Enlightened
अविवेको मायासौषुप्तम् ॥१०॥

Aviveko māyāsauṣuptam.

Literal: Non-discernment is the deep sleep associated with Māyā.

Now we’ve reached the third condition.

No ordinary waking perception.

No elaborate dream imagery.

No conceptual soap opera.

Just profound non-discernment.

You might be tempted to conclude:

“Excellent. No thoughts, no suffering, no separation. Therefore deep sleep is enlightenment.”

Unfortunately, Vasugupta is not having it.

Because the absence of differentiated experience is not automatically the recognition of your true nature.

A person emerging from dreamless sleep ordinarily hasn’t acquired the knowledge:

“I am universal consciousness, intrinsically free, the source of manifestation.”

They’ve acquired the knowledge:

“Where’s my phone?”

The subtle distinction is between the absence of manifest differentiation and the positive recognition of nondual awareness.

One is a condition in which distinctions are unmanifest.

The other is recognition of the conscious reality underlying all distinctions, whether they are manifest or not.

And Māyā remains relevant because non-discernment does not itself undo the structures of limitation.

This is why Trika refuses to equate enlightenment with unconsciousness, blankness, or simply suppressing thought.

You cannot become Śiva by knocking yourself unconscious.

Terrible news for anyone hoping to attain liberation through an exceptionally long afternoon nap.

1.11 — Congratulations, You’re Now the Lord of the Three Worlds
त्रितयभोक्ता वीरेशः ॥११॥

Tritayabhoktā vīreśaḥ.

Literal: The experiencer of the three is the lord of heroes.

We’ve now moved through waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

And Vasugupta asks an obvious question.

What is the condition of someone who has realised turya throughout these three states?

The answer:

Vīreśa.

Lord of the heroes.

Now that’s a title.

No certificate.

No embossed membership card.

Just Lord of the Heroes.

The word vīra means hero, but in the tantric context it can also relate to the powers of the senses.

Kṣemarāja interprets the realised yogi as sovereign over these powers, enjoying the conscious reality underlying the three conditions.

Why sovereignty?

Because such a yogi no longer fundamentally mistakes the passing forms of experience for separate, self-existing authorities.

Waking occurs.

Dreaming occurs.

Deep sleep occurs.

The underlying conscious reality is not diminished by any of them.

There is an enormous philosophical difference between being absorbed into an experience and recognising the source through which it appears.

An ordinary person becomes caught up in the drama.

The realised person can participate without losing the recognition of their deeper nature.

And notice that the text says bhoktā — experiencer or enjoyer.

Not somebody permanently fleeing experience.

Not a cosmic hermit refusing all manifestation.

The world continues to appear.

But its meaning has changed.

The hero doesn’t necessarily stop watching the performance.

The hero understands the nature of the theatre.

1.12 — Enlightenment Is Apparently Going “WHAT THE ACTUAL HELL” Forever
विस्मयो योगभूमिकाः ॥१२॥

Vismayo yogabhūmikāḥ.

Literal: The stages of yoga are wonder.

This is absolutely gorgeous.

Vismaya means wonder, astonishment, amazement.

Yoga is union or spiritual integration.

Bhūmikāḥ are stages, levels, or grounds.

Now you might expect Vasugupta to give us a complicated hierarchy.

Level one: mild bliss.

Level two: unusual forehead sensations.

Level three: a very impressive dream about serpents.

Level four: you start wearing white linen.

No.

He says wonder.

Kṣemarāja explains that the extraordinary yogi encounters ever-renewed astonishment in the manifestation of reality.

Not because everything is mysterious in the sense of being incomprehensible.

But because consciousness continually reveals its own extraordinary nature.

What previously appeared mundane becomes marvellous.

A sound.

A colour.

The sensation of breathing.

The manifestation of a thought.

Each is an expression of the same inexhaustible reality.

The yogi discovers something rather surprising.

Recognition does not necessarily make the world less interesting.

It makes the world infinitely more astonishing.

Because the object is no longer merely an object.

It is an expression of Śakti.

And this is not wonder that depends on finding something new and exotic.

It’s wonder at what has been right in front of you the entire time.

Imagine a man studying a lamp.

He understands the electricity.

He understands the circuitry.

He understands the filament.

But suddenly there is an astonishment at the very fact that anything whatsoever manifests.

Not merely, “How does this device work?”

But, “What is this astonishing reality in which the device, the observer, and the act of knowing appear?”

The answer isn’t conceptual bewilderment.

It’s camatkāra — the aesthetic and experiential astonishment so beloved in the Kashmiri tradition.

Kṣemarāja even stresses that these stages are not simply a checklist of unusual yogic sensations. They concern the ever-fresh wonder of resting in one’s own conscious nature. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The universe has not become boring.

You have finally stopped taking its appearance for granted.

1.13 — Śiva’s Girlfriend Turns Out to Be Your Own Will
इच्छाशक्तिरुमा कुमारी ॥१३॥

Icchāśaktir umā kumārī.

Literal: The power of will is Umā, the Maiden.

Here comes Umā.

Śiva’s consort. The goddess.

And Vasugupta identifies her with icchāśakti — the power of will.

But don’t imagine ordinary personal wanting.

“I fancy some tea.”

“I want a nicer house.”

“I would quite like that person to reply to my message.”

Those are limited expressions of desire within differentiated existence.

The icchāśakti being described here belongs to the realised yogi whose will is united with the supreme freedom of consciousness.

Kṣemarāja associates this power with the divine sovereignty of Parabhairava.

And the word kumārī, maiden, matters.

It points towards the freshness, independence, and unsullied character of this divine power.

Kṣemarāja offers several interpretive possibilities, including her freedom from limiting associations and her identification with the cosmic play of manifestation and withdrawal. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Consider the three fundamental powers of Śakti:

Icchā — will.

Jñāna — knowledge.

Kriyā — action.

In ordinary life, they appear fragmented.

You want something.

You don’t know how to obtain it.

You try to act.

You fail.

You become frustrated.

You make tea.

In the metaphysical description of Śiva, however, will, knowledge and action are powers of the one free consciousness.

They are not independent faculties fighting over resources.

And the underlying freedom in which these powers belong is precisely what was introduced in the first sūtra.

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

Thirteen statements later, we’re discovering what that means for divine agency.

1.14 — Your Body Is the Entire Bloody Universe
दृश्यं शरीरम् ॥१४॥

Dṛśyaṃ śarīram.

Literal: The seen is the body.

Oh, this one’s magnificent.

Dṛśya — that which is seen or perceived.

Śarīra — body.

Vasugupta is saying that for the realised yogi, the entire field of perceptible manifestation is experienced as their body.

Not merely the collection of flesh and bones they happen to be inhabiting.

Everything.

The trees.

The rivers.

The mountains.

The sky.

Other people.

The entire manifest universe.

Now, before you go outside shouting, “THAT BUS IS MY LEFT ARM,” we need to understand the metaphysical claim.

Your ordinary body is experienced as intimately belonging to you.

Your hand does not ordinarily appear as a completely foreign object with which you have no relationship.

You experience it as part of your embodied existence.

For the liberated yogi, Kṣemarāja describes an analogous but vastly expanded identification.

The whole field of manifestation is recognised as inseparable from the universal consciousness that is one’s true nature.

This isn’t a claim that your individual organism physically extends to every planet.

Nor does it mean other persons cease to have their own lived perspectives.

It is a radical transformation of the fundamental distinction between self and world.

What was previously experienced as alien and external is recognised as manifestation within the one conscious reality.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly includes both internal and external appearances.

The physical body, too, is something perceived.

It is not the ultimate perceiver.

Which means that your ordinary body is itself part of the manifested field.

Not a prison containing consciousness.

An expression of consciousness.

The universe has become the body of Śiva.

And the realised yogi recognises it as such.

Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

1.15 — Put Your Mind Back Where It Came From, You Lunatic
हृदये चित्तसङ्घट्टाद्दृश्यस्वापदर्शनम् ॥१५॥

Hṛdaye cittasaṅghaṭṭād dṛśyasvāpadarśanam.

Literal: Through the merging of mind into the Heart, the true nature of the seen and the state of apparent void become visible.

Now Vasugupta is going to explain how the extraordinary recognition described in the previous sūtra becomes possible.

And the key word is hṛdaya.

The Heart.

Not merely the muscular organ pumping blood around your chest.

The Heart in Trika is the centre, the innermost reality of consciousness.

The source from which manifestation arises.

Everything you’ve ever experienced has appeared in awareness.

Every sound.

Every image.

Every thought.

Every memory.

But ordinary attention continually moves towards the things appearing.

A noise appears.

You follow the noise.

A thought appears.

You follow the thought.

A sensation appears.

You construct a story about the sensation.

Before long, you’ve become entirely occupied with the contents of consciousness while ignoring the reality in which those contents appear.

The instruction involves cittasaṅghaṭṭa — bringing the mind into union with the Heart.

Instead of merely following the succession of objects, awareness becomes established in its own conscious ground.

And now something fascinating happens.

Dṛśya — the seen.

Svāpa — the condition of apparent absence or void.

Both are recognised in their underlying reality.

Presence and absence.

Manifestation and apparent emptiness.

The appearance of a universe and the disappearance of its differentiated contents.

Neither is ultimately independent of consciousness.

And this is a genuinely subtle point.

Because you might assume that the way to find consciousness is to remove every possible experience.

Close your eyes.

Stop thinking.

Become absolutely still.

Find a blank space.

And declare victory.

But Kṣemarāja doesn’t simply identify the void with the ultimate.

He describes the recognition of both manifested objects and the apparent void through their unity in conscious reality. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The point is not that consciousness is a special object hiding behind experience.

Nor is it a blankness that replaces experience.

It is the fundamental reality through which both appearing and disappearing are possible.

You’re not trying to manufacture the Heart.

You’re recognising what was already fundamental.

1.16 — You Are No Longer a Bound Little Animal
शुद्धतत्त्वसन्धानाद्वापशुशक्तिः ॥१६॥

Śuddhatattvasandhānād vāpaśuśaktiḥ.

Interpreted through Kṣemarāja: Through union with the pure principle, the power of bondage associated with the limited being is absent.

Right.

We have to talk about paśu.

Because in Śaiva terminology, paśu is the bound individual.

Literally, the word can mean animal or beast.

But don’t misunderstand this as an insult to animals.

It’s a technical designation for the individual limited by the bonds called pāśa.

The paśu experiences itself as separate, incomplete, and constrained.

And the tradition contrasts this with Pati — the Lord.

The bound being and the Lord.

Now, if Śiva were fundamentally separate from the individual, you’d have a terrible philosophical problem.

How does a finite being ever become truly infinite?

How does something inherently limited acquire the nature of the limitless?

You can’t simply glue infinity onto a finite object.

Very messy business.

But Trika’s answer is different.

The limitation is a contraction within the manifestation of consciousness.

It is not the ultimate nature of consciousness.

Through śuddhatattvasandhāna, union with the pure principle — identified by Kṣemarāja with Paramaśiva — the binding power no longer governs the yogi’s identity.

And notice the word vā.

“Or.”

Kṣemarāja introduces this as another means of understanding the attainment described previously.

The yogi recognises the world as inseparable from the pure reality of Śiva.

The power of paśutva, bound individuality, is overcome.

In the language of recognition:

You do not turn a fundamentally non-Śiva entity into Śiva.

You recognise that the apparently limited individual was never ultimately separate from Śiva.

Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The chains weren’t another cosmic being’s property.

They were part of the Lord’s own self-concealment.

And now the self-concealment is being recognised.

1.17 — Your New Thought Is “I AM ŚIVA”, but Please Don’t Become Insufferable
वितर्क आत्मज्ञानम् ॥१७॥

Vitarka ātmajñānam.

Literal: Reflective ascertainment is knowledge of the Self.

Now this one’s particularly interesting because we’ve just spent half the text discussing how conceptual knowledge can constitute bondage.

And suddenly Vasugupta says a form of reflective understanding is Self-knowledge.

WHAT?

I thought thinking was the problem.

Apparently we’re thinking again?

The crucial distinction concerns what the thinking reveals.

Kṣemarāja glosses vitarka here as the firm reflection:

Viśvātmā śiva evāsmi.

“I am indeed Śiva, whose Self is the universe.”

This is Kṣemarāja’s explanatory Sanskrit, rather than an additional Śiva Sūtra. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The statement is extraordinary.

The limited person ordinarily thinks:

“I am this particular organism.”

“I am this mind.”

“I am separate from the rest of existence.”

But now understanding reverses the structure of identification.

The yogi recognises their deepest identity with the consciousness whose manifestation is the universe.

And importantly, this does not license the individual ego to proclaim unlimited personal authority.

It isn’t:

“My personal opinions are infallible because I’m God.”

That’s merely the old limited ego putting on a particularly extravagant crown.

Rather, the point is that the fundamental subject is no longer mistaken for the contracted personality.

The recognition is not the inflation of the little self.

It is the recognition that the little self was never the complete Self.

And here’s the beautiful philosophical reversal.

Earlier:

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Knowledge is bondage.

Now:

Vitarka ātmajñānam.

A particular form of reflective understanding is knowledge of the Self.

Knowledge that reinforces limitation binds; understanding that discloses one’s universal nature liberates.

Same broad territory of cognition.

Completely different orientation.

It’s almost as though the problem wasn’t thinking.

It was misunderstanding what you are.

1.18 — The Entire World Has Become Your Meditation
लोकानन्दः समाधिसुखम् ॥१८॥

Lokānandaḥ samādhisukham.

Literal: The bliss of the world is the happiness of samādhi.

Oh, this is beautiful.

Consider what many people imagine meditation to be.

You sit down.

Close your eyes.

Withdraw from the horrible noisy world.

Eventually, if you’re lucky, you get a special experience of peacefulness.

Then somebody starts drilling next door.

And your enlightenment has been cancelled.

The neighbour’s power tools have defeated the Absolute.

A tragic outcome.

But Vasugupta is describing something more radical.

Loka — the world, understood through the field of knowable objects and their experiencers.

Ānanda — bliss.

Samādhi — absorption, integration, collectedness.

Sukha — happiness or delight.

For the yogi established in recognition, the very appearance of the world is inseparable from the delight of conscious reality.

This doesn’t mean every worldly event becomes conventionally pleasant.

Pain can still be pain.

Loss can still be loss.

The claim is that the underlying conscious reality is not separated from its manifestations by the ordinary division between meditation and daily life.

The yogi doesn’t necessarily have to escape the world to recover their fundamental nature.

The world itself becomes an occasion for recognition.

Kṣemarāja connects this to resting in the position of the perceiver while the relation between experiencer and experienced remains manifest. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Imagine sitting beside a river.

A bird calls.

The water moves.

The air touches your skin.

A thought appears.

Normally, you might interpret these as separate things happening to a particular individual.

But in the nondual recognition described here, their entire manifestation is apprehended within the indivisible reality of consciousness.

The river is not merely an obstruction to meditation.

The bird is not interrupting your spiritual attainment.

The world is not an unfortunate distraction from Śiva.

It is Śiva’s own manifestation.

This is why Trika is so radically affirmative.

It is not primarily trying to get rid of existence.

It is trying to recognise its deepest nature.

And now we arrive at a rather unexpected turn.

Because Vasugupta is about to start talking about supernatural powers.

Naturally.

1.19 — Oh Brilliant, Apparently You Can Create Bodies Now
शक्तिसन्धाने शरीरोत्पत्तिः ॥१९॥

Śaktisandhāne śarīrotpattiḥ.

Literal: Through union with Śakti, there is the production of bodies.

Right.

Well.

We’ve had a lovely philosophical discussion about consciousness.

We’ve examined bondage, recognition, meditation, the three states, the divine powers, and the unity of the universe.

And Vasugupta has now decided that we’re going to start creating bodies.

Of course we are.

This is Tantra.

At some point, the metaphysics was always going to become absolutely outrageous.

First, the traditional explanation.

Kṣemarāja links this directly to the icchāśakti described in 1.13.

Through profound union with divine will, the realised yogi is said to gain the capacity to manifest a body according to desire.

This is part of what traditional texts call vibhūti or siddhi — extraordinary spiritual powers.

And Kṣemarāja really does interpret the statement as a supernatural accomplishment. He is not merely describing ordinary creativity, imagination, or a poetic change in self-image. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

So we shouldn’t pretend the text says something modest just because the literal claim makes modern readers uncomfortable.

At the same time, this is a traditional metaphysical and yogic claim, not an experimentally demonstrated human capacity.

Now why would such a claim appear here?

Because the entire first section has been describing a transformation from contracted agency to identification with universal consciousness.

In ordinary life, our capacity to act is restricted.

We can imagine countless things that we cannot actually produce.

But divine svātantrya, in the Trika system, is not restricted by those ordinary limitations.

The siddhi claims are expressions of that metaphysical distinction.

You can follow the philosophical logic without assuming that a contemporary practitioner can literally conjure an additional human organism into existence.

And perhaps the funniest thing is that this apparently astonishing power receives only one sūtra.

Vasugupta mentions the creation of bodies.

Then immediately moves on.

As though he’s just told you to remember to buy milk.

1.20 — Congratulations, You’re Now Playing Minecraft With Reality
भूतसन्धानभूतपृथक्त्वविश्वसङ्घट्टाः ॥२०॥

Bhūtasandhāna-bhūtapṛthaktva-viśvasaṅghaṭṭāḥ.

Literal components: The joining of existents, their separation, and the bringing together of the universe.

Here come more siddhis.

Three broad forms of extraordinary accomplishment are presented.

First, bhūtasandhāna.

The bringing together or integration of existent things.

Second, bhūtapṛthaktva.

Their separation or differentiation.

Third, viśvasaṅghaṭṭa.

The bringing together of things throughout the universe.

Kṣemarāja associates these with powers affecting bodies and other entities, including their integration, separation, and the apprehension of otherwise distant things.

Yes, the actual traditional interpretation includes extraordinary abilities. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

But what is their philosophical significance?

Return to the beginning.

The universe manifests through Śakti.

Differentiation arises through the powers of consciousness.

And limitations associated with space, time, agency and knowledge constitute the contracted experience of the individual.

From the standpoint of the tradition, a yogi established in the supreme source of those powers is no longer understood as subject to ordinary limitation in the same way.

Hence the claims about siddhis.

But there is an important hierarchy here.

The extraordinary abilities are not the fundamental definition of liberation.

Recognition of one’s true nature is more fundamental than the acquisition of unusual capabilities.

Otherwise, the goal of spiritual life would simply become accumulating supernatural party tricks.

You could assemble worlds, separate elements, perceive distant things—

And still spend your afternoon being annoyed because somebody didn’t reply to your message.

Quite an achievement.

1.21 — You’ve Been Promoted to Manager of the Entire Śakti Department
शुद्धविद्योदयाच्चक्रेशत्वसिद्धिः ॥२१॥

Śuddhavidyodayāc cakreśatvasiddhiḥ.

Literal: Through the arising of pure knowledge, mastery over the circle of powers is attained.

Now Vasugupta connects spiritual sovereignty to śuddhavidyā.

Pure knowledge.

Importantly, this is not simply a person becoming exceptionally well educated.

Nor is it a collection of correct philosophical opinions.

Śuddhavidyā is also a technical category within the Trika system of the thirty-six tattvas.

The five pure tattvas are:

Śiva
Śakti
Sadāśiva
Īśvara
Śuddhavidyā
They describe pure modalities of consciousness and its manifestation before the limitations associated with Māyā and the contracted individual.

Within this pure domain, the relation between aham — I — and idam — this — is not experienced through the same fundamental alienation that characterises ordinary differentiated existence.

And that’s the essential point.

At the beginning, the individual was bound by limited knowing.

Now pure knowledge has arisen.

The yogi recognises the fundamental relation between the conscious subject and its powers of manifestation.

And this leads to cakreśatva.

Lordship over the circle of powers.

Remember our previous encounter with śakticakra?

The wheel or collective of powers.

At 1.6, their union was associated with the withdrawal of the universe.

Here, the rise of pure knowledge is associated with mastery over them.

The progression is deliberate.

The powers that once seemed to scatter your consciousness into countless apparently separate objects are no longer experienced as fundamentally alien to their source.

The master of the powers is not an independent ego controlling an external collection of cosmic instruments.

The sovereign reality is Śiva himself.

And the realised yogi recognises their identity with that reality.

1.22 — The Great Lake, and Why Mantras Are More Than Just Words
महाह्रदानुसन्धानान्मन्त्रवीर्यानुभवः ॥२२॥

Mahāhradānusandhānān mantravīryānubhavaḥ.

Literal: Through union with the Great Lake comes the experience of the potency of mantra.

And here we are.

The final sūtra of the first section.

And Vasugupta ends with an absolutely beautiful image.

Mahāhrada.

The Great Lake.

Mahā — great.

Hrada — lake, deep pool, or reservoir.

Anusandhāna — sustained connection, contemplation, or identification.

Mantravīrya — the potency, vital efficacy, or generative power of mantra.

Anubhava — direct experience.

Now think about what a mantra ordinarily seems to be.

A collection of sounds.

Perhaps a Sanskrit phrase.

You pronounce it.

You repeat it.

You listen to the vibration.

But Trika has a much deeper philosophy of mantra.

Remember sūtra 1.4?

Mātṛkā.

The mother power of the phonemes.

The alphabet as an expression of Śakti and the ground of differentiated cognition.

Well, now we’re at the other end of the journey.

The first encounter with Mātṛkā revealed how differentiated knowledge participates in bondage.

But the final sūtra describes the power of mantra when recognised through its supreme source.

Kṣemarāja identifies the Great Lake with the supreme divine consciousness, Parā Śakti, whose manifestations extend throughout the entire universe.

The mantravīrya is not merely the mechanical acoustic effect of pronouncing certain syllables.

It is the potency of mantra understood through the conscious power from which it arises. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And this changes the meaning of sacred speech.

The mantra is not fundamentally a little verbal machine that forces reality to obey you.

Its highest efficacy, within this system, is inseparable from recognition of Śakti.

The power of the sound is rooted in the power of consciousness.

Think about the relationship between a river and its source.

Downstream you encounter the flowing water.

Its movements.

Its branching channels.

Its individual forms.

But by following its origin, you arrive at the reservoir from which the movement proceeds.

The Great Lake.

In the same way, the manifested phonemes, the mental activities, and the differentiated forms of knowledge can be traced back to their conscious source.

And there, the yogi experiences the potency underlying mantra.

The teaching has travelled from Mātṛkā as the basis of bondage to mantra as an expression of recognised divine power.

The same Śakti.

First misunderstood.

Then recognised.

The mother hasn’t changed.

Your relation to her has.

The Great Reveal — What Did Vasugupta Actually Just Tell Us?
Let’s look at the structure of the first twenty-two sūtras.

Because this is not a random collection of spiritual one-liners.

It’s an extraordinarily compressed progression.

Sūtras 1–4: The diagnosis.

Your true nature is consciousness.

Limited knowing produces bondage.

The three malas explain the forms of contraction.

Mātṛkā helps explain how differentiated cognition is structured.

In other words:

You’re Śiva, but you’re experiencing yourself as a limited individual in a divided world.

Sūtras 5–7: The breakthrough.

The immediate upsurge of awareness is Bhairava.

The universe is withdrawn into its conscious source.

Turya becomes recognisable throughout the three ordinary states.

In other words:

Recognition isn’t the creation of a new Self.

It’s the disclosure of the reality that was never absent.

Sūtras 8–12: What consciousness is doing.

Waking.

Dreaming.

Deep sleep.

The sovereignty of the realised experiencer.

The ever-renewed wonder of recognition.

In other words:

Everything changes, but the fundamental nature of consciousness isn’t exhausted by those changes.

Sūtras 13–18: The transformation of experience.

Will is recognised as divine Śakti.

The entire seen universe becomes the yogi’s body.

Mind unites with the Heart.

Bondage loses its governing power.

The yogi recognises their identity with Śiva.

The world’s manifestation becomes inseparable from the bliss of samādhi.

In other words:

Liberation is not fundamentally the rejection of manifestation.

It is its recognition.

Sūtras 19–22: The powers of consciousness.

The tradition describes extraordinary accomplishments arising through union with Śakti.

Pure knowledge confers mastery over the wheel of powers.

And union with the Great Lake reveals the potency of mantra.

In other words:

The very Śakti that made limitation possible is also the power through which liberation is recognised.

And that brings us right back to the original statement.

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

The entire first section has been an exploration of those two words.

Not bad, Vasugupta.

Not bad at all.

End of the First Awakening — Śāmbhavopāya.

Next comes Book II: Śāktopāya — The Way of Śakti, beginning with:

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

“The mind is mantra.”

Which is a spectacular statement, considering Vasugupta has just spent twenty-two sūtras explaining why ordinary mental limitation is the problem.

Apparently we’re about to find out how the very mind that binds you can become an instrument of liberation.

And we’re getting into mantra, effort, the guru, Mātṛkā, sacrificial offering, and the rather wonderful declaration that knowledge is food.

Ten sūtras. An entire theory of the transformative power of awareness.

Textual basis: Vasugupta’s 22 Śāmbhavopāya sūtras, read in sequence with Kṣemarāja’s Śivasūtravimarśinī. Sanskrit is attested; the English narration and jokes are original explanatory paraphrase, not quotations from the commentary. Minor textual variants occur across editions.

