शिवसूत्राणि — THE ŚIVA SŪTRAS
Book II: Śāktopāya — The Way of Śakti

How to Turn Your Own Mind into a Divine Weapon, Discover That the Alphabet Is Literally a Goddess, and Finally Stop Being Such a Cosmically Confused Little Bastard.

Previously, on the Śiva Sūtras…
Right.

Welcome back to Kashmir.

When we last encountered our spiritual protagonist Vasugupta, he’d made twenty-two rather explosive statements about the nature of reality.

We discovered that consciousness is the Self.

That limited knowledge is bondage.

That the entire universe is the body of Śiva.

That enlightenment involves an extraordinary sense of wonder.

And that, under certain rather ambitious tantric interpretations, a sufficiently accomplished yogi may possess supernatural powers.

All perfectly reasonable.

But then Vasugupta ended the first book with something particularly intriguing.

महाह्रदानुसन्धानान्मन्त्रवीर्यानुभवः।

Mahāhradānusandhānān mantravīryānubhavaḥ.

Through union with the Great Lake comes the experience of the potency of mantra.

The Great Lake.

The unfathomable reservoir of consciousness.

The divine source of every sound, every thought, every manifestation.

And now, naturally, we’ve got a question.

If the power of mantra comes from consciousness…

What the bloody hell actually is a mantra?

Is it a magic word?

A sequence of Sanskrit syllables?

A cosmic password?

Does Śiva have some sort of metaphysical voice-recognition software?

Do you chant Oṃ Namaḥ Śivāya fourteen thousand times and suddenly receive administrative privileges over the universe?

Well.

Vasugupta is about to disappoint everyone who’s purchased a very expensive singing bowl.

Because the answer is considerably more interesting.

Welcome to Śāktopāya.

The way of Śakti.

Whereas Śāmbhavopāya centres on the immediate upsurge of recognition through divine will, Śāktopāya works through the power of awareness, understanding, contemplation and the transformation of cognition.

It’s not merely thinking about Śiva.

It’s discovering what thinking actually is when illuminated by its own conscious source.

And this is where Kṣemarāja, writing his Śivasūtravimarśinī, really gets going.

Ten sūtras.

One extraordinarily sophisticated theory of mantra, language, consciousness and liberation.

All ten Sanskrit formulations below follow the traditional Śāktopāya sequence; the explanations follow Kṣemarāja rather than treating the brief aphorisms as self-explanatory. Sanskrit Documents +1

2.1 — Congratulations, Your Mind Is Now a Mantra
चित्तं मन्त्रः ॥१॥

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

Literal: The mind is mantra.

Ladies and gentlemen.

He’s done it again.

Two words.

Two bloody words.

And the entire philosophy of mantra has just been turned upside down.

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Now, hang on.

Didn’t we just spend the entire first book explaining that ordinary cognition produces bondage?

Wasn’t differentiated knowledge responsible for the whole miserable business of individual limitation?

And now we’re being told that the mind is a sacred instrument of liberation?

What’s going on?

Has Vasugupta forgotten what he wrote?

Has the manuscript been assembled in the wrong order?

Is Śiva having an administrative disagreement with himself?

No.

This is the central brilliance of Śāktopāya.

The same consciousness that appears contracted into ordinary mental activity can be recognised in its divine nature.

But Kṣemarāja immediately makes an important qualification.

He isn’t saying every random mental fluctuation automatically functions as a liberating mantra.

Your thoughts about what to eat for dinner are not necessarily the supreme mantra.

Neither is mentally rehearsing a conversation in which you absolutely destroy somebody who disagreed with you three years ago.

Although, admittedly, that can feel rather powerful.

The relevant citta is consciousness actively engaged in contemplating the supreme reality.

Let’s take the Sanskrit apart.

Cit — consciousness, awareness.

Citta — mind or mental consciousness, particularly in its active, modified condition.

Mantra — a sacred formula, but also understood traditionally through the acts of contemplation and protection.

Kṣemarāja gives a characteristic tantric explanation connecting mantra with:

Manana — inward contemplation.

Trāṇa — protection or deliverance.

This is a traditional interpretive derivation, not a claim that the two words mechanically combine according to ordinary Sanskrit morphology.

But the philosophical meaning is fantastic.

The mantra is that through which contemplation becomes liberating.

Liberating from what?

From the fundamental misidentification that makes you experience yourself as a finite subject surrounded by an entirely separate world.

Here’s the really important bit.

Kṣemarāja insists that the efficacy of mantra does not ultimately reside in an arrangement of letters considered separately from consciousness.

The living power of mantra is realised through identification with the conscious reality of its deity.

And this is an absolutely enormous claim.

Imagine chanting:

ॐ नमः शिवाय।

Oṃ namaḥ śivāya.

You can pronounce those sounds mechanically.

You can repeat them while making breakfast.

You can count them on a string of beads.

You can construct an exceptionally complicated spreadsheet documenting your progress.

Very impressive.

Ten thousand repetitions.

Beautiful chart.

Excellent consistency.

But what if your fundamental understanding remains:

“I am a small individual over here, desperately attempting to contact a distant divine being over there”?

Then the deepest nondual meaning of the mantra has not yet been recognised.

For Kṣemarāja, the culmination involves recognising the non-separation of the practitioner, mantra and deity.

The sound is not merely sent towards Śiva.

The very awareness in which the mantra appears is recognised as Śiva’s own conscious power.

That’s the transformation.

And notice how this relates to the previous book.

At 1.4:

ज्ञानाधिष्ठानं मातृका।

Jñānādhiṣṭhānaṃ mātṛkā.

Mātṛkā is the foundation of differentiated knowledge.

Language participates in the structure of limitation.

But now:

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind itself becomes mantra when its activity is directed towards recognition of its supreme source.

The problem was never that consciousness possessed the ability to articulate experience.

The problem was the failure to recognise the true nature of that articulation.

The same Śakti binds.

The same Śakti liberates.

An extraordinary business model.

She’s manufacturing the locks and distributing the keys.

And the keys, apparently, were made from the same material as the locks.

Kṣemarāja’s point: The mantra’s highest power is not mere pronunciation but contemplative identification with the divine consciousness embodied in it. He supports this reading with tantric scriptural passages and the Spandakārikā. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

2.2 — Stop Trying So Hard, but Also Don’t Stop Trying
प्रयत्नः साधकः ॥२॥

Prayatnaḥ sādhakaḥ.

Literal: Effort is effective in accomplishment.

Right.

Now we’re discussing effort.

Which is already slightly concerning.

Because every spiritual tradition seems to alternate between two completely contradictory instructions.

“Make an enormous effort.”

“Stop making an effort.”

“Practise constantly.”

“There’s nothing to attain.”

“Apply yourself.”

“Let go.”

And the poor practitioner is sitting in the middle, thinking:

CAN SOMEBODY PLEASE TELL ME WHAT I’M SUPPOSED TO BE DOING WITH MY EYEBROWS?

Fortunately, Vasugupta is talking about a rather specific kind of effort.

Prayatna — effort, exertion, endeavour.

Sādhaka — that which accomplishes, or the effective means of attainment. The word also commonly denotes a practitioner, but here Kṣemarāja emphasises the efficacy of the effort.

Now, when Kṣemarāja explains this, he doesn’t simply recommend forcing the mind into submission through sheer muscular determination.

He describes an effort rooted in the spontaneous arising of the desire to contemplate the mantra’s true nature.

This is extraordinarily subtle.

Because we’re dealing with the relationship between icchā, jñāna and kriyā.

Will.

Knowledge.

Action.

Imagine you’re contemplating Śiva.

Perhaps repeating a mantra.

Perhaps reflecting on the nature of awareness.

Suddenly there’s a genuine movement towards recognition.

Not merely the thought, “I should meditate because my schedule says so.”

But a living impulse towards the source of awareness.

An interest so immediate that the mind naturally turns towards it.

Kṣemarāja says the practitioner must firmly seize this arising.

And he illustrates the process with imagery taken from the Tantrasadbhāva.

A bird sees food and moves towards it with spontaneous intensity.

An arrow, released from its bow, travels towards its target.

The point isn’t to strain continuously.

It’s that a particular, concentrated movement of attention can arise with remarkable naturalness.

And the practitioner follows it.

This is the difference between distracted, mechanical effort and living contemplative engagement.

Consider trying to remember somebody’s name.

You sit there.

“What’s his name?”

“Starts with an M.”

“No, perhaps a B.”

You force it.

Nothing.

Then something shifts.

A connection appears.

Suddenly the name becomes obvious.

The breakthrough isn’t merely the product of pushing harder.

But neither is it completely unrelated to the preceding attention.

There was a directed engagement, and then an opening.

That’s only an analogy, not an equation between memory recall and divine recognition.

But it captures something about the interplay between effort and spontaneous illumination.

Kṣemarāja is particularly interested in directing the mind towards bindu, here understood as the undivided light of consciousness.

The effective effort does not consist in collecting more mental objects.

It moves towards the conscious source.

And the ultimate result is identification with the deity of the mantra.

The practitioner, the contemplation, and its supreme object cease to be apprehended as fundamentally separate.

So the advice is not:

“Just relax, mate, you’re already Śiva.”

Nor is it:

“Clench every spiritual muscle until enlightenment pops out.”

It’s something closer to:

When the movement towards recognition arises, enter it wholeheartedly.

Don’t get distracted.

Don’t substitute a theory of recognition for recognition itself.

Don’t spend the entire meditation writing an imaginary academic paper about what the meditation would mean if you were actually doing it.

The Sanskrit is prayatna.

Not procrastinationa.

Which, regrettably, is not an attested tantric category.

Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

2.3 — The Secret of Mantra Is That You’ve Been Worshipping the Alphabet Without Knowing Its Mother
विद्याशरीरसत्ता मन्त्ररहस्यम् ॥३॥

Vidyāśarīrasattā mantrarahasyam.

Interpretive gloss: The conscious being underlying the knowledge-formed body of mantra is its secret.

Oh, now we’re getting somewhere.

Vidya.

Śarīra.

Sattā.

Mantra.

Rahasya.

Let’s get the Sanskrit properly unpacked, because this is an absolutely gorgeous compound.

Vidyā — knowledge, particularly liberating or pure knowledge in this context.

Śarīra — body, embodiment.

Sattā — existence, being.

Mantra — sacred formula.

Rahasya — secret, mystery, hidden essence.

Kṣemarāja interprets vidyāśarīra as that whose essential body consists of supreme nondual knowledge.

And the sattā, the conscious being of that knowledge-body, is the luminous power of pure I-consciousness.

This is the secret of mantra.

Now, an ordinary person sees a written mantra.

A series of letters.

Perhaps:

न मः शि वा य

Na — maḥ — śi — vā — ya.

Five syllables in the traditional namaḥ śivāya formulation, with namaḥ sandhi and pronunciation treated according to the chant.

There’s a sequence.

The sounds occur one after another.

Your mouth moves.

Your tongue changes position.

The sound resonates.

And you might naturally conclude that the mantra consists fundamentally of those audible units.

But Kṣemarāja wants us to look deeper.

The syllables belong to Mātṛkā, the divine matrix of phonemes.

And the phonemes are expressions of Śakti.

So what ultimately gives the mantra its sacred identity?

Not merely its acoustic properties.

Not merely a collection of letters.

But the conscious power manifested through them.

This is where the tantric philosophy of speech becomes absolutely outrageous.

Because language isn’t treated simply as a human invention for labelling pre-existing things.

It is understood through a cosmological theory in which the unfolding of speech corresponds to the unfolding of manifestation.

The phenomenal world appears articulated through innumerable distinctions.

And speech expresses, organises, and, within this tantric metaphysics, participates in the very manifestation of those distinctions.

Now, before everyone starts claiming Sanskrit is physically generating hydrogen atoms, we need to be precise.

This is a metaphysical doctrine of consciousness and manifestation.

It isn’t a laboratory demonstration that pronouncing a vowel creates an element of the periodic table.

But within the Trika system, there is a deep relationship between the powers of conscious manifestation and the articulated structure of language.

And Kṣemarāja supports his explanation with an astonishing passage from the Tantrasadbhāva.

He describes Śakti in her sleeping, coiled condition.

The potential universe lies within her.

The powers associated with bindu, the divine point, and visarga, emission, participate in the manifestation of the phonemes.

The letters appear through increasingly differentiated powers.

And the many forms of speech and manifestation arise from the one Śakti.

This passage involves Kuṇḍalinī, subtle sound, the arrangement of letters, divine powers, and the emergence of multiplicity.

It’s not a casual metaphor casually added by Kṣemarāja.

It’s part of the actual scriptural material he deploys to explain the secret of mantra. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Think of a piece of music.

You can analyse its individual notes.

Their frequencies.

Their duration.

Their order.

You can write every note onto a page.

But the living experience of the music is not simply a pile of isolated acoustic measurements.

Now imagine a much more radical theory.

The very possibility of articulation, intelligibility, experience and manifestation belongs to one conscious power.

The notes are expressions of that power.

The listener is an expression of that power.

The experienced unity of the music is also an expression of that power.

That’s the kind of nondual move Kṣemarāja is making with mantra.

And the final secret?

The living divine power is not separate from the consciousness through which the mantra is contemplated.

This is why merely knowing the correct syllables is insufficient for the highest recognition.

You can possess the letters and remain ignorant of their source.

You can pronounce the mantra perfectly and still approach Śiva as an entirely separate deity.

You can become the world’s leading expert in the phonology of Oṃ Namaḥ Śivāya and spend your entire life wondering whether you’ve finally reached the divine.

While, according to the doctrine, the conscious power making the entire investigation possible is precisely what you’re seeking.

It’s like studying the chemical composition of ink on a love letter while ignoring the love letter.

Very accurate chemistry.

Very unfortunate relationship.

The philosophical point: For Kṣemarāja, the inner secret of mantra is Śakti as self-aware, universal consciousness, not the mere external sequence of phonemes. The phonemes remain significant, but their highest efficacy depends upon recognition of that conscious source.

And now, having explained the extraordinary secret of mantra, Vasugupta offers a warning.

Because apparently you can get all the way into advanced tantric practice, encounter unusual experiences, and still completely miss the point.

Which is deeply embarrassing.

2.4 — You Got One Spiritual Experience and Now You Think You’re Gandalf
गर्भे चित्तविकासोऽविशिष्टविद्यास्वप्नः ॥४॥

Garbhe cittavikāso ’viśiṣṭavidyāsvapnaḥ.

Interpretive gloss: The mind’s expansion within the womb of Māyā, even through extraordinary powers, is merely a dream of inferior knowledge.

Oh dear.

We’ve arrived at the warning.

And Vasugupta is about to deliver an absolutely devastating assessment of the spiritually ambitious.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Garbhe — in the womb, in the matrix.

Citta — mind.

Vikāsaḥ — expansion, blossoming, development.

Aviśiṣṭa — ordinary, undistinguished, lacking the special excellence of supreme knowledge.

Vidyā — knowledge.

Svapnaḥ — dream.

Now, on a superficial reading, this sounds almost positive.

The mind expands!

Knowledge develops!

There are extraordinary experiences!

Wonderful!

Perhaps you’re meditating one afternoon and begin seeing lights.

Beautiful colours.

A luminous sphere.

Mysterious sounds.

Perhaps an extraordinary sense of energy moving through the body.

You leap to the obvious conclusion.

I HAVE BECOME A SUPERNATURAL BEING.

Naturally, you immediately consider starting a YouTube channel.

Perhaps a retreat centre.

A very tasteful website.

And a line of branded meditation cushions.

But Vasugupta has some disappointing news.

You’re still dreaming.

Kṣemarāja interprets garbha here as the domain of Māyā, associated with primordial ignorance of one’s own true nature.

And the cittavikāsa, the expansion of mind within that domain, can include fascination with limited supernatural attainments.

He specifically mentions experiences associated with bindu and nāda — luminous points and subtle sounds — when they occur as secondary yogic phenomena.

These must not be confused with the supreme metaphysical principles denoted by the same terms in other contexts.

Here’s the extraordinary thing.

Kṣemarāja explicitly draws upon the Spandakārikā and Patañjali’s Yoga Sūtras to warn that extraordinary experiences can become obstacles to samādhi. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And that’s a rather important correction to the assumption that Tantra simply encourages collecting increasingly spectacular experiences.

Because from the nondual standpoint, extraordinary experiences aren’t necessarily extraordinary recognition.

You might experience a thousand luminous visions.

But if you’re still fundamentally identifying as a limited subject acquiring impressive objects of experience, the basic structure of bondage remains.

“I have seen a divine light.”

“I have heard an extraordinary sound.”

“I have acquired a supernatural power.”

Notice the grammar.

I have acquired something.

The subject is still opposed to the object.

And unless the true nature of that subject is recognised, the fundamental metaphysical misunderstanding persists.

You could theoretically become capable of astonishing feats and remain thoroughly bound.

A very powerful paśu.

An extremely talented prisoner.

A man with the ability to perceive distant objects who still cannot recognise the conscious reality in which his perceptions arise.

Wonderful.

You’ve developed cosmic binoculars.

And forgotten your own eyes.

The sūtra’s deeper warning isn’t that visionary experiences are necessarily false or meaningless.

It’s that none of them, by themselves, establishes the recognition of Śiva.

Kṣemarāja calls absorption in their limited enjoyments dreamlike.

Because the yogi has mistaken a phenomenon appearing within consciousness for the recognition of consciousness itself.

And Vasugupta now tells us what happens when this fascination is transcended.

2.5 — No, Khecarī Doesn’t Mean Shoving Your Tongue Up Your Nose
विद्यासमुत्थाने स्वाभाविके खेचरी शिवावस्था ॥५॥

Vidyāsamutthāne svābhāvike khecarī śivāvasthā.

Interpretive gloss: When supreme knowledge arises spontaneously, Khecarī manifests as the state of Śiva.

Right.

We’ve reached Khecarī.

And unfortunately, we’ve got to clear something up immediately.

Because if you search for Khecarī Mudrā, you’re likely to encounter extremely flexible yogis doing extraordinary things with their tongues.

In certain haṭhayoga traditions, khecarīmudrā refers to a technique involving the tongue and the upper palate.

But that is not what Kṣemarāja means here.

And he explicitly distinguishes this sūtra’s Khecarī from physical or specialised yogic procedures promising extraordinary powers, including flight.

Quite.

It’s always a relief when a medieval tantric philosopher tells you that you don’t have to rearrange your mouth to attain supreme consciousness.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Vidyā — knowledge, here supreme knowledge.

Samutthāna — arising, emergence.

Svābhāvika — natural, spontaneous, belonging to one’s own nature.

Khecarī — she who moves through kha, space or the sky.

Śivāvasthā — the state of Śiva.

Now the word kha is doing something extremely important.

In ordinary Sanskrit, it can mean space or sky.

But in this tantric context, Kṣemarāja interprets it as the boundless space of consciousness.

So Khecarī is the power moving freely within that space.

And when supreme knowledge arises naturally, the yogi enters the state of Śiva.

Not the state of an individual who has acquired another special experience.

The state of recognising the nature of consciousness itself.

Imagine the mind as a bird.

Ordinarily, it flies towards objects.

A sound.

A memory.

A desire.

A fear.

It moves between limited things.

And its sense of freedom is conditioned by those things.

But now the bird discovers something astonishing.

Its real freedom does not consist merely in choosing which object to fly towards.

The openness through which every movement is possible was never absent.

This is the metaphorical significance of Khecarī.

Not a bird imprisoned in a box getting a slightly larger box.

Not even a bird acquiring a particularly impressive pair of wings.

But the recognition of the boundless conscious space in which its movements arise.

And Kṣemarāja links this with the spontaneous emergence of bliss.

Ānanda.

Not simply ordinary pleasure.

Not an exciting sensation that eventually disappears and leaves you wanting another one.

But the delight associated with consciousness recognising its own freedom.

And notice what happened between 2.4 and 2.5.

At 2.4, the practitioner became fascinated by exceptional phenomena appearing within Māyā.

At 2.5, those limited accomplishments are subordinated to the spontaneous arising of supreme knowledge.

It’s a complete reorientation.

Instead of asking:

“What amazing thing can I experience next?”

The yogi discovers the ground of experience itself.

And that, within Kṣemarāja’s interpretation, is the state of Śiva.

Not supernatural tourism.

Not a spiritual upgrade package.

Recognition of the freedom that makes every experience possible.

Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

2.6 — Unfortunately, You’re Going to Need a Guru
गुरुरुपायः ॥६॥

Gurur upāyaḥ.

Literal: The guru is the means.

Oh, for God’s sake.

We’ve been doing so well.

We discovered that consciousness is the Self.

That Śakti is our own fundamental power.

That mantra is rooted in awareness.

That supreme knowledge arises naturally.

And now Vasugupta has decided we need another person involved.

Gurur upāyaḥ.

The guru is the means.

Well, who’s the guru?

And why do we need one if we’re already Śiva?

Kṣemarāja gives a beautifully nuanced answer.

The guru is not merely somebody who has memorised a large quantity of Sanskrit.

Nor simply someone who knows how to perform a complicated ceremony.

The genuine guru communicates the meaning of reality and reveals the potency of mantra.

The guru makes recognition possible through instruction and, in the tantric understanding, the operation of divine grace.

And here’s the key.

Kṣemarāja gives two related interpretations.

First, the guru is the teacher who reveals the true meaning of the principles and practices.

Second, the guru can be understood as the grace-bestowing power of the Supreme Lord.

This is crucial.

Because it means the ultimate efficacy of the guru isn’t reducible to their ordinary personality.

The guru’s liberating function is rooted in Śakti.

Kṣemarāja invokes the Mālinīvijayatantra and other tantric authorities in explaining the relation between the guru, divine grace, and mantra’s efficacy. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

So imagine the situation.

You’ve spent several years reading philosophy.

You understand prakāśa.

You understand vimarśa.

You’ve learned the thirty-six tattvas.

You know the difference between āṇavamala and māyīyamala.

You can explain the five kañcukas without accidentally confusing them with the five kośas.

Wonderful.

You’re now an exceptionally knowledgeable individual.

But perhaps you’ve become exceptionally knowledgeable about your own fundamental freedom without actually recognising it.

You’re studying the map.

Beautiful map.

Gorgeous contour lines.

Very impressive bibliography.

But you haven’t arrived.

Then a genuine teacher helps you understand what the teachings are pointing towards.

Not merely by adding another concept.

But by directing your awareness towards the very reality in which all concepts appear.

And suddenly the knowledge changes character.

It’s no longer merely information about consciousness.

It’s recognition.

That is the transformative possibility of upadeśa — instruction.

And in the tantric framework, śaktipāta, the descent of grace, is deeply relevant.

But we should be careful.

This doesn’t mean that every person calling themselves a guru automatically possesses divine authority.

It certainly doesn’t mean suspending ethical judgement or surrendering autonomy to a charismatic personality.

The classical ideal is the guru who genuinely discloses reality, not somebody demanding obedience because they’ve acquired a sufficiently elaborate beard.

The deepest point is that Śiva’s own grace can operate through instruction.

Consciousness, apparently, has developed a teaching department.

And sometimes that department takes human form.

2.7 — The Entire Sanskrit Alphabet Is a Secret Diagram of the Universe
मातृकाचक्रसम्बोधः ॥७॥

Mātṛkācakrasambodhaḥ.

Literal: Complete awakening to the circle of Mātṛkā.

Right.

Now we’re getting into the properly beautiful stuff.

This is one of the most philosophically rich sūtras of the entire second section.

And Kṣemarāja’s commentary goes absolutely nuclear.

We had three words.

Mātṛkā.

Cakra.

Sambodha.

And he responds with an extraordinary exposition of the entire Sanskrit phonemic system as a manifestation of divine consciousness.

Apparently the alphabet is not just the alphabet.

It’s a metaphysical procession of Śakti.

Let’s unpack this.

Mātṛkā — the little mother; here, the divine matrix of phonemic powers.

Cakra — wheel, circle, collective.

Sambodha — complete awakening, full understanding or recognition.

In the preceding sūtra, the guru was introduced as the means.

Now we learn what genuine instruction can disclose.

The practitioner awakens to the true nature of Mātṛkā.

And this is where the entire discussion comes full circle.

Remember 1.4?

Jñānādhiṣṭhānaṃ mātṛkā.

Mātṛkā is the foundation of differentiated knowledge.

Earlier, we encountered her as a power associated with bondage.

Now we’re encountering the exact same power as a vehicle of enlightenment.

Why?

Because the letters are no longer apprehended merely as differentiated expressions concealing their source.

They are recognised as expressions of supreme consciousness.

And Kṣemarāja unpacks this through a detailed tantric phonology.

Let’s begin.

अ — A

The first vowel.

Kṣemarāja associates a with Anuttara.

The unsurpassable.

That beyond which nothing is higher.

The supreme reality.

Not simply a thing at the beginning of a sequence, but the fundamental source through which the entire sequence can be understood.

The alphabet begins with the unsurpassable.

Very ambitious for a vowel.

आ — Ā

The extended vowel.

Associated with Ānanda.

Bliss.

The supreme reality understood through its own fullness and delight.

So the movement from a to ā is not simply interpreted as a longer sound.

It is given a symbolic meaning within the unfolding of consciousness.

The unsurpassable and its bliss.

इ — I, and ई — Ī

Now we encounter icchā, will, and īśana, sovereignty or lordly power.

The divine doesn’t merely exist as an inert absolute.

It possesses the freedom to manifest.

This is absolutely fundamental to Trika.

If ultimate consciousness were entirely passive, we would have difficulty explaining manifestation as an expression of its own nature.

But Śiva is inseparable from Śakti.

And divine will belongs to that freedom.

उ — U, and ऊ — Ū

Now Kṣemarāja connects the phonemic unfolding with unmeṣa, opening or emergence, and ūnatā, a further differentiation associated with the developing appearance of objectivity.

The movement of manifestation becomes increasingly articulated.

This is not ordinary physical chronology.

We’re not claiming somebody pronounced a, then ā, and three seconds later the first galaxy appeared.

It is a symbolic, metaphysical account of the articulation of consciousness.

The vowels represent powers through which manifestation is understood.

And the sequence continues.

ऋ, ॠ, ऌ, ॡ — ṛ, ṝ, ḷ, ḹ.

These are connected with further differentiations involving the powers and their relations to elemental principles.

ए — E is associated with a particular union involving the powers symbolised by a and i.

ओ — O is associated with the corresponding union involving a and u, connected with the emergence of the power of action.

ऐ — Ai and औ — Au carry further symbolic meanings through combinations of those powers, including the integration of will, knowledge and action.

Then come the particularly important signs:

अं — Aṃ.

Bindu.

The point of concentrated unity.

And:

अः — Aḥ.

Visarga.

The power of emission.

Now things become extraordinarily interesting.

Because the alphabet is associated with the movement between undivided consciousness and the manifestation of differentiated reality.

The consonants then receive their own metaphysical interpretations.

The five consonantal classes, from ka through ma, are correlated with principles of manifestation.

The semivowels:

य र ल व — Ya, ra, la, va

are associated with further intermediate levels, including the limiting powers.

The sibilants and ha are related to the higher movement between difference and non-difference.

This is a structured tantric symbolism, not the ordinary linguistic explanation of how the Sanskrit alphabet historically developed.

And then Kṣemarāja introduces an absolutely magnificent idea.

The Mahāmantra.

The great mantra of I-consciousness.

अहम् — Aham.

“I.”

Now think about this.

The beginning of the alphabet.

A.

The unfolding powers of Śakti.

The sequence extending to ha and the concluding nasal element.

The tradition reads this totality symbolically in the great expression:

Aham.

I.

But not merely the ordinary personal pronoun.

Not the limited ego saying:

“I am hungry.”

“I am tired.”

“I am better at metaphysics than my neighbour.”

This is pūrṇāhantā.

Full I-consciousness.

The universal Self-awareness of Śiva.

The indivisible consciousness in which the entire manifest universe is included.

The great mantra is not merely a word used by an individual to refer to themselves.

It is an expression of the fundamental self-awareness of reality.

And now we can see the extraordinary elegance of the entire second section.

At 2.1:

The mind is mantra.

At 2.3:

The secret of mantra is its living divine consciousness.

At 2.7:

The entire matrix of phonemic powers is recognised as a manifestation of that consciousness.

And the culmination is Aham.

Not the small, contracted “I”.

But the universal I-consciousness in which every sound, every object, every cognition, and every form of manifestation is included.

Kṣemarāja’s extensive discussion here draws on the phonemic cosmology of the Parātriṃśikā tradition and the teachings developed by Abhinavagupta. It’s far more elaborate than the three-word sūtra alone, but entirely relevant to the reading we’re following. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism +1

And now, having realised that the alphabet is a manifestation of the Goddess, we’re ready for the next instruction.

Which is, apparently:

Throw your body into a fire.

Excellent.

Another perfectly normal day in Kashmir.

2.8 — Congratulations, You’re the Sacrifice
शरीरं हविः ॥८॥

Śarīraṃ haviḥ.

Literal: The body is the sacrificial offering.

RIGHT.

Before anybody gets any ideas, let’s establish something important.

Kṣemarāja is not telling practitioners to physically burn themselves.

The fire in question is the supreme fire of consciousness.

And the body is offered through the dissolution of identification with it as the ultimate Self.

Now let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Śarīra — body.

Havis — sacrificial oblation or offering; haviḥ is its nominative form.

And this refers us to the ancient Indian sacrificial vocabulary of yajña.

The fire.

The offering.

The act of offering.

The officiant.

The divine recipient.

Ordinarily, these are differentiated elements of a ritual.

Somebody performs the sacrifice.

Something is offered.

The offering goes into the fire.

The ritual is directed towards a divine reality.

But Trika makes an extraordinary internal transformation of this structure.

The body becomes the offering.

Consciousness becomes the fire.

And the false identification of the limited body as the ultimate perceiver is consumed.

Now, notice the specificity.

Kṣemarāja isn’t merely saying:

“Stop being attached to your body.”

He’s talking about the pramātṛ, the knowing subject.

Ordinarily, you identify the knower with your embodied individuality.

“I am this body.”

“My eyes are seeing.”

“My brain is thinking.”

“My personal history defines what I am.”

But remember the very first sūtra.

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

From the standpoint of Kṣemarāja’s nondual metaphysics, the body itself belongs to the field of manifestation.

It is not the ultimate foundation of the conscious subject.

So what does it mean to offer the body?

It means that the body, together with its subtle forms and the structures of limited identification, is recognised as an expression within supreme consciousness.

The body is not destroyed.

Its erroneous status as an independently existing ultimate Self is relinquished.

Imagine standing before a fire.

You take the entire notion:

“I am nothing more than this limited organism.”

And you offer it into the recognition:

“My deepest nature is the consciousness in which this organism, its experiences, and the universe itself are manifest.”

The body hasn’t vanished.

You’ve not stopped eating.

You’ve not magically become impervious to a falling coconut.

But its ontological status within your understanding has changed.

And the beauty is that Kṣemarāja draws on the Vijñānabhairava to explain this inner sacrifice.

He describes the elements, senses, their objects, and mind as offerings into the fire of consciousness.

And the power that performs the offering is itself cetanā, conscious Śakti.

So who’s the priest?

Śakti.

What’s the offering?

The limited structures of embodiment.

What’s the fire?

Consciousness.

And who’s the divine recipient?

Śiva.

So the entire sacrificial process is ultimately an expression of the same reality.

The priest is Śiva’s power.

The fire is Śiva.

The offering belongs to Śiva.

And the sacrifice discloses the nondual nature of the whole performance.

It’s rather like turning up to a wedding and discovering you’re the bride, groom, priest, venue, and the entire congregation.

Except this is an internal metaphysical ritual rather than an unusually complicated marital arrangement.

And this is especially beautiful when compared with the Bhagavadgītā’s vision of sacrificial action.

Different traditions develop the idea in different ways, but the structural resemblance is unmistakable.

The apparent separation between the act, its instruments, its object, and the divine reality is overcome through knowledge.

Kṣemarāja explicitly cites both the Vijñānabhairava and Bhagavadgītā when explaining the sūtra. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The offering is not a rejection of embodiment.

It is the end of mistaking embodiment for your ultimate identity.

And now we arrive at one of my favourite statements in the entire text.

Because having turned the body into a sacrificial offering, Vasugupta announces that knowledge is dinner.

2.9 — Śiva Eats Your Thoughts for Breakfast
ज्ञानमन्नम् ॥९॥

Jñānam annam.

Literal: Knowledge is food.

Now this is absolutely glorious.

Because Vasugupta has been playing a very sophisticated game with the word jñāna.

Remember 1.2?

ज्ञानं बन्धः।

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Knowledge is bondage.

And now?

ज्ञानमन्नम्।

Jñānam annam.

Knowledge is food.

So we’ve gone from knowledge being the prison to knowledge being lunch.

Quite an upgrade.

Let’s unpack it.

Jñāna — knowledge.

Anna — food, that which is eaten.

And Kṣemarāja offers two interconnected interpretations.

The first is especially powerful.

The limited knowledge previously described as bondage is now food for the realised yogi.

The yogi devours it.

Not by forgetting everything.

Not by becoming incapable of ordinary thought.

And certainly not by physically eating books.

Although that might explain some academic departments.

The devouring is a metaphor for the absorption of limited knowledge into nondual recognition.

Imagine a thought arises.

“There is something separate from me.”

Under ordinary conditions, this differentiated cognition reinforces the structure of bondage.

The object appears independent.

The subject appears limited.

The relationship between them is experienced through separation.

But now suppose the fundamental reality of consciousness has been recognised.

The same differentiated appearance occurs.

A sound.

A colour.

A sensation.

A thought.

But its ultimate nature is no longer misunderstood.

The difference appears within the undivided field of consciousness.

And therefore the difference no longer necessarily obscures its conscious source.

This is what Kṣemarāja means by the devouring of limited knowledge.

The distinction is not necessarily annihilated at the level of conventional functioning.

Instead, its apparent independence is overcome.

A wave appears.

The ocean does not cease to be the ocean.

And the wave, recognised in its true nature, is never something fundamentally foreign to the water.

Now imagine the yogi encountering the entire universe in this manner.

Each differentiated cognition becomes an occasion for recognising its source.

The world is no longer a collection of alien objects confronting a finite subject.

It is the manifestation of consciousness, continually recognised.

And there’s something extraordinarily affirmative about this.

Because the yogi doesn’t have to become incapable of perceiving differences.

They don’t have to destroy the mental faculty that makes ordinary life possible.

They don’t have to wander around insisting that chairs and elephants are literally identical at every empirical level.

That would be a terrible way to organise a zoo.

Instead, differences are apprehended without mistaking them for ultimate separations in consciousness.

The second interpretation Kṣemarāja offers is equally beautiful.

Knowledge of the Self becomes food through the satisfaction it produces.

The yogi is nourished by recognition.

And the term tṛpti — satisfaction or fulfilment — becomes relevant.

Not a temporary pleasure requiring another object.

But the fullness associated with resting in one’s own conscious nature.

This recalls the Trika understanding of ānanda as intrinsic to the supreme reality.

You’re not acquiring completeness from an external object.

You’re recognising the completeness fundamental to consciousness.

Kṣemarāja draws on the Vijñānabhairava and Spandakārikā to make these points. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And now notice the beautiful progression of the preceding two sūtras.

At 2.8, the body is offered into consciousness.

At 2.9, limited knowledge is consumed by consciousness.

First, the embodied identity is relinquished as the ultimate Self.

Then differentiated cognition is absorbed into recognition.

The fire consumes the body.

Consciousness consumes knowledge.

And throughout this sacrificial imagery, there is no second reality outside Śiva.

The offerer, the offering, and the act of consumption are manifestations of his own power.

This is what makes Trika so magnificent.

The same consciousness that manifested the universe now recognises it through the very powers by which it was manifested.

The world hasn’t become spiritually irrelevant.

It has become the meal.

And apparently Śiva has quite the appetite.

2.10 — You Realise You’re Śiva, Then Immediately Forget Again Like an Absolute Idiot
विद्यासंहारे तदुत्थस्वप्नदर्शनम् ॥१०॥

Vidyāsaṃhāre tadutthasvapnadarśanam.

Interpretive gloss: When pure knowledge subsides, the dreamlike appearances arising from its fading become visible again.

Right.

This final sūtra is absolutely devastating.

And honestly, perhaps the most relatable statement in the entire second book.

Because after all this extraordinary metaphysics, after the divine mantra, after the awakening of Mātṛkā, after the sacrifice of the body, after the devouring of differentiated knowledge—

Vasugupta says:

You might forget.

That’s it.

You’ve recognised the truth.

You’ve understood the fundamental nature of consciousness.

You’ve experienced the dissolution of limited identification.

You’ve seen the world as the manifestation of Śakti.

And then…

Someone sends you an annoying message.

Suddenly you’re back.

“I cannot BELIEVE this person.”

“How dare they.”

“Do they not appreciate who they’re dealing with?”

And just like that, the individual identity has returned to centre stage.

The cosmic drama has resumed.

Śiva has once again become a bloke arguing with his phone.

Now, obviously, this is a modern psychological illustration.

Kṣemarāja’s actual discussion is more technical.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Vidyā — knowledge, here pure liberating knowledge.

Saṃhāra — withdrawal, subsidence, disappearance.

Taduttha — arising from that, or resulting from it.

Svapna — dream.

Darśana — seeing, appearance.

Kṣemarāja describes what happens when the practitioner’s attention to pure knowledge weakens.

The awareness of supreme reality is no longer maintained.

The impressions of that awareness gradually fade.

And the differentiated mental world reasserts itself.

The svapna here isn’t necessarily a literal dream during sleep.

It is the dreamlike manifestation of limited, differentiated cognition.

The world once again appears in terms of subject and object, possession and loss, attraction and aversion.

And the yogi can become caught up in those distinctions.

Now here’s something very important.

Kṣemarāja is not saying Śiva has literally stopped existing.

Or that universal consciousness has somehow disappeared.

That would contradict the basic doctrine.

Rather, the recognition of pure consciousness has subsided for the practitioner.

The underlying reality has not changed.

The mode of awareness has changed.

This is the heart of the issue.

You see clearly.

Then you become distracted.

Then you remember.

Then you become absorbed in something else.

Then you recognise again.

A continual alternation of recognition and contraction.

And Kṣemarāja is concerned with what happens when awareness lacks the steadiness necessary for sustained recognition.

He invokes the Mālinīvijayatantra and Spandakārikā to explain that even a genuine opening can be followed by renewed involvement in differentiated experience.

The latent impressions of recognition may remain, but attention is no longer established in their source. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And this is why the sūtra is a warning.

Not because the world is inherently evil.

Not because ordinary experience is something to hate.

But because the yogi may become identified once more with the limitations appearing within experience.

Consider what happens when someone understands a profound philosophical truth.

For a moment, everything is clear.

You see how all the elements fit together.

You understand the problem.

You feel an extraordinary freedom from the confusion that previously occupied you.

And then, a few hours later, you find yourself behaving as though none of that understanding had ever happened.

The knowledge was real at the level at which it occurred.

But it had not become stable.

This is a useful analogy for what Kṣemarāja is discussing, though the actual sūtra concerns a much more profound yogic recognition than an intellectual insight.

The important point is stabilisation.

Not merely having recognised.

But remaining established in recognition.

And here Kṣemarāja provides the transition to the next section.

If the practitioner cannot maintain the attentive recognition characteristic of Śāktopāya, another form of practice becomes appropriate.

One employing more concrete supports.

More articulated procedures.

More deliberate engagement with the faculties of the limited individual.

And thus, the third book emerges.

Āṇavopāya.

The way of the aṇu.

The limited individual.

Where the teaching begins working with the very structures through which consciousness experiences itself as finite.

Which is a rather beautiful conclusion.

Because even when the practitioner becomes distracted from the highest recognition, the tradition doesn’t simply abandon them.

There remains another way.

The capacity for practice belongs to the same Śakti.

The limited individual has not fallen outside consciousness.

They have merely returned to a contracted mode of awareness.

And that contracted mode can itself become the starting point for liberation.

The same Śiva.

The same Śakti.

Another method.

Another opportunity.

Another chance for consciousness to recognise what it never ceased to be.

The Great Reveal — What Was Śāktopāya Actually Teaching?
Let’s revisit the extraordinary progression of these ten statements.

In the first book, Vasugupta established the supreme nature of consciousness and described the immediate recognition characteristic of Śāmbhavopāya.

But Śāktopāya provides a more articulated account of how mental activity itself can participate in liberation.

The teaching is not:

“Stop thinking and become Śiva.”

Nor is it:

“Think hard enough and manufacture Śiva.”

It’s something rather subtler.

Recognise the conscious power from which thought arises, and through which its differentiation becomes manifest.

Consider the structure.

2.1 — Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind becomes mantra through contemplation of its supreme conscious source.

2.2 — Prayatnaḥ sādhakaḥ.

Spontaneous, directed effort makes this contemplation effective.

2.3 — Vidyāśarīrasattā mantrarahasyam.

The hidden reality of mantra is the living power of nondual consciousness.

2.4 — Garbhe cittavikāso ’viśiṣṭavidyāsvapnaḥ.

The practitioner must not confuse limited accomplishments and visionary phenomena with supreme knowledge.

2.5 — Vidyāsamutthāne svābhāvike khecarī śivāvasthā.

When supreme knowledge arises spontaneously, Khecarī, the state of Śiva, is revealed.

2.6 — Gurur upāyaḥ.

The guru, as genuine instruction and the vehicle of divine grace, is a means of recognition.

2.7 — Mātṛkācakrasambodhaḥ.

The practitioner awakens to the divine nature of the entire wheel of phonemic powers.

2.8 — Śarīraṃ haviḥ.

Limited bodily identification becomes an offering into the fire of consciousness.

2.9 — Jñānam annam.

Differentiated knowledge is consumed within nondual recognition.

2.10 — Vidyāsaṃhāre tadutthasvapnadarśanam.

When that recognition subsides, dreamlike differentiation returns, providing the reason for further practice.

And there’s an absolutely beautiful symmetry here.

The first section began:

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

The second section began:

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

And the third section will begin:

आत्मा चित्तम्।

Ātmā cittam.

The individual self is mind.

Three different points of departure.

Three distinct approaches to the problem of recognition.

The supreme standpoint of Śiva.

The transformative power of Śakti.

And the limited standpoint of the individual.

All directed towards the recognition of the one conscious reality.

The Final Teaching — You Cannot Escape Śakti
Here’s the extraordinary thing about Śāktopāya.

Everything that appears to separate you from your true nature is itself an expression of the power of that nature.

Thought.

Language.

Desire.

Mental activity.

Differentiation.

The sense of being an individual.

All belong, in the final analysis, to the manifestation of Śakti.

And therefore, the path to recognition doesn’t always require rejecting them.

It can involve understanding them so completely that their conscious source becomes apparent.

The same language that articulates limitation can become mantra.

The same mental activity that constructs difference can become contemplative recognition.

The same Mātṛkā that structures differentiated knowledge can disclose the universal I-consciousness.

The same body that appears to define your individuality can become an offering into its conscious source.

The same knowledge that constitutes bondage can become food for liberation.

And when recognition disappears?

Even that forgetting occurs within the power of consciousness.

It has not created a second reality independent of Śiva.

The extraordinary conclusion is that the path and the apparent obstacle are not made from two fundamentally different substances.

They are different modes of the same divine manifestation.

Śakti is not a ladder somebody built so you could climb out of the universe.

She’s the power through which the universe appears.

The power through which you experience yourself as limited.

And the power through which that limitation can be recognised as a contraction of the infinite.

The Goddess isn’t waiting at the end of the path.

She’s been making the path, walking the path, and appearing as the confused individual wondering where the path leads.

An astonishing amount of work for one woman.

But apparently infinite power does have its advantages.

End of Book II — Śāktopāya.

Textual note: The ten Sanskrit sūtras are attested in the standard 77-sūtra recension. The explanatory reading follows Kṣemarāja’s Śivasūtravimarśinī, especially his distinctions concerning mantra, limited siddhis, Khecarī, Mātṛkā and the subsidence of pure knowledge. Traditional supernatural and cosmological claims have been presented as the tradition’s claims, not verified physical phenomena. The humorous narration is original rather than a quotation of Kṣemarāja.

Next comes the longest and most extraordinary part of the text.

Book III — Āṇavopāya
Forty-five sūtras.

The limited individual, the return of bondage, the construction of experience, the role of the senses, the breath, embodiment, liberation in daily life, and one of the most beautiful sequences in all Kashmir Śaivism:

नर्तक आत्मा।

Nartaka ātmā.

The Self is the dancer.

रङ्गोऽन्तरात्मा।

Raṅgo ’ntarātmā.

The inner Self is the stage.

प्रेक्षकाणीन्द्रियाणि।

Prekṣakāṇīndriyāṇi.

The senses are the spectators.

Which means that after two books of studying the metaphysical nature of consciousness, Vasugupta is about to explain that the entire business of being alive is essentially Śiva putting on a theatrical production for himself.

And you’ve been taking your role rather seriously.

Continue Book III — Āṇavopāya


शिवसूत्राणि — THE ŚIVA SŪTRAS
BOOK III — ĀṆAVOPĀYA

The Way of the Limited Individual: Or, How Śiva Somehow Managed to Become a Confused Little Human, Forget He’s God, and Then Spend Several Lifetimes Trying to Work Out What the Bloody Hell Happened.

Part I — Sūtras 3.1–3.15

Previously, on the Śiva Sūtras…
Right.

Welcome back to Kashmir.

We’ve survived two books.

In Book I, Vasugupta told us that consciousness is the Self, the universe is the body of Śiva, and reality is an extraordinary expression of divine freedom.

Beautiful.

In Book II, we discovered that the mind can become mantra, the Sanskrit alphabet is a manifestation of Śakti, knowledge is food, and apparently the entire human body is a sacrificial offering.

Also beautiful.

Slightly concerning, but beautiful.

And now we’ve arrived at Book III.

Āṇavopāya.

The means belonging to the aṇu.

The limited individual.

And this is where things get really interesting.

Because thus far, we’ve been considering recognition from relatively exalted standpoints.

Śāmbhavopāya — the way associated with Śiva’s immediate power of will.

Śāktopāya — the way associated with the transformative power of knowledge.

But now Vasugupta has decided to address a rather awkward situation.

What if you’re not a supreme yogi?

What if the spontaneous recognition of Bhairava doesn’t arise?

What if you’ve read an entire book about the divine potency of mantra, but the moment somebody speaks rudely to you, you become a furious little monkey again?

What if you understand svātantrya intellectually, but your actual daily experience consists mainly of confusion, desire, frustration, and checking whether somebody has replied to your messages?

Well.

Congratulations.

You are the target audience.

Āṇavopāya begins with the limited perspective and employs more definite supports: contemplation, breath, concentration, bodily practices, the senses, and the transformation of ordinary experience.

But don’t mistake this for a completely different philosophy.

We’re not abandoning nonduality.

We’re asking how the individual who experiences limitation can begin to recognise the freedom that was always their deepest nature.

And Kṣemarāja is going to give us an extraordinary explanation.

The individual mind.

The thirty-six tattvas.

The five limiting powers.

The dissolution of the cosmos within the body.

Subtle channels and extraordinary yogic claims.

The world as the radiance of consciousness.

And then, perhaps most beautifully of all, the entire universe as a theatrical performance.

Śiva the actor.

The subtle body the stage.

The senses the audience.

And a cosmic drama in which the actor has become so absorbed in the role that he’s forgotten he’s the one performing it.

Absolutely magnificent.

One textual point before the curtain rises: we’re following Kṣemarāja’s 45-sūtra third section, making 77 sūtras overall. Another recension inserts Visargasvābhāvyād abahiḥsthites tatsthitiḥ before Bījāvadhānam, resulting in 46 sūtras in this section. We’ll stay with the 45-sūtra sequence so the numbering remains consistent. Sanskrit Documents +1

Now then.

Lights down.

Curtain up.

The Lord of the Universe is about to explain why you think you’re a person.

3.1 — Congratulations, You’re a Mind Now. Quite the Demotion.
आत्मा चित्तम् ॥१॥

Ātmā cittam.

Literal: The Self is mind.

EXCUSE ME?

Vasugupta.

My friend.

My brother in Bhairava.

WHAT?

The first sūtra of the entire text was:

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

We’ve spent thirty-two sūtras establishing the supreme identity of consciousness.

And now you’ve changed your bloody mind?

Ātmā cittam.

The Self is mind.

Well, which is it?

Are we infinite consciousness, or are we a bundle of thoughts wandering around worrying about the price of vegetables?

Fortunately, Kṣemarāja anticipated this objection.

And his explanation is absolutely crucial.

He’s not contradicting the first book.

He’s changing the standpoint.

In the first book, we considered the true nature of the Self.

In this book, we consider the Self as it appears in the contracted condition of individuality.

That’s the distinction.

Let’s break down the Sanskrit.

Ātman — Self.

Citta — mind, mental consciousness.

But Kṣemarāja is very specific about citta.

Here it encompasses the antaḥkaraṇa, the internal instrument of cognition.

And that instrument consists of three major functions.

Buddhi — determining intelligence.

The faculty that makes judgements.

“This is a tree.”

“That is a dog.”

“That person is being unreasonable.”

Ahaṃkāra — the ego-making or I-making principle.

The faculty of appropriation.

“This is MY tree.”

“That is MY dog.”

“That person has insulted ME.”

Manas — the ordinary mind.

The faculty of mental construction, fluctuation, and engagement with sensory objects.

“What shall I do about this?”

“What if the tree falls?”

“What if the dog doesn’t like me?”

“What if everybody secretly hates me?”

And suddenly we’ve got the entire human predicament.

Buddhi identifies the object.

Ahaṃkāra appropriates the experience.

Manas starts producing a twelve-part Netflix documentary about it.

And the whole thing takes place within consciousness.

But because consciousness has contracted into identification with this mental apparatus, the limited individual experiences:

I am this mind.

Not:

“I am the universal consciousness in which these mental activities manifest.”

Just:

“I am this collection of thoughts, desires, judgements and personal history.”

And that’s the condition of the aṇu.

The little one.

The apparently finite individual.

But here’s the extraordinarily important move.

Kṣemarāja says the Self doesn’t genuinely become a different ultimate substance.

In its true nature, it remains consciousness.

Its identification with citta belongs to its āṇavadaśā — the condition of limitation.

So there’s no actual contradiction between:

Caitanyam ātmā.

And:

Ātmā cittam.

The first describes the Self in its fundamental nature.

The second describes the Self under the condition of contraction.

Now think about what that means.

The individual isn’t some metaphysical mistake that exists outside Śiva.

Individuality is itself a manifestation within Śiva’s consciousness.

The limitation is real as an experienced condition, but it does not establish an ultimately independent second reality.

The infinite has become involved in its own finite appearance.

And this is why saṃkoca, contraction, is so important in Trika.

Consciousness hasn’t been physically compressed into a tiny container.

It’s not as though somebody took an infinitely large Śiva, shoved him into a human skull, and slammed the lid.

Rather, the conscious subject appears under the conditions of limited knowing and agency.

Like an actor becoming completely absorbed in a character.

The actor doesn’t cease to be the actor.

But from within the performance, the character’s world becomes the apparent horizon of experience.

And the tragedy begins when the actor forgets the distinction.

Kṣemarāja explicitly states that there is no ultimate wandering of the Self, whose nature remains consciousness. The appearance of transmigration belongs to its contracted identification with the mental apparatus. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The philosophical revelation: Your ordinary selfhood is not the complete truth of what you are. But neither is it something standing outside consciousness. It is consciousness appearing under conditions of limitation.

And now we know who our protagonist is.

Śiva.

Playing a person.

Who thinks he’s a mind.

And is about to become terribly confused.

3.2 — Knowledge Is Bondage. Yes, Again. Apparently You Didn’t Get It the First Time.
ज्ञानं बन्धः ॥२॥

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Literal: Knowledge is bondage.

RIGHT.

WE’VE SEEN THIS ONE.

It’s literally the second sūtra of the first book.

Vasugupta has copied and pasted himself.

The man’s producing sacred scripture and apparently can’t be bothered writing a new sentence.

But the repetition is entirely deliberate.

Because the same statement is now being explained from the standpoint of the limited individual.

In Book I, knowledge as bondage was connected with the fundamental contraction of universal consciousness.

Here, Kṣemarāja gets much more specific.

He’s talking about the ordinary mental cognitions associated with:

Pleasure.

Pain.

Delusion.

Judgement.

Appropriation.

The perception of difference.

And the experience of being an individual subject moving through a world of external objects.

Now imagine you’re having a perfectly pleasant afternoon.

You see something beautiful.

The mind responds:

“I like this.”

Then:

“I want this.”

Then:

“I need to keep this.”

Then:

“What happens if I lose this?”

Then:

“Why does that bastard have a better one?”

And within approximately seventeen seconds, an innocent pleasant perception has developed into a complete metaphysical hostage situation.

Now, Kṣemarāja isn’t suggesting that pleasure is intrinsically sinful.

Nor that distinguishing objects is itself an ethical failure.

The issue is that differentiated knowledge becomes entangled with limited identity.

The individual knows through an apparatus already structured by the conditions of limitation.

And so every act of knowing can reinforce the apparent division between the finite knower and the independent known.

The limited person begins from:

“I am here.”

“The world is over there.”

“I must obtain what completes me.”

“I must resist what threatens me.”

And all these experiences produce further involvement in saṃsāra.

Kṣemarāja connects this process with puryaṣṭaka.

The eightfold subtle constitution.

Traditionally comprising the intellect, ego, ordinary mind and five subtle sensory elements.

This subtle constitution, together with karmic tendencies, is implicated in transmigration.

He draws on the Spandakārikā to describe the bound individual experiencing pleasure, pain and successive forms of existence through the activities of the subtle body. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now here’s what makes this so philosophically interesting.

The actual problem isn’t that the mind knows something.

Because from the standpoint of Trika, even the act of limited knowing is a manifestation of consciousness.

The problem is the failure to recognise the conscious reality of the knower and the known.

Suppose I look at a flower.

There’s a cognition.

“Flower.”

There’s a perceiver.

“Me.”

There’s a perceived object.

“That flower.”

Ordinary experience organises those into a relation of difference.

Now, at the conventional level, that’s perfectly functional.

You shouldn’t try to water yourself because you’ve decided the distinction between you and the flower is ultimately nondual.

The flower still needs water.

You probably need breakfast.

But at the metaphysical level, the Trika claim is that the perceiver, perception and perceived object are not ultimately independent of the one self-revealing consciousness.

And without that recognition, the subject mistakes their finite cognitive standpoint for their complete nature.

So the same world can be experienced through two fundamentally different orientations.

One: limited knowledge reinforcing separation.

The other: differentiated cognition recognised as the manifestation of consciousness.

And Vasugupta’s point is that the former constitutes bondage.

Which brings us to a very uncomfortable question.

Why does consciousness fail to recognise its own nature?

Enter Māyā.

Again.

She’s back.

And she’s brought paperwork.

3.3 — Māyā Has Given You Five Absolutely Terrible Subscription Plans
कलादीनां तत्त्वानामविवेको माया ॥३॥

Kalādīnāṃ tattvānām aviveko māyā.

Literal: Māyā is non-discernment concerning the principles beginning with Kalā.

Now we’re getting into the serious machinery of Kashmir Śaivism.

Because Kṣemarāja is not merely going to say:

“The world is an illusion, mate.”

That would be an extremely inadequate account of Trika.

Instead, he explains Māyā through the system of tattvas.

The principles or categories of manifestation.

Thirty-six of them.

A complete metaphysical architecture describing how universal consciousness manifests as limited subjectivity and the world of differentiated experience.

And at a crucial point in that architecture, we encounter Māyātattva.

The sixth tattva.

Followed by five limiting powers called kañcukas.

Literally, coverings or sheaths.

And these are magnificent.

Because each represents a contraction of something that, in the supreme condition of Śiva, is unlimited.

Let’s meet them.

1. Kalā — Limited agency.

Not to be confused with kāla, time.

Kalā here is the limitation of the power of action.

In the supreme condition, Śiva possesses unrestricted freedom of manifestation.

But the individual experiences:

“I can only do certain things.”

“I have limited abilities.”

“I want to accomplish this, but I can’t.”

The infinite power of action is experienced under limitation.

Congratulations.

You’ve been promoted from the universal agent to a person who can’t find his keys.

2. Vidyā — Limited knowledge.

In its supreme condition, consciousness is not restricted by the epistemic limitations of an individual standpoint.

But the contracted being knows particular things.

And doesn’t know other things.

“I know what’s in front of me.”

“I don’t know what’s happening in the next room.”

“I don’t know what tomorrow will bring.”

“I don’t know why the Wi-Fi has stopped working.”

An infinite field of possible knowing has been contracted into a particular cognitive perspective.

3. Rāga — Wanting, attachment, incompleteness.

This one’s especially nasty.

Because the limited individual experiences desire through a sense of lacking something.

“I need that.”

“I’ll be complete when I get this.”

“If that person loves me, everything will finally be okay.”

The fullness of consciousness is experienced as incomplete.

So now the individual begins searching outside themselves for something that will remove the sense of deficiency.

Which is a rather unfortunate undertaking.

Because if the deficiency is rooted in the primordial contraction of consciousness, no particular external acquisition can, by itself, completely undo it.

You can buy another item.

Achieve another goal.

Receive another compliment.

And the basic structure of wanting may simply reappear.

The cosmic shopping cart has no checkout button.

4. Kāla — Time.

Now we’ve got time.

Past.

Present.

Future.

The individual experiences reality through temporal succession.

“I was.”

“I am.”

“I will be.”

The universal freedom of consciousness is now experienced within ordered moments.

You’re no longer simply examining manifestation.

You’re late for something.

Which is perhaps the most humiliating metaphysical contraction of all.

Infinite consciousness.

Late for a bus.

5. Niyati — Restriction, order and necessity.

Finally, there is limitation through ordered circumstances.

You are situated here rather than there.

Events occur within particular relations and conditions.

Your actions have consequences.

You cannot simply disregard the circumstances through which your individual life unfolds.

In the Trika system, niyati involves the restrictions associated with causal and spatial order.

And so the unrestricted nature of Śiva is experienced through a network of definite constraints.

Now consider what has happened.

The supreme consciousness possesses freedom of knowledge and action.

But the individual experiences limited agency, limited knowledge, desire, time and necessity.

This is the architecture of contraction.

And Kṣemarāja’s explanation goes beyond the five kañcukas.

He identifies the principles extending from Kalā down to Pṛthivī, the earth principle, as components of the differentiated embodied condition.

The problem is aviveka.

Non-discernment.

Failing to recognise the real nature and relations of these principles and their dependence on the supreme reality.

And here’s the subtlety.

The tattvas themselves aren’t independent evil substances.

They are principles of manifestation.

The problem is the ignorance under which their differentiated forms are taken to constitute an ultimately separate reality.

Kṣemarāja explicitly raises an objection from the Vijñānabhairava: if knowledge itself is luminous, how can knowledge be bondage?

His answer turns on recognition. Through divine grace, even limited cognition can be known as light; without that recognition, Māyā’s differentiating power sustains bondage. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

So Māyā is not merely someone throwing a blanket over consciousness.

She’s the divine power through which unity is experienced as multiplicity without its underlying unity being recognised.

And that’s why the doctrine is so radical.

The limitation isn’t something that has escaped Śiva’s sovereignty.

It’s a manifestation of that sovereignty.

The universe has not fallen outside the Absolute.

But the individual is experiencing the Absolute through contracted conditions.

And now Vasugupta is going to explain what to do about it.

Apparently the solution is to dissolve the entire cosmos inside your body.

Naturally.

3.4 — Right, Time to Uninstall the Universe from Your Body
शरीरे संहारः कलानाम् ॥४॥

Śarīre saṃhāraḥ kalānām.

Literal: In the body, the dissolution of the constituent powers or parts.

We’ve arrived at our first major concrete contemplative instruction.

And Kṣemarāja explains it using bhāvanā.

Creative contemplation.

Or more precisely, an intentionally cultivated contemplative apprehension.

Now, unlike the sudden recognition characteristic of Śāmbhavopāya, we’re working with structured practices.

The practitioner deliberately employs the mind to transform the way manifestation is understood.

And the practice here concerns the dissolution of the principles of manifestation into their sources.

Saṃhāra.

Withdrawal.

Reabsorption.

Dissolution.

Remember the thirty-six tattvas?

The entire hierarchy extending from Śiva down to earth?

Well, now we’re moving in reverse.

The universe has manifested through successive degrees of differentiation.

The contemplative process reverses that movement.

And the body becomes the field of practice.

Now, Kṣemarāja distinguishes three levels of embodiment.

The gross body, constituted by the gross elements.

The subtle body, including the mental faculties and subtle elements.

And a higher causal level, involving subtler conditions of consciousness and vital power extending towards Samanā.

The practitioner contemplates the progressive dissolution of the manifest principles.

Earth is reabsorbed into its causal principle.

And so on through the hierarchy.

The differentiated structures of manifestation are traced back towards their ultimate source.

But the point isn’t merely to memorise the thirty-six tattvas backwards like an exceptionally committed Sanskrit student.

Although, to be fair, that would be quite impressive.

The point is the transformation of how the practitioner understands the relationship between the manifest and its source.

Imagine looking at a golden ornament.

A bracelet.

You can identify its particular form.

Its weight.

Its decoration.

Its shape.

But you can also recognise that the bracelet’s existence depends upon the gold.

The bracelet is not an independent substance standing apart from the gold.

Now imagine reversing the process conceptually.

You cease attending to the bracelet as a separate form.

You recognise the underlying material reality.

Then you consider that the same reality can appear through many different ornaments.

The distinction between the forms remains intelligible.

But their substantial unity becomes apparent.

This is only an analogy.

The tattva system is far more elaborate, and Śiva is not merely an inert substance underlying objects.

But it illustrates the direction of contemplation.

From differentiation towards source.

From appearance towards its ground.

From limited identity towards consciousness.

And now Kṣemarāja brings in a rather dramatic method.

The body consumed by fire.

He cites the Vijñānabhairava, which describes contemplating a fire arising from the right foot and burning the body, culminating in the appearance of peaceful divine luminosity.

Yes.

A tantric visualisation of your entire body being incinerated.

Lovely.

Very relaxing.

But the fire is imagined within contemplation, not physically applied.

Nobody needs to set themselves on fire.

Nobody needs to damage the body.

The aim is to dissolve the mental fixation upon the body as the ultimate identity.

And this is where the philosophical and practical dimensions meet.

Ordinarily you perceive:

“I am this organism, situated in this particular place, surrounded by a separate universe.”

But through the contemplative dissolution of the embodied principles, that identification is progressively loosened.

The body, subtle faculties and differentiated categories are considered in relation to their source.

Ultimately, that source is consciousness.

Kṣemarāja connects this practice to dhyāna, meditation, and cites the Mālinīvijayatantra on the methods characteristic of Āṇavopāya, including recitation, meditation, phonemic contemplation and visualisation. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

The extraordinary point is that you begin with the very structures through which limitation is experienced.

The body.

The mind.

The elements.

And use them to contemplate what exceeds their limitations.

The prison becomes the meditation hall.

And this is when the text starts getting into yogic physiology.

Because apparently dissolving the universe wasn’t quite enough.

Now we’ve got to deal with the plumbing.

3.5 — Śiva Explains the Cosmic Plumbing System
नाडीसंहारभूतजयभूतकैवल्यभूतपृथक्त्वानि ॥५॥

Nāḍīsaṃhāra-bhūtajaya-bhūtakaivalya-bhūtapṛthaktvāni.

Literal components: Withdrawal of the subtle channels; mastery of the elements; isolation from the elements; separation from the elements.

Look at the length of this thing.

Vasugupta has suddenly remembered Sanskrit allows compounds.

He’s gone from two-word philosophical explosions to what appears to be the password for a particularly secure monastery.

But the sūtra is important because it introduces a series of yogic operations.

Kṣemarāja understands these through the interconnected disciplines of breath regulation, concentration, withdrawal and absorption.

Let’s work through them.

Nāḍīsaṃhāra — Withdrawal of vital movement
Nāḍī refers to a subtle channel through which prāṇa, vital energy, is understood to operate in traditional yogic physiology.

Saṃhāra means withdrawal or reabsorption.

Now, according to the relevant tantric model, ordinary vital activity operates through differentiated channels and functions.

Among the familiar terms are:

Prāṇa — one principal form of vital movement.

Apāna — another, associated with downward-moving or eliminative functions.

Udāna — an upward-associated vital function, especially important in these subtle-body accounts.

And the central channel:

Suṣumṇā.

Kṣemarāja describes the reabsorption of vital movement into the middle channel, involving the relation between prāṇa and apāna and the central function of udāna.

He cites detailed breath-regulation methods from the Svacchandatantra.

These include inhalation, exhalation, retention and the meditative unification of vital movement.

The historical text really does prescribe breath practices. It’s not merely using the vocabulary metaphorically.

But the subtle-channel system is a traditional yogic framework, not an established anatomical network, and forceful breath retention is not something to improvise from an audiobook.

For our purposes, the philosophical point is sufficient.

Ordinary vital activity is experienced through multiple differentiated functions.

The contemplative aim is their unification and withdrawal towards a subtler centre.

Bhūtajaya — Mastery of the elements
Bhūta means an existent or element; here the gross elements.

Jaya means conquest or mastery.

The five familiar elements are:

Earth.

Water.

Fire.

Air.

Space.

In the tantric account, the practitioner employs specific dhāraṇās, concentrations, associated with these elements.

Kṣemarāja cites the Svacchandatantra on their bodily locations and the extraordinary powers traditionally attributed to such practices.

These include concentrating on air at the foot, fire near the navel, earth at the throat, water around the uvula and space at the crown of the head.

It’s an unusual arrangement if you’re expecting a modern anatomy textbook.

But this is ritual-yogic physiology, not ordinary physiology.

And the text’s claims about mastery and supernatural accomplishments should be understood as traditional claims rather than demonstrated physical abilities.

Bhūtakaivalya — Withdrawal from the elements
Kaivalya conveys isolation or separateness.

Here Kṣemarāja associates the process with pratyāhāra.

Withdrawal of attention from sensory objects.

Ordinarily, the mind rushes towards its objects.

A sound arises.

Attention follows.

A smell arises.

Attention follows.

Your phone vibrates.

The entire nervous system declares a state of emergency.

The practitioner deliberately withdraws from this outward dispersion.

Not because sensory experience is inherently evil.

But because the mind is ordinarily dominated by its constant engagement with differentiated objects.

The withdrawal creates a condition in which the source of awareness can become the focus.

Bhūtapṛthaktva — Separation from the elements
And here we arrive at the deepest point.

Pṛthaktva means separation or distinctness.

But this isn’t a denial of embodiment.

It is the recognition that consciousness is not exhausted by the elements constituting embodied experience.

Earth, water, fire, air and space belong to the field of manifestation.

The conscious reality in which they appear is not reducible, within this metaphysical system, to any one of them.

Kṣemarāja associates this culmination with samādhi and recognition of the independent nature of consciousness.

His discussion draws extensively on the Svacchandatantra, moving through prāṇāyāma, dhāraṇā, pratyāhāra and the transcendence of elemental identification. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And notice what we’ve done.

We began with ordinary embodied existence.

Then moved through its vital functions.

Then its elemental constitution.

Then withdrawal from identification with those elements.

And finally towards the conscious reality underlying the entire process.

This is Āṇavopāya in action.

Starting from the limited individual.

Employing definite techniques.

And moving towards recognition.

But Vasugupta immediately issues another warning.

Because apparently the practitioner can get extremely good at these exercises and still completely miss the point.

3.6 — Congratulations, You’ve Acquired Superpowers. Unfortunately, You’re Still Deluded.
मोहावरणात्सिद्धिः ॥६॥

Mohāvaraṇāt siddhiḥ.

Literal: Supernatural accomplishment arises because of the covering of delusion.

Oh, this is BRUTAL.

Absolutely ruthless.

Imagine you’ve spent twenty years practising yoga.

You’ve mastered concentration.

You’ve performed elaborate contemplations of the elements.

You’ve become adept at extraordinary states of absorption.

And perhaps, within the traditional framework, you’ve acquired unusual siddhis.

You arrive before Kṣemarāja.

“Master, I have attained extraordinary abilities.”

And Kṣemarāja replies:

“Very impressive. That’s Māyā.”

“Sorry?”

“Delusion, mate.”

“But I can do remarkable things!”

“Yes, within the realm of delusion.”

“I CAN LITERALLY—”

“Still Māyā.”

Absolutely devastating.

But philosophically, he’s making a precise distinction.

Siddhi — accomplishment, perfection, supernatural power.

Moha — delusion, bewilderment.

Āvaraṇa — covering, concealment.

And the sūtra says that certain extraordinary powers arise within the covering of delusion.

Why?

Because the powers described here concern differentiated realities.

They belong to the manifested domain of experience.

And the acquisition of unusual abilities within that domain doesn’t necessarily disclose the supreme reality beyond limited subject-object relations.

Imagine somebody becomes extraordinarily skilled at lucid dreaming.

They can manipulate their dream environment.

Change the scenery.

Fly through the sky.

Conjure objects.

Perform impossible feats.

Wonderful.

But does mastery over the dream automatically mean they’ve awakened from it?

Not necessarily.

They may simply have become a very powerful character within the dream.

This is analogous to Kṣemarāja’s argument.

The siddhis are not necessarily the same as liberation.

And the deeper problem is that the practitioner may mistake control over phenomena for freedom from fundamental ignorance.

“I can control this.”

“I can manifest that.”

“I have acquired this power.”

Notice that the entire grammar of limited selfhood remains intact.

A finite subject acquires powers over finite or extraordinary objects.

The structure of identification has not necessarily changed.

Kṣemarāja explicitly differentiates powers obtained through yogic techniques from direct manifestation of the supreme principle.

But he also makes an important qualification.

The same disciplines can contribute to genuine recognition when directed towards their highest aim.

He cites teachings from the Netratantra describing withdrawal from subtle objects, meditation on supreme consciousness, and the contemplative understanding of one’s identity with Śiva. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

So he’s not rejecting practice.

He’s rejecting a confusion about its purpose.

The aim of Āṇavopāya isn’t to become the most impressive individual in the universe.

It’s to recognise the conscious reality in which individuality itself appears.

And this is the wonderful paradox.

You could acquire the power to perform astonishing feats.

And still be bound.

Or you could have no unusual abilities whatsoever and recognise the nature of awareness.

Within this system, the latter is the more fundamental spiritual accomplishment.

Which is a terrible blow to the supernatural entertainment industry.

But excellent news for anyone whose principal skill is sitting quietly and investigating consciousness.

3.7 — Defeat Māyā, Unlock the Infinite, and Discover the Knowledge You Never Actually Lost
मोहजयादनन्ताभोगात्सहजविद्याजयः ॥७॥

Mohajayād anantābhogāt sahajavidyājayaḥ.

Interpretive gloss: Through boundless conquest of delusion comes mastery of innate knowledge.

Right.

Now we’re getting somewhere.

Because after warning us about siddhis, Vasugupta gives the actual direction of the practice.

Not the accumulation of extraordinary abilities.

But the conquest of delusion and the manifestation of sahajavidyā.

Innate knowledge.

Let’s unpack the compound.

Moha — delusion.

Jaya — conquest, victory.

Ananta — endless, boundless.

Ābhoga — expansion, extension, pervasive reach.

Sahaja — innate, natural, born together with.

Vidyā — knowledge.

Jayaḥ — conquest or mastery.

And this word sahaja is absolutely beautiful.

Because it doesn’t mean you’ve constructed something entirely new.

It points to something intrinsic.

Something belonging to your own nature.

So what is the knowledge being revealed?

Kṣemarāja identifies it with the recognition of the supreme nature of consciousness, particularly through the movement towards Śivavyāpti.

And now we need to introduce two extremely important terms.

Ātmavyāpti.

Śivavyāpti.

Vyāpti means pervasion or permeation.

Ātmavyāpti is the attainment or recognition of one’s own conscious Self, beyond the limiting identifications constituting ordinary individuality.

Śivavyāpti is the fuller recognition of the supreme Śiva-nature, including the universal manifestation and divine powers.

These are technical levels within the doctrinal explanation, not merely two interchangeable synonyms for having a nice meditation.

Kṣemarāja, citing the Svacchandatantra, distinguishes them.

The practitioner first moves beyond identification with the bonds of limited existence.

But the movement does not end with the recognition of an isolated inner consciousness.

It culminates in the universal recognition of Śiva’s nature.

And this is where Trika differs sharply from interpretations that describe liberation simply as the discovery of a passive witnessing principle separate from the world.

For Trika, the supreme consciousness is not merely a witness.

It is svatantra.

Free.

Creative.

Self-revealing.

The source of manifestation.

So the fullest recognition is not simply:

“I am the awareness watching everything.”

It’s the much more radical understanding:

“The reality of awareness is inseparable from the divine power through which everything manifests.”

Now, Kṣemarāja connects this movement with Unmanā.

A state beyond ordinary conceptual mind, associated with the supreme divine condition.

And here’s an important distinction.

Ordinary knowledge proceeds through successive cognitions.

You know one thing.

Then another.

Then another.

But the supreme knowledge described here isn’t merely the accumulation of many individual cognitions.

It is the nondual recognition of the universal conscious reality.

The yogi does not become liberated by collecting every piece of information in the cosmos.

You don’t need to memorise the entire contents of Wikipedia.

Thank God.

You’ve already got enough Sanskrit to learn.

What is required is a transformation of the fundamental standpoint of knowing.

Kṣemarāja describes the transition from limited self-recognition towards the universal condition of Śiva, emphasising that Āṇavopāya can culminate in Śāktopāya and the fuller awakening of innate knowledge. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And now, ladies and gentlemen, something extraordinary happens.

Because Vasugupta is about to explain what the world looks like when this knowledge has arisen.

And the answer is absolutely gorgeous.

The universe becomes the yogi’s own light.

Not an enemy.

Not an illusion to be despised.

Not a foreign territory from which consciousness needs to escape.

The world becomes the radiance of the Self.

Which means we’re about to enter one of the most beautiful passages in the entire Śiva Sūtras.

The cosmic theatre.

And Śiva, the absolute show-off, is taking centre stage.

3.8 — The Entire Universe Is Apparently Your Own Bloody Sunshine
जाग्रद्द्वितीयकरः ॥८॥

Jāgrad dvitīyakaraḥ.

Interpretive gloss: The awakened yogi sees the apparently second reality — the world — as a ray of their own consciousness.

Now this is a fascinating sūtra.

Because taken without commentary, it’s fairly cryptic.

Let’s unpack it.

Jāgrat — awake, waking, watchful.

Dvitīya — second, another.

Kara — ray, beam, radiance.

And Kṣemarāja provides the crucial interpretation.

The yogi established in innate knowledge remains awake in the deeper sense of being established in recognition.

And the apparently second reality — the world of objects — appears as the radiance of consciousness.

This is the relationship between:

Aham — I.

And:

Idam — this.

Now we’ve encountered these before, but this is where their significance becomes particularly beautiful.

Ordinarily, experience seems to involve two fundamentally separate realities.

There’s me.

And there’s everything else.

I look at a tree.

The tree is over there.

I am over here.

Subject and object.

But within the Trika metaphysical system, the relationship is more subtle.

The supreme Aham, universal I-consciousness, possesses the freedom to manifest the entire domain of Idam, this-ness or objectivity.

The world is not some entirely independent substance standing opposite Śiva.

It is the manifestation of Śiva’s own Śakti.

So what happens when the yogi recognises this?

The objects don’t necessarily disappear.

The tree is still there.

The river still flows.

The mountains remain.

The mosquitoes still apparently regard your ankles as a charitable buffet.

But the ontological understanding of these appearances is transformed.

They’re recognised as expressions of the same consciousness whose nature has become apparent.

Now think of the sun.

The sun gives off light.

Its rays illuminate the world.

And although the rays are distinguishable in their manifestations, they are not some fundamentally foreign power independent of their source.

This is the metaphor Vasugupta uses.

Kara.

A ray.

The world is the radiance of consciousness.

And this is enormously significant because it means liberation isn’t simply a withdrawal from objectivity.

It’s a transformation in the recognition of objectivity.

You’re not supposed to sit there with your eyes closed forever, desperately trying to avoid the existence of tables.

You’re supposed to recognise the fundamental nature of the reality in which the tables appear.

The world is no longer encountered as an ultimately alien second reality.

And Kṣemarāja cites the Vijñānabhairava to support this.

He describes the recognition of consciousness in whatever is perceived — including colour and pleasure — and the attainment of fullness through absorption into omnipresent consciousness.

He also cites a tantric statement describing the entire world as the powers of Śiva, the possessor of Śakti. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now let’s consider the philosophical implication.

If the world is the radiance of consciousness, then recognition doesn’t have to depend on a privileged sensory object.

You don’t need the perfect sunset.

You don’t need an extraordinary vision.

You don’t need the Himalayas to appear through the mist while somebody plays a rather moving piece of devotional music.

Lovely though that would be.

Any manifestation can become an occasion for recognition.

A colour.

A sound.

The feeling of movement.

The appearance of another person.

The emergence of a thought.

Because the essential point is not the particular content.

It’s the conscious reality through which the content becomes manifest.

And here we arrive at something particularly elegant.

In the first book, Vasugupta said:

दृश्यं शरीरम्।

Dṛśyaṃ śarīram.

The seen is the body.

Now he’s explaining the same expanded recognition through the imagery of light.

The world is the yogi’s radiance.

The apparent second is not ultimately separate from the first.

And therefore the liberated yogi can remain awake in the midst of ordinary experience.

Not because they’ve stopped perceiving differences.

But because the differences no longer conceal their conscious source.

And that brings us to a revelation so magnificent that Vasugupta apparently decides to abandon metaphysical exposition and become a theatre critic.

3.9 — Śiva Is Literally Doing a One-Man Show, and You’re Every Character
नर्तक आत्मा ॥९॥

Nartaka ātmā.

Literal: The Self is the dancer.

OH, THIS IS BEAUTIFUL.

Absolutely beautiful.

Two words.

Nartaka.

Dancer.

Ātmā.

Self.

The Self is the dancer.

Now, given everything we’ve just learned, consider what this means.

The universe is the manifestation of consciousness.

The apparent objects are its radiance.

The individual is consciousness experienced under conditions of contraction.

And so what is the relationship between Śiva and the manifest universe?

A performance.

A dance.

A cosmic drama.

The Self is the dancer.

And here’s the really extraordinary part.

The dancer doesn’t simply stand outside the performance controlling it from a distance.

The dance is the dancer’s own activity.

The movement is not an independently existing substance.

It’s the dancer expressing their power.

And this is precisely the kind of relationship Trika describes between Śiva and Śakti.

Śiva is not an inert observer watching Śakti do all the work.

Śiva’s very nature is inseparable from the freedom of his power.

Prakāśa and vimarśa.

The luminosity of consciousness and its reflexive self-awareness.

The manifestation of reality is not foreign to this unity.

It’s its expression.

Now imagine an actor on a stage.

He plays a king.

The king has a kingdom.

A family.

An enormous palace.

A terrible enemy.

And an extraordinarily complicated political situation.

The actor performs every emotion.

Anger.

Love.

Fear.

Triumph.

Loss.

And for the duration of the drama, an entire world becomes manifest through the performance.

But the actor is not ultimately identical with the limited role.

The king can die.

The actor remains.

The kingdom can collapse.

The actor remains.

The play can end.

The actor remains.

And now Vasugupta takes this analogy and applies it to consciousness.

The waking world.

The dream world.

The apparent absence of differentiated experience in deep sleep.

These are different modes of the cosmic performance.

And the Self is the dancer moving through them.

Kṣemarāja explicitly explains the sūtra through the manifestation of different roles across the states of consciousness.

The yogi recognises the underlying Self as the actor displaying these changing conditions through its own powers.

He supports the interpretation with references to the Naiśvāsatantra, Bhaṭṭa Nārāyaṇa’s Stavacintāmaṇi and the Īśvarapratyabhijñā. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And here’s the profound complication.

Ordinarily, the actor becomes identified with the role.

The individual says:

“I am this person.”

“This is my story.”

“These are my successes.”

“These are my failures.”

“These are the terrible things that happened to me.”

And the role becomes the apparent totality of existence.

Imagine an actor who becomes so absorbed in playing Hamlet that he forgets he’s an actor.

He wanders around after the performance convinced that Denmark is in crisis.

He’s still fighting with Claudius.

Still mourning his father.

Still contemplating the skull.

Somebody tries to offer him a sandwich.

“BEGONE, WRETCH, THE KINGDOM IS LOST.”

Mate.

You’re in a theatre.

The performance finished twenty minutes ago.

That’s an imperfect but helpful analogy for the structure of contraction.

The actor becomes absorbed in a particular form of its own manifestation.

Recognition is the disclosure of the actor’s deeper nature.

But there is something even more subtle here.

Because in an ordinary theatre, the actor and the audience are separate people.

The stage is an independent physical structure.

The script has been written by somebody else.

And the actor is constrained by circumstances that exist independently of him.

But in the Trika metaphysical vision, the supreme reality is the source of the entire performance.

The actor.

The activity.

The manifested world.

The powers through which it is experienced.

All belong to the same consciousness.

And that means the cosmic theatre is not merely an analogy for pretending.

It’s an analogy for manifestation through freedom.

The performance is real as manifestation.

But its deepest reality is not separate from its source.

And the actor is none other than Śiva.

Now here’s something worth appreciating.

The sūtra doesn’t say:

“The Self is a spectator sitting motionless while the universe dances.”

It says:

The Self is the dancer.

An active principle.

A manifesting power.

And this is what makes the Trika understanding of consciousness so distinctive.

The divine is not merely pure existence stripped of all activity.

It possesses svātantrya.

Freedom.

Its manifestation is līlā — play.

Not necessarily frivolous amusement, but spontaneous divine expression.

And the realised yogi recognises the relationship between the changing performance and the conscious actor.

Which means that ordinary life doesn’t have to be rejected as spiritually meaningless.

The performance can continue.

The actor can still play the role.

But now the role is understood through its source.

Imagine doing something ordinary.

Walking.

Eating.

Talking.

Listening.

Instead of identifying entirely as the individual agent moving through a separate world, the yogi recognises the activity as a manifestation of conscious power.

The dance continues.

But the dancer is recognised.

And now, naturally, we need a stage.

Because even Śiva can’t put on a production without somewhere to stand.

Apparently the cosmic theatre has a technical rider.

3.10 — The Stage Is Your Inner Self. The Universe Has Gone Full Broadway.
रङ्गोऽन्तरात्मा ॥१०॥

Raṅgo ’ntarātmā.

Literal: The inner Self is the stage.

Right.

So we’ve got our actor.

Śiva.

Very talented.

Infinite range.

Can play literally anybody.

But where does the performance occur?

Vasugupta says:

Raṅgaḥ.

The stage.

And:

Antarātmā.

The inner self.

Now this needs careful interpretation, because it would be tempting to say:

“The stage is pure consciousness.”

Which sounds beautiful.

But Kṣemarāja’s explanation is more specific.

Here antarātman refers to the inner individual constitution — particularly the subtle and causal dimensions of embodied experience.

This isn’t simply another name for supreme consciousness.

The distinction matters.

Remember puryaṣṭaka?

The eightfold subtle constitution comprising the mental faculties and subtle sensory principles.

Kṣemarāja connects the stage with this subtle embodiment.

He also mentions the causal condition associated with voidness and vital energy.

In other words, the stage is the inner structure through which the individual performance becomes possible.

Think about how you experience the world.

You’re not merely receiving a sequence of completely uninterpreted objects.

Your mental faculties organise experience.

Memory contributes continuity.

Desire contributes motivation.

The senses contribute differentiated contents.

The intellect establishes judgements.

The ego appropriates experiences.

And the entire inner apparatus participates in the construction of your lived world.

Now Kṣemarāja isn’t proposing a modern neuroscientific theory.

He’s describing a tantric metaphysical structure.

But the theatrical metaphor becomes extraordinarily illuminating.

The actor manifests different roles through the stage of the inner being.

And the subtle body provides the conditions through which those roles are enacted.

Take a waking experience.

You see somebody you recognise.

Immediately, memory comes into play.

You remember past interactions.

You anticipate future possibilities.

You feel attraction or aversion.

You construct a particular identity in relation to them.

All of this belongs to the inner apparatus through which experience unfolds.

And then you dream.

The same individual may appear in a completely different setting.

Their behaviour changes.

Your own role changes.

The entire situation is transformed.

Yet the experience arises through the powers of consciousness operating in a different condition.

The stage has changed its scenery.

But the deeper actor remains.

Now here’s the point.

The ordinary person becomes absorbed in the stage and the roles.

The realised yogi recognises their underlying nature.

Kṣemarāja explains that the actor manifests the world’s drama through the successive activities of the inner senses, while the subtle body constitutes the stage upon which the performance unfolds. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

This also explains why the inner faculties aren’t simply enemies to be destroyed.

They’re the instruments of manifestation.

And within Āṇavopāya, they can become instruments of recognition.

The individual starts from the stage.

The bodily and mental structures.

And through their contemplation, moves towards recognition of the actor.

Now, think of the relationship between these last two sūtras.

Nartaka ātmā.

The Self is the dancer.

Raṅgo ’ntarātmā.

The inner self is the stage.

The dancer acts.

The stage provides the locus of the drama.

And now all we need is an audience.

But because this is Kashmir Śaivism, the audience is going to be something completely unexpected.

3.11 — Your Eyes Are Sitting in the Front Row Watching Śiva Perform
प्रेक्षकाणीन्द्रियाणि ॥११॥

Prekṣakāṇīndriyāṇi.

Literal: The senses are the spectators.

LADIES AND GENTLEMEN.

WE HAVE A FULL THEATRE.

The Self is the dancer.

The inner self is the stage.

And the senses?

The bloody audience.

Now this is the sort of metaphor you could spend years contemplating.

Let’s unpack it.

Prekṣaka — spectator, observer, someone who watches a performance.

Indriya — sense faculty.

The plural forms give us:

Prekṣakāṇi indriyāṇi.

The senses are the spectators.

Now, in ordinary life, we think of our senses primarily as channels for acquiring information about an external world.

Eyes receive visual information.

Ears receive sound.

The skin responds to touch.

And so on.

But in the Trika metaphysical system, the senses are powers of consciousness.

They’re not ultimately independent entities capable of knowing without the conscious reality through which experience occurs.

And here’s where Kṣemarāja makes an absolutely beautiful move.

The senses, ordinarily directed outward towards their objects, are described as becoming inwardly aware of their own essential nature.

And this inward recognition is associated with camatkāra.

Astonishment.

Wonder.

Aesthetic delight.

The extraordinary savour of consciousness recognising itself.

Now, you might remember this from the first book.

विस्मयो योगभूमिकाः।

Vismayo yogabhūmikāḥ.

The stages of yoga are wonder.

And now the same aesthetic dimension returns through the theatre metaphor.

The universe is a performance.

The Self is the actor.

The senses are the spectators.

And the experience of recognition becomes a kind of supreme aesthetic delight.

This is where Trika’s philosophy connects beautifully with the wider Kashmiri tradition of aesthetics.

Think about watching a magnificent play.

For a moment, you’re completely absorbed.

The performance draws you in.

The characters, emotions, movement, music and atmosphere form an extraordinary unity.

You experience something beyond simply processing individual bits of sensory information.

There’s a distinctive delight in the manifestation itself.

And Kṣemarāja uses the concept of camatkāra to describe the heightened experience of the yogi whose senses become established in recognition.

But the metaphysical claim goes further than ordinary aesthetic appreciation.

In an ordinary performance, the spectator remains distinct from the actor.

Here, the senses themselves are powers of the same Self that performs the drama.

So the separation between actor and spectators is not ultimate.

The conscious reality is the source of both.

And now comes a rather extraordinary consequence.

The senses no longer have to function exclusively as sources of distraction.

Seeing can become an occasion for recognition.

Hearing can become an occasion for recognition.

The appearance of a sensation can become an occasion for recognition.

Because the senses themselves are not outside Śiva.

Their capacity to disclose manifestation belongs to Śakti.

And this is a major difference between simply suppressing sensory experience and recognising its conscious source.

The point isn’t:

“Never look at anything.”

“Never listen to anything.”

“Never enjoy anything.”

It’s more subtle.

Don’t mistake the objects disclosed by the senses for realities ultimately independent of consciousness.

And don’t mistake the sensory faculties themselves for autonomous sources of awareness.

Recognise the conscious reality through which the entire sensory drama becomes manifest.

Kṣemarāja explicitly invokes the Kaṭha Upaniṣad in explaining the inward turn of the senses.

The ordinary direction of attention is outward.

But the wise seeker turns inward towards the Self.

And in the context of this sūtra, the result is the delightful recognition of the conscious actor through the senses themselves. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now imagine standing in a busy marketplace.

People moving.

Voices overlapping.

Colours appearing.

Smells rising from food stalls.

A tremendous sensory performance.

Ordinarily, attention is scattered among its objects.

But in the contemplative interpretation, the practitioner begins recognising the underlying conscious reality through which all these perceptions appear.

The sights and sounds don’t necessarily have to stop.

The theatre continues.

But the spectators discover the true nature of the actor.

And this is why the three sūtras belong together.

नर्तक आत्मा।

The Self is the dancer.

रङ्गोऽन्तरात्मा।

The inner self is the stage.

प्रेक्षकाणीन्द्रियाणि।

The senses are the spectators.

What an extraordinary little metaphysical trilogy.

The universe is a drama of consciousness.

And the yogi, through recognition, discovers the conscious unity underlying the entire performance.

Now, the obvious question is:

How does this recognition become stable?

Because it’s all very well understanding the theatre metaphor.

But you can intellectually appreciate the idea that consciousness is the actor and still spend your entire life absolutely furious about the plot.

So Vasugupta moves to the next question.

What enables the yogi to recognise the subtle conscious reality underlying the performance?

The answer involves a rather important form of intelligence.

3.12 — Use Your Bloody Intelligence, but the Proper Kind This Time
धीवशात्सत्त्वसिद्धिः ॥१२॥

Dhīvaśāt sattvasiddhiḥ.

Interpretive gloss: Through refined understanding, the subtle reality of inner consciousness becomes manifest.

Now this is a beautiful statement.

Because we’ve spent so much time discussing how limited knowledge contributes to bondage that you might imagine Trika regards intelligence as something fundamentally suspicious.

But no.

Vasugupta is not advocating stupidity.

Thank goodness.

Sanskrit studies may continue.

Let’s unpack the words.

Dhī — intelligence, insight, understanding.

Vaśāt — through the power of, by virtue of.

Sattva — being, essential reality; here interpreted by Kṣemarāja in a particular yogic sense.

Siddhi — accomplishment, attainment, manifestation.

And we need to be particularly careful with sattva.

Because you may know sattva as one of the three guṇas.

Sattva, rajas and tamas.

Clarity, activity and inertia.

But that is not Kṣemarāja’s intended sense here.

He interprets sattva as the subtle inner reality characterised by sphurattā — the luminous flashing forth of I-consciousness — and parispanda, subtle inner pulsation.

Now this is quite something.

Because it means dhī isn’t simply the ordinary intellect collecting facts.

It’s an understanding capable of discerning the actual nature of the Self.

Kṣemarāja specifically defines this intelligence through skill in contemplating or recognising tāttvikasvarūpa — one’s essential reality.

And through that refined understanding, the subtle inner reality becomes manifest.

Now let’s connect this to the theatre analogy.

An actor may possess tremendous technical skill.

They understand timing.

Movement.

Expression.

The emotional structure of a performance.

They know how to bring a particular inner feeling into visible expression.

And Kṣemarāja actually invokes this kind of theatrical expertise.

Just as refined intelligence enables accomplished dramatic expression, spiritual intelligence enables recognition of the subtle inner reality of consciousness.

It’s an unusual but extraordinarily elegant comparison. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And it has a deep philosophical implication.

Understanding isn’t necessarily an obstacle.

Misunderstanding is.

A conceptual structure that reinforces the independence of the limited individual can sustain bondage.

But an understanding that discloses the universal nature of consciousness can participate in liberation.

Remember our encounter with:

वितर्क आत्मज्ञानम्।

Vitarka ātmajñānam.

Reflective ascertainment is Self-knowledge.

The same general principle returns.

It’s not merely the presence or absence of thought that matters.

It’s the relation of thought to recognition.

Now, this is especially important for anyone studying Trika through philosophical analysis.

You can investigate the structure of experience.

Examine the distinction between perceiver and perceived.

Study the relation between prakāśa and vimarśa.

Consider how limited identity arises through saṃkoca.

And these investigations can help clarify what the tradition is pointing towards.

But the aim isn’t merely to accumulate an increasingly elaborate conceptual representation of consciousness.

It’s to understand the conscious reality in which those representations themselves arise.

Imagine a man building an extraordinarily accurate map of the ocean.

He’s got currents.

Depths.

Salinity.

Tides.

Every conceivable measurement.

Absolutely magnificent cartography.

But he’s forgotten that he’s standing in water.

At some point, a different kind of recognition is required.

The map is useful.

But the map is not the ocean.

And the understanding of consciousness is not identical to the direct recognition of consciousness.

Still, the map can direct the investigation.

And that’s the role of refined intelligence here.

Dhī becomes an instrument of disclosure.

The apparent limitation of cognition is transformed into a means of recognising its source.

And now Vasugupta gives us the result.

A word we’ve been circling around since the first sūtra.

Svātantrya.

Freedom.

Absolute freedom.

And this is one of the most beautiful statements in the text.

3.13 — Svātantrya: The Entire Point of the Bloody Operation
सिद्धः स्वतन्त्रभावः ॥१३॥

Siddhaḥ svatantrabhāvaḥ.

Literal: The state of being free and independent is attained.

Now we’re talking.

Because this is where everything comes together.

Siddhaḥ — accomplished, attained, realised.

Svatantra — independent, self-governing, free.

Bhāvaḥ — state, condition, manner of being.

And the key word is svatantra.

Let’s look at its construction.

Sva — one’s own, belonging to oneself.

Tantra — here conveying the principle of control, dependence or governance.

Svatantra therefore means self-dependent, independent, governed by oneself rather than by another.

And the abstract noun:

Svātantrya.

Freedom.

Independence.

Autonomy.

But in Trika, it becomes something much more radical than ordinary personal autonomy.

It is the intrinsic freedom of supreme consciousness.

The power through which Śiva knows, acts and manifests.

And Kṣemarāja explains that this freedom involves sahajajñatva and kartṛtva.

Innate knowledge and agency.

The natural powers of knowing and acting.

Now think about the limited individual.

The aṇu.

Its knowledge is restricted.

Its agency is restricted.

Its experience is structured by time, necessity, desire and differentiated cognition.

And the entire third book began by describing precisely this condition.

But now the yogi, through the recognition of the subtle reality of consciousness, attains the condition of freedom.

What’s being reversed?

The fundamental limitations through which Śiva experienced himself as a finite subject.

The point isn’t simply:

“I can now do whatever I want.”

That’s ordinary individual desire enlarged into a fantasy.

Nor is it:

“I am an exceptionally powerful personality who deserves to control everybody else.”

That would be ahaṃkāra wearing a very expensive Śaiva costume.

The claim is that the yogi recognises the nature of consciousness as intrinsically free.

And this freedom isn’t merely freedom from particular external obstacles.

It’s the freedom through which manifestation itself is understood.

Consider the difference.

Ordinary freedom means:

“I have a choice between several possibilities.”

Divine svātantrya means something more fundamental.

The very existence of manifested possibilities belongs to the freedom of consciousness.

Śiva doesn’t merely select among pre-existing options arranged by some independent cosmic administrator.

Within the Trika system, the appearance of those options is itself an expression of Śakti.

That’s why svātantrya is so central.

It distinguishes Śiva from an inert absolute.

Consciousness is not merely luminous.

It is self-aware, active, and endowed with the freedom of manifestation.

Kṣemarāja explicitly describes the attained freedom as sovereignty involving natural knowledge and agency.

He cites a teaching attributed to Nāthapāda identifying the power of freedom with Kālī, the supreme power.

And he cites the Svacchandatantra on the yogi’s sovereignty over the tattvas, beings, letters and mantras through contemplation of Śiva. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, the latter includes a traditional claim of extraordinary yogic sovereignty.

That should not be mistaken for a scientifically established power to control the physical universe.

But its philosophical significance is profound.

The yogi’s highest freedom is not another limited object of attainment.

It is the recognition of the divine power that makes manifestation possible.

And this brings us back to the beginning.

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Consciousness is the Self.

But now that statement has acquired a far richer meaning.

Consciousness is not merely a container of appearances.

It is not merely an observer.

It is the free, self-revealing reality through which appearances become manifest.

Svātantrya is inseparable from what Śiva is.

And liberation is recognition of that nature.

Not the construction of an entirely new Self.

Not the acquisition of something fundamentally external.

The recognition of one’s own deepest reality.

And there’s something rather beautiful about the fact that the aṇu — the limited individual — is not abandoned.

The journey begins from limitation.

But the destination is the recognition of freedom.

It’s not the replacement of the individual with a completely different metaphysical substance.

It’s the disclosure of the universal nature underlying individuality.

And now Vasugupta adds another sentence.

A very short one.

But an absolutely enormous claim.

3.14 — Doesn’t Matter Where You Go, Śiva’s Already There
यथा तत्र तथान्यत्र ॥१४॥

Yathā tatra tathānyatra.

Literal: As there, so elsewhere.

Now, this is a magnificent little statement.

Four words.

And Kṣemarāja uses it to explain the universality of the freedom just described.

Let’s unpack it.

Yathā — just as, in whatever manner.

Tatra — there.

Tathā — so, likewise.

Anyatra — elsewhere.

As there, so elsewhere.

Now the question is:

What exactly is being compared?

Kṣemarāja answers by referring to the yogi’s manifestation of freedom.

Just as the yogi experiences the divine power of manifestation in their own body, so that power is understood to operate elsewhere.

The practitioner who is steadily established in recognition doesn’t confine freedom to one particular location.

And the traditional interpretation includes an extraordinary claim.

The realised yogi is said to be capable of manifesting divine freedom beyond the limits of their own bodily situation.

That is part of the commentary’s metaphysical and yogic claim, rather than merely a poetic statement about feeling peaceful while travelling.

But it also has a deeply illuminating nondual significance.

Because if consciousness is the fundamental reality of the manifest universe, then its nature isn’t ultimately restricted to one particular physical location.

Think about the ordinary individual.

They experience themselves as situated here.

Within this body.

At this time.

Surrounded by things located elsewhere.

And these distinctions are perfectly meaningful within empirical experience.

But the Trika claim is that the universal conscious reality is not ultimately divided by those spatial distinctions.

The limited standpoint is localised.

The supreme reality is not.

And the realised yogi recognises that distinction.

Now consider the practical implication without exaggerating the supernatural claims.

Suppose someone has a profound meditative experience.

They sit quietly.

Their mind becomes still.

There’s an extraordinary sense of openness.

The usual sense of separation temporarily relaxes.

And they conclude:

“Wonderful. This is where I experience Śiva.”

Then they leave the meditation room.

Traffic.

Noise.

People.

Problems.

And suddenly:

“Ah, unfortunate. Śiva has apparently remained inside the meditation hall.”

But if the fundamental nature of consciousness is not confined to that place, why would recognition be restricted to those particular conditions?

The same reality is present through the changing circumstances of experience.

The task is the stabilisation of recognition, not the construction of an exclusively sacred geographical enclosure.

And this is precisely where Kṣemarāja connects the sūtra to the yogi’s sustained attention.

The manifested freedom is described as present wherever the yogi remains established in that recognition.

He cites the Svacchandatantra and the Spandakārikā, emphasising the natural, nonartificial character of this freedom. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, compare this with the first book’s teaching that the entire seen world is the body of the realised Self.

The same metaphysical movement appears again.

The limited individual ordinarily identifies consciousness with a particular organism.

But recognition reveals its universal nature.

And therefore the yogi no longer fundamentally confines the Self to the individual body.

Now, this doesn’t mean ordinary physical constraints magically disappear for everyone who understands the doctrine.

You still need to eat.

You still need to sleep.

You still need to respect other people’s boundaries.

And attempting to walk through a wall because you’ve read a Sanskrit aphorism is not an advisable method of testing nondual metaphysics.

But within the tradition, the deepest freedom of consciousness is not limited by the ordinary spatial identity of the finite subject.

As there, so elsewhere.

The same Śiva.

The same Śakti.

The same fundamental reality.

And now we’ve reached the final sūtra of this instalment.

And after all this talk about divine freedom, cosmic dancing and the universality of consciousness, Vasugupta has one last instruction.

It’s astonishingly simple.

But perhaps one of the most practically important in the entire third section.

Pay attention to the seed.

3.15 — The Whole Universe Comes from One Seed, and You Keep Getting Distracted
बीजावधानम् ॥१५॥

Bījāvadhānam.

Literal: Attention to the seed.

Oh, that’s lovely.

Absolutely lovely.

We’ve travelled through fifteen sūtras.

We’ve examined the limited mind.

The conditions of bondage.

The five kañcukas.

The dissolution of the universe through contemplation.

The regulation of vital movement.

The dangers of supernatural powers.

The awakening of innate knowledge.

The cosmic theatre.

The attainment of divine freedom.

And now Vasugupta brings us back to something exceptionally direct.

Bījāvadhānam.

Attention to the seed.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Bīja — seed, germ, source.

Avadhāna — attention, attentiveness, concentration.

And Kṣemarāja gives us a beautifully precise interpretation.

The seed is Parā Śakti.

The supreme power.

The source of the entire manifested universe.

And its nature is sphurattā — the luminous, vibrant manifestation of self-aware consciousness.

Now, let’s think about that.

Ordinarily a seed is something small.

You plant it.

It grows.

And eventually an entire tree emerges.

The tree develops branches.

Leaves.

Flowers.

Fruit.

And within those fruits, further seeds.

The seed appears almost insignificant compared with the vast complexity that unfolds from it.

But it contains, in a particular causal sense, the potential for the subsequent development.

Now, Kṣemarāja is using the seed as a metaphysical principle.

Not suggesting that there is a tiny physical object somewhere inside your skull containing miniature galaxies.

The seed is the divine power from which manifestation proceeds.

And that power is not independent of consciousness.

It is Parā Śakti.

The supreme creative power of Śiva.

Now, here’s the beautiful part.

Kṣemarāja explains that even after the attainment of freedom, the yogi should not become udāsīna — indifferent or inattentive.

Instead, they must repeatedly direct the mind towards the seed.

The commentary uses the expression:

भूयो भूयश्चित्तनिवेशनम्।

Bhūyo bhūyaś citta-niveśanam.

Repeatedly placing or establishing the mind.

That’s Kṣemarāja’s own explanatory wording.

And it is absolutely crucial.

Because it explains why the text doesn’t simply end after announcing the attainment of freedom.

You might imagine that once the yogi has recognised divine consciousness, there’s nothing left to discuss.

Congratulations.

Done.

Roll credits.

Everybody goes home.

But Kṣemarāja insists upon continuing attention to the supreme source.

Why?

Because the teaching concerns the stable establishment of recognition, not merely a passing experience.

Remember the end of Book II?

विद्यासंहारे तदुत्थस्वप्नदर्शनम्।

Vidyāsaṃhāre tadutthasvapnadarśanam.

When pure knowledge subsides, dreamlike differentiation reappears.

That was our warning.

You can recognise the truth and then become absorbed once again in limited cognition.

And now, in Book III, we find the corresponding instruction.

Return your attention to the seed.

Again.

And again.

Not necessarily by repeating a particular verbal formula endlessly.

But by sustaining contemplative attention towards the fundamental power of consciousness.

Kṣemarāja identifies the seed as the cause of the universe and cites the Netratantra on the supreme Goddess as the source of the gods and divine powers, characterised symbolically through fire and moon. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now this is where the symbolism becomes magnificent.

Fire and moon.

Power and luminosity.

The varied differentiations through which divine manifestation is described.

The supreme Śakti is the source from which the innumerable powers proceed.

But the practitioner is instructed to attend not merely to their countless differentiated expressions.

Rather, to the source.

The seed.

And notice how this relates to everything we’ve studied.

At 3.1, the Self in its contracted condition was identified with mind.

At 3.2, limited knowledge was bondage.

At 3.3, Māyā was associated with the failure to discern the principles of manifestation.

Then the practitioner began the work of contemplation.

The body was dissolved into its causal principles.

The senses and subtle faculties became instruments of practice.

The whole universe was recognised as the manifestation of consciousness.

The yogi attained the understanding of divine freedom.

And now the mind, which was initially bound by its own limited operation, becomes directed towards the very source of manifestation.

The mind that was absorbed in the branches turns towards the seed.

And here there’s a wonderful relationship with Śāktopāya.

Remember:

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Not every wandering thought automatically functioning as a liberating formula, but mental activity transformed through contemplation of the supreme reality.

And now:

बीजावधानम्।

Bījāvadhānam.

Attention to the seed.

The mind becomes directed towards Parā Śakti.

The source of the entire universe.

The power through which manifestation arises.

The consciousness in which the entire process appears.

Now, consider an ordinary practical analogy.

You sit down to meditate.

You recognise the presence of awareness.

A thought appears.

You follow it.

A memory appears.

You follow that.

A plan appears.

You begin constructing tomorrow.

A worry appears.

You begin solving a problem that doesn’t currently exist.

And approximately twenty minutes later, you’re mentally having an argument with a man you haven’t seen since secondary school.

Remarkable.

You’ve travelled through an entire personal universe without leaving your cushion.

But now the instruction is:

Return to the source.

Not by violently suppressing every mental event.

Not by trying to construct an artificial blankness.

But by directing attention towards the conscious reality through which all those events become manifest.

Again.

And again.

And again.

The point is not that each return creates consciousness.

Consciousness was never absent in the first place.

The practice concerns recognition.

And the stabilisation of that recognition.

This is where Trika becomes extremely practical.

Because the metaphysics is enormous.

Thirty-six tattvas.

Divine powers.

Phonemic cosmology.

Subtle bodies.

Worlds within worlds.

But the fundamental orientation is beautifully simple.

The entire manifestation arises through Śakti.

And the practitioner turns towards its source.

Not as some distant object.

But as the conscious power fundamental to their own existence.

And now the cosmic theatre becomes almost unbearably beautiful.

The dancer is Śiva.

The stage is the inner being.

The spectators are the senses.

The drama is the manifestation of the universe.

And the seed is the supreme power through which the entire production becomes possible.

The actor has been performing for so long that he’s become absorbed in the role.

He thinks he’s the character.

He worries about the plot.

He fights with the other characters.

He clings to the scenery.

He becomes terrified that the performance might end.

And then something shifts.

He recognises the actor.

He recognises the stage.

He recognises the spectators.

He recognises the divine power through which the whole performance appears.

And the drama can continue.

But the fundamental misunderstanding is beginning to dissolve.

The performance no longer conceals its source.

Bījāvadhānam.

Attention to the seed.

What a way to finish this first stretch of Āṇavopāya.

The Great Reveal — What Did Vasugupta Actually Just Do?
Fifteen sūtras.

And the progression is astonishingly coherent.

Think about where we began.

आत्मा चित्तम्।

Ātmā cittam.

The Self is mind.

The apparently limited individual identifies with the mental apparatus.

Knowledge reinforces the distinction between subject and object.

Māyā sustains the misrecognition of the principles of manifestation.

And the individual remains entangled in conditioned existence.

But then the practices begin.

शरीरे संहारः कलानाम्।

Śarīre saṃhāraḥ kalānām.

The manifested principles are contemplated in their withdrawal towards their source.

The body becomes a field of practice.

The subtle faculties and vital powers become instruments of concentration.

And the yogi begins moving beyond the ordinary identification with differentiated manifestation.

But there is a danger.

मोहावरणात्सिद्धिः।

Mohāvaraṇāt siddhiḥ.

Extraordinary powers can remain within delusion.

The practitioner must not mistake unusual experiences for recognition of the supreme.

Then the deeper knowledge arises.

मोहजयादनन्ताभोगात्सहजविद्याजयः।

Mohajayād anantābhogāt sahajavidyājayaḥ.

The conquest of delusion culminates in the mastery of innate knowledge.

And now the world becomes transformed in meaning.

It is recognised as the radiance of consciousness.

Then the extraordinary theatrical sequence begins.

The Self is the dancer.

The inner self is the stage.

The senses are the spectators.

And refined intelligence discloses the subtle inner nature of consciousness.

Finally:

सिद्धः स्वतन्त्रभावः।

Siddhaḥ svatantrabhāvaḥ.

Freedom is attained.

Not merely personal autonomy.

But recognition of the supreme divine freedom of consciousness.

And this freedom is not restricted to a single particular locus.

यथा तत्र तथान्यत्र।

Yathā tatra tathānyatra.

As there, so elsewhere.

But the yogi must not become inattentive.

The supreme source remains the object of contemplative orientation.

And therefore:

बीजावधानम्।

Bījāvadhānam.

Attention to the seed.

The Most Beautiful Thing About This Section
There’s something extraordinary about the structure of the three books.

Book I began:

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

Book II began:

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Book III began:

आत्मा चित्तम्।

Ātmā cittam.

The Self is mind.

Three statements.

Three different standpoints.

But not three ultimately separate realities.

From the highest standpoint, the Self is universal consciousness.

From the standpoint of Śakti, mental activity can become a vehicle of divine recognition.

From the standpoint of the limited individual, the Self appears contracted into identification with the mind.

And the entire practice involves recognising the relationship between these conditions.

The point is not to create a supreme Self somewhere beyond the individual.

Nor is it to turn the individual ego into an infinitely powerful superhuman.

It’s to recognise the conscious reality through which the individual, its mind, its body, and the entire universe become manifest.

And the most beautiful part?

The limited individual is never actually outside Śiva.

Even the ignorance belongs to the manifestation of consciousness.

Even the effort belongs to Śakti.

Even the practices are expressions of divine power.

And even the forgetting occurs within the reality that can never be fundamentally absent.

The dancer has never ceased to be the dancer.

But he has become absorbed in his performance.

The recognition isn’t a new actor entering the stage.

It’s the original actor recognising his own nature.

And the play continues.

End of Book III, Part I — Āṇavopāya, Sūtras 3.1–3.15.

Next, at 3.16:

आसनस्थः सुखं ह्रदे निमज्जति।

Āsanasthaḥ sukhaṃ hrade nimajjati.

Established in the seat, the yogi effortlessly sinks into the lake.

And we’re returning to that magnificent image from the end of the first book.

The Great Lake.

The oceanic source of manifestation.

But now we’ll discover why Kṣemarāja’s idea of āsana is much deeper than simply sitting cross-legged on a cushion.

The next fifteen sūtras also take us through the creation of one’s world, the ending of rebirth, the Mothers associated with the Sanskrit phonemes, the infusion of the fourth state into waking, dreaming and deep sleep like oil, the transformation of breath, and the extraordinary declarations:

शिवतुल्यो जायते।

Śivatulyo jāyate.

One becomes like Śiva.

And:

कथा जपः।

Kathā japaḥ.

Conversation becomes mantra-recitation.

Apparently even talking can become worship.

Which is excellent news for a tradition that’s been talking about itself for approximately a thousand years.

Textual basis: Vasugupta’s Śiva Sūtras, third section 3.1–3.15, with Kṣemarāja’s Śivasūtravimarśinī. The explanations follow the commentary’s doctrinal sequence, including its citations of the Spandakārikā, Vijñānabhairava, Svacchandatantra and Netratantra. The audiobook narration is an original paraphrase; modern analogies are illustrative rather than translations. Siddhis and subtle-body mechanisms are identified as traditional claims.

