There’s a table.

A chair.

Some dust floating in a beam of sunlight.

An irritating noise outside.

And the question is:

Has the divine disappeared because objects have returned?

From the Trika standpoint, no.

Because the objects themselves are manifestations of consciousness.

The apparent distinction between meditation and ordinary experience doesn’t establish two fundamentally different ultimate realities.

The supreme conscious nature is not restricted to the absence of objects.

And Kṣemarāja makes precisely this point.

He explains that even as the intellect and prāṇa extend towards external objects, the realised yogi remains established in universal consciousness without the contracted standpoint returning.

He cites the Pratyabhijñā tradition and a passage associated with Ānandabhairava concerning equal vision across divine beings, social distinctions and things. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now let’s be careful.

Equal vision doesn’t mean empirical differences cease to matter.

A tiger and a cup of tea remain very different things.

And treating them identically could lead to a rather unpleasant afternoon.

It doesn’t mean morality disappears.

Nor that harmful actions become acceptable merely because everything is Śiva.

The philosophical point is that the underlying conscious reality is not ultimately divided by the differentiated forms through which it manifests.

And here’s the extraordinary result.

The yogi can participate in the world without losing recognition of its source.

The senses function.

The body functions.

Speech occurs.

Action occurs.

But their apparent differentiation no longer conceals the universal conscious reality.

This is integration.

And it’s a profound contrast with the idea that liberation consists solely in withdrawing from the world.

The inward movement is important.

But so is the outward return.

The contemplative state and ordinary activity are not ultimately alien realities.

Now, this is where things become particularly relevant to the problem of stabilising recognition.

Because Vasugupta is not claiming that everyone who has experienced turya will automatically remain established in it.

Quite the opposite.

The next two sūtras address what happens when the recognition becomes interrupted.

And they are absolutely brilliant.

Because we’re about to encounter something deeply familiar.

You recognise the truth.

You forget.

You remember again.

You become distracted.

You recognise again.

And Vasugupta is going to explain the entire cycle through the relationship between the middle of experience and its conscious source.

Which is exactly the sort of thing you’d expect from a man who has already informed us that the universe is an elaborate one-man theatrical production.

3.23 — You Were Enlightened for Five Minutes, Then Your Brain Started Being a Dick Again
मध्येऽवरप्रसवः ॥२३॥

Madhye ’varaprasavaḥ.

Literal: In the middle, inferior manifestation arises.

Oh, this one hurts.

Because it’s absolutely devastatingly relatable.

We’ve just been through an extraordinary sequence.

The yogi enters the Great Lake.

The world is recognised as the expression of Śakti.

Innate knowledge becomes established.

Turya permeates waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

The outward movement of vital activity no longer obscures the conscious source.

Absolutely beautiful.

And then Vasugupta says:

In the middle, inferior manifestation arises.

For God’s sake.

WE WERE DOING SO WELL.

What happened?

Let’s unpack it.

Madhye — in the middle, in the intervening condition.

Avara — lower, inferior, lesser.

Prasavaḥ — production, generation, arising.

And here’s where Kṣemarāja becomes wonderfully precise.

He explains that the practitioner may experience the bliss of turya at the initial and final boundaries of the ordinary states.

But may fail to maintain recognition throughout their middle portions.

And in that intervening condition, ordinary mental activity reasserts itself.

Vyutthāna.

The state of ordinary mental emergence or activity, contrasted with spiritual absorption.

This is not necessarily evil or immoral thought.

It’s the return of limited cognition, particularly when its differentiated contents are no longer recognised through their supreme source.

Now think about how extraordinary this is.

At the transition between states, the practitioner may recognise an opening.

Perhaps as a thought subsides.

Perhaps at the emergence of waking.

Perhaps in a contemplative pause.

But then ordinary mental activity resumes.

And the mind becomes absorbed in its contents.

There’s the perception.

Then the interpretation.

Then the appropriation.

Then the personal story.

Then the emotional reaction.

And suddenly the universe has become a matter of what somebody said to you yesterday.

Śiva has been reduced from universal consciousness to a participant in a completely imaginary argument.

Again.

Now Kṣemarāja’s actual point is not simply that people become annoyed.

That’s our modern illustration.

His concern is the interruption of the yogi’s continuous recognition of turya.

The practitioner can apprehend the fourth state at privileged moments yet fail to sustain it throughout the entirety of experience.

And this is why the word madhya is so important.

The middle.

The interval.

The ordinary continuity of experience.

Not merely its beginnings and endings.

Imagine listening to a symphony.

At the beginning, you become profoundly attentive.

The opening notes are extraordinary.

Then you start thinking about dinner.

Twenty minutes pass.

You become mentally involved in some unrelated matter.

Then the final movement begins.

You suddenly return.

And the ending is magnificent.

Now, you did indeed experience the opening and closing of the symphony.

But you missed much of the performance.

Something analogous is being described here.

Recognition at the boundaries does not necessarily imply uninterrupted recognition throughout the intervening states.

And now comes an important distinction.

Kṣemarāja does not say that the practitioner is permanently lost.

The condition of limitation has reappeared, but the recognition already cultivated has not necessarily become entirely ineffective.

The possibility of returning remains.

He explicitly relates the phenomenon to Book II’s final sūtra, where the subsidence of pure knowledge produces dreamlike differentiated experience.

He also invokes the Mālinīvijayatantra to warn that the yogi can become diverted towards transient enjoyments and extraordinary attainments. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And this is where the text becomes unusually psychologically acute.

Because the mere memory of a profound experience is not identical to living within its recognition.

You can remember what clarity felt like.

You can remember an extraordinary philosophical insight.

You can even describe it with complete precision.

But at the present moment, you may be operating through precisely the limitations that the insight had disclosed.

And then the mind performs another trick.

It begins chasing the previous recognition.

“I need to get back.”

“I’ve lost it.”

“How do I recover the state?”

But the very structure of trying to obtain an experience can reinforce the sense of being a limited subject separated from something desirable.

It’s an exceptionally awkward situation.

You’ve recognised the source.

Then forgotten.

Then turned the memory of recognition into another object of attachment.

Congratulations.

You’ve managed to become attached to freedom.

A spectacular achievement.

But Vasugupta doesn’t leave us here.

Because the very next sūtra explains how recognition returns.

And this one is absolutely beautiful.

3.24 — You Forgot You Were Śiva. Here’s How to Remember Without Running Back to Your Cushion.
मात्रास्वप्रत्ययसन्धाने नष्टस्य पुनरुत्थानम् ॥२४॥

Mātrāsvapratyayasandhāne naṣṭasya punarutthānam.

Interpretive gloss: Through uniting the objects of experience with awareness of one’s true Self, what was lost arises again.

Right.

This is one of the most practically significant instructions in the entire work.

Because we’ve just seen how the recognition of turya can become interrupted by ordinary mental activity.

And now Vasugupta says it can be restored.

But the method is fascinating.

It’s not necessarily a withdrawal from the objects of experience.

It’s a transformation of the relationship between those objects and consciousness.

Let’s unpack the compound.

Mātrā — measure or measured object; here the objects of cognition.

Sva — one’s own.

Pratyaya — cognition, apprehension, recognition.

Sandhāna — joining, connecting, bringing into union.

Naṣṭasya — of that which was lost.

Punarutthānam — rising again, reappearance, restoration.

The critical term is svapratyaya.

In Kṣemarāja’s interpretation, it concerns the recognition of the true I-consciousness.

Not the ordinary individual ego.

Not merely thinking your personal name.

But awareness of the Self as the conscious reality underlying manifestation.

And when the objects of experience are brought into relation with that recognition, the previously obscured bliss of turya reappears.

Now let’s understand the actual method.

A sound arises.

Ordinarily, you might simply follow the sound outward.

You identify it.

Judge it.

React to it.

Construct a story around it.

But the practitioner can instead apprehend the sound through the recognition of consciousness.

Not:

“This sound is a completely independent thing confronting a fundamentally separate subject.”

But:

“This sound is appearing within the luminous reality of consciousness.”

Now, be precise.

The sound isn’t merely being imagined away.

The object remains an object within the conventional structure of experience.

But its ultimate metaphysical status changes.

It is recognised as a manifestation of consciousness.

And Kṣemarāja’s commentary is very explicit about this.

He cites the Svacchandatantra, instructing the practitioner to investigate the supreme reality through whatever the eyes perceive, speech expresses, mind thinks, intellect determines or ego appropriates.

That’s an extraordinary list.

Because it means the entire apparatus of ordinary cognition can become a field of recognition.

Seeing.

Speaking.

Thinking.

Judging.

Even the activity of ego-appropriation.

The practitioner doesn’t have to begin by annihilating all these faculties.

They can become occasions for investigating the conscious source.

And now comes an even more radical teaching.

Kṣemarāja cites further passages from the Svacchandatantra explaining that the yogi should recognise the supreme reality wherever the mind moves.

Wherever it goes.

Not only during meditation.

Not only inside a shrine.

Not only while repeating a mantra.

Wherever cognition occurs.

And the explanation is profoundly nondual.

If the whole universe is understood as pervaded by Śiva, where can the mind actually go that is ultimately outside Śiva?

That’s the point. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And now imagine this.

You’re walking through a market.

There’s a noise.

You become distracted.

You start thinking about something that needs doing.

A memory arises.

An emotion appears.

Then you remember the teaching.

Instead of treating the thought as evidence that you’ve departed from some sacred reality, you investigate the consciousness in which it appears.

The thought itself becomes an occasion for recognition.

Not because every thought is automatically liberating.

But because the practitioner can recognise its manifestation within the conscious source.

This is extraordinarily important.

Because otherwise spiritual practice can become a strange dualism.

There’s the sacred state.

And the ordinary state.

There’s meditation.

And there’s life.

There’s Śiva.

And there’s everything that distracts me from Śiva.

But if the world is Śiva’s own manifestation, that division cannot be ultimate.

The point is not to manufacture a private spiritual experience insulated from reality.

It’s to recognise reality through all its appearances.

And here we can see why Śāktopāya was so important.

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Mental activity can become an instrument of recognition when its source is understood.

And now:

मात्रास्वप्रत्ययसन्धाने नष्टस्य पुनरुत्थानम्।

Through union between objects and true Self-awareness, what was lost returns.

Notice the structure.

The practitioner has become distracted by objects.

But the objects themselves can become the means through which recognition is restored.

The supposed obstacle becomes the occasion for liberation.

The very cognition that previously sustained limitation can now disclose its source.

It’s extraordinary.

Imagine somebody searching frantically for the door out of a room.

They examine every wall.

Every corner.

Every possible escape route.

Eventually, somebody points out that the wall they’ve been leaning against contains the door.

The door was not created by the discovery.

But the relationship to the room changes completely.

And that’s the kind of reversal being described.

The practitioner doesn’t have to escape from manifestation to find consciousness.

Manifestation itself can disclose consciousness.

And this is where Kṣemarāja’s interpretation becomes particularly beautiful.

Recognition is recoverable through the very world that seemed to obscure it.

But once that recognition becomes fully established, what happens to the yogi?

Apparently he becomes like Śiva.

Oh, excellent.

We’ve reached the promotion ceremony.

3.25 — Congratulations, You’ve Become Like Śiva. Please Don’t Put It on Your LinkedIn.
शिवतुल्यो जायते ॥२५॥

Śivatulyo jāyate.

Literal: One becomes equal or comparable to Śiva.

RIGHT.

THERE IT IS.

The big one.

We’ve been waiting for this.

Thirty-two sūtras in the first two books.

Twenty-five into the third.

And finally Vasugupta announces:

Śivatulyo jāyate.

You become like Śiva.

Now, before anybody purchases a trident and starts demanding worship, we need to understand this properly.

Let’s break down the Sanskrit.

Śiva — Śiva, the auspicious Lord.

Tulya — equal, comparable, similar in nature.

Jāyate — becomes, arises, comes to be.

And Kṣemarāja’s explanation is absolutely crucial.

The yogi becomes like Śiva through the intensification and stabilisation of recognition.

But this does not mean the ordinary individual ego becomes an independent rival deity.

There’s no cosmic job vacancy.

Śiva isn’t retiring.

He’s not looking for a replacement.

The yogi’s recognition of their supreme conscious nature is what makes this equality intelligible.

And Kṣemarāja introduces another important term.

Turyātīta.

Beyond the fourth.

Now, we’ve already encountered turya.

The fourth state underlying waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

But turyātīta points towards a condition in which the recognition of supreme consciousness is no longer apprehended merely as another distinguishable state among states.

The ordinary divisions of experience no longer obscure the underlying nondual reality.

And the yogi is said to attain equality with Śiva through this full identification.

Now here’s a particularly fascinating detail.

Kṣemarāja distinguishes the yogi’s condition while still embodied from the condition following the body’s disappearance.

While the body remains, the liberated yogi is described as like Śiva.

After the body’s dissolution, Kṣemarāja expresses the traditional position as complete identity with Śiva.

That’s a genuine distinction in the commentary, not something we should erase for the sake of a catchy slogan. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And this brings us to the concept of jīvanmukti.

Liberation while living.

The individual body continues.

The senses function.

The mind may still engage in ordinary activity.

But the yogi is no longer fundamentally identified with the limitations of that embodied condition.

Now consider how radical this is.

The yogi doesn’t need to wait until physical death for the recognition of the supreme nature of consciousness.

The recognition can occur within life.

The manifest body is not, in itself, an absolute barrier to liberation.

And that is particularly important for Trika.

Because the tradition is not simply concerned with abandoning the world.

It describes the possibility of recognising Śiva in the midst of manifestation.

The body remains.

The world remains.

But the relationship to both is transformed.

And here’s the wonderful thing.

The equality is not established through a competition.

The yogi doesn’t outperform Śiva.

Doesn’t become more powerful than Śiva.

Doesn’t acquire a better divine résumé.

It’s a recognition of identity at the level of fundamental conscious nature.

Imagine a wave spending its entire existence trying to become water.

It practises diligently.

Studies hydrodynamics.

Attends retreats.

Memorises the ocean’s sacred teachings.

And eventually realises that water was never something it lacked.

The wave doesn’t become a second ocean.

Nor does it need to destroy its wave-form to recognise its watery nature.

The analogy isn’t perfect, because Śiva’s freedom is not the passive physical substance of water.

But it captures the key point.

The deepest reality was never absent.

The limitation belonged to the failure of recognition.

And now the yogi is established in that recognition.

Which raises a practical problem.

If you’ve become like Śiva, what do you actually do with the rest of your life?

Do you continue meditating?

Do you perform rituals?

Do you undertake vows?

Do you abandon society?

Do you start an extraordinarily expensive spiritual academy?

Well.

Vasugupta’s answer is rather wonderful.

The body itself becomes your vow.

3.26 — Being Alive Is Your Religious Practice. Don’t Make It Weird.
शरीरवृत्तिर्व्रतम् ॥२६॥

Śarīravṛttir vratam.

Literal: The continuance or activity of the body is the vow.

Now this is beautiful.

Because we’ve reached an extraordinary transition.

Earlier, the practitioner was engaged in definite practices.

Meditation.

Concentration.

Mantra.

Contemplation.

The discipline of the body and senses.

But now the yogi has become established in recognition of Śiva.

So what becomes of religious practice?

Vasugupta says:

Śarīravṛttir vratam.

The body’s continuation is the vow.

Let’s unpack it.

Śarīra — body.

Vṛtti — activity, functioning, continuation, maintenance.

Vrata — vow, religious observance, sacred discipline.

And Kṣemarāja gives a specific explanation.

The liberated yogi remains embodied.

They continue to experience the circumstances of bodily existence.

And the continuation of that body becomes their sacred observance.

Now this is not an invitation to do whatever you like and call it Tantra.

That’s a misunderstanding worth preventing immediately.

The sūtra does not mean that every impulse of the body automatically constitutes enlightened conduct.

Nor does it abolish responsibility for harmful action.

Kṣemarāja is describing the condition of a yogi whose identity is already established in the supreme reality.

Their life has become inseparable from their recognition of Śiva.

And therefore the ordinary continuation of embodiment is no longer fundamentally separate from worship.

Think about the conventional structure of a religious vow.

You undertake a particular discipline.

Perhaps fasting.

Perhaps recitation.

Perhaps a form of worship.

You establish a special period or activity dedicated to the divine.

There’s ordinary life.

And then there’s the vow.

But Kṣemarāja describes a state in which that separation has been transformed.

The yogi remains devoted to the supreme consciousness through the very continuation of embodied existence.

Living itself becomes a sacred expression.

And the commentary explicitly mentions the traditional understanding that the remaining circumstances of embodiment are allowed to run their course.

The yogi doesn’t have to destroy the body.

Or treat bodily survival as a spiritual failure.

Kṣemarāja also invokes a tantric analogy involving fire, vital energy and the Self, in which the embodied condition is related to the attainment of supreme Śiva.

And he draws on the Trikasāra tradition to explain how ordinary bodily and ritual activity can be transformed through recognition. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, here’s what I find particularly beautiful.

The yogi has ceased to regard the body as the ultimate Self.

But that doesn’t make the body worthless.

The body becomes an expression within consciousness.

And therefore its continued activity can be understood through its divine source.

This is an enormously affirmative position.

It’s not:

“My body is disgusting, and I must escape it.”

Nor:

“My body is the supreme and complete identity.”

It’s:

“My body is a manifestation of consciousness, and its activity can occur without obscuring the recognition of that consciousness.”

And this creates an extraordinary possibility.

Eating can become an expression of sacred life.

Walking.

Resting.

Breathing.

Speaking.

Acting.

Not because each activity automatically produces liberation.

But because the liberated yogi is no longer fundamentally estranged from the divine nature of manifestation.

The life itself becomes permeated by recognition.

And here’s the lovely reversal.

Earlier, bodily identification was a central aspect of bondage.

Now bodily continuation is a vow.

Same body.

Different standpoint.

The body that once seemed to imprison consciousness becomes the field through which its freedom is expressed.

And this prepares us for one of the most wonderful sūtras in the entire work.

Because Vasugupta now extends the same transformation to speech.

And the result is absolutely hilarious when translated literally.

Conversation becomes mantra-recitation.

Which means that apparently the enlightened yogi can go down to the market, ask for some vegetables, and still be engaged in worship.

Now that’s efficient.

3.27 — Every Conversation Is a Mantra. You’ve Been Chanting the Entire Time, You Absolute Donut.
कथा जपः ॥२७॥

Kathā japaḥ.

Literal: Conversation is mantra-recitation.

OH, THIS IS GLORIOUS.

Absolutely glorious.

Two words.

Katha.

Speech, conversation, discourse.

Japa.

Repetition or recitation of mantra.

And Vasugupta announces:

Kathā japaḥ.

Conversation is japa.

Now, if you take that out of context, it’s completely ridiculous.

Imagine somebody spending three hours complaining about the weather.

And announcing:

“Actually, I have just performed three hours of supreme tantric mantra practice.”

Well.

Not quite.

Kṣemarāja makes a very important qualification.

We’re talking about the speech of the yogi established in recognition of supreme I-consciousness.

And this is where the whole philosophical journey becomes extraordinarily beautiful.

Remember the beginning of Book II?

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Now, in Book III:

कथा जपः।

Kathā japaḥ.

Speech is mantra-recitation.

The same basic transformation is being expressed at a more complete level.

The mind no longer simply produces differentiated cognitions that reinforce bondage.

It becomes an instrument of recognition.

And now even ordinary speech can arise from the uninterrupted awareness of the supreme Self.

But here’s the really important part.

Kṣemarāja connects this with the Mahāmantra.

The great mantra of universal I-consciousness.

Aham.

I.

Not the little ego.

Not the ordinary personality.

But the primordial I-consciousness of Śiva.

And within the Trika understanding, this I-consciousness is inseparable from the manifestation of the universe.

Now imagine the yogi speaking.

The words arise.

They may concern ordinary things.

Food.

Travel.

A philosophical discussion.

A question.

An answer.

But throughout the movement of speech, the yogi remains established in the universal conscious source.

So the verbal activity no longer obscures that source.

Instead, it becomes an expression of the uninterrupted recognition of Śiva.

And Kṣemarāja cites the Kālikākrama to explain that the yogi’s speech constitutes japa because it arises from the continual apprehension of the great mantra’s deity as one’s own Self.

This is very different from mechanically repeating a formula.

The highest japa is inseparable from recognition.

And then he cites the Vijñānabhairava.

Which is where things become particularly interesting.

Because the text describes spontaneous mantra in relation to the movement of breath.

Haṃsa.

The subtle mantra associated with the natural movement of respiration.

In a traditional account, the outgoing and incoming breaths are correlated with the syllables sa and ha, producing the continuous mantra of life.

And the text gives the traditional daily count:

21,600 repetitions.

Now, this is a conventional yogic calculation based on an assumed average breathing rhythm, not a universal physiological measurement applicable to every human being.

But its symbolic significance is extraordinary.

The living being is understood as continually participating in an unspoken mantra.

Breathing itself becomes associated with the divine power of manifestation.

And Kṣemarāja relates this to the higher understanding of japa as uninterrupted contemplation of the supreme reality. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now consider the progression.

Ordinarily, a mantra seems to be something you deliberately repeat.

You sit down.

You count the syllables.

You focus your attention.

You try to maintain concentration.

But in the condition described here, the yogi’s awareness of the divine is no longer restricted to deliberate recitation.

Speech itself becomes an expression of recognition.

And this is magnificent.

Because it means the sacred is no longer confined to special verbal formulas.

Not because the formulas have become meaningless.

But because the conscious source of all speech has been recognised.

The mantra is no longer merely an activity performed at particular times.

The underlying I-consciousness is apprehended as continuously present.

And here’s the funniest thing.

You can be talking about something completely ordinary.

And the philosophical meaning is transformed by the standpoint from which the speech arises.

Imagine a realised yogi asking:

“Could you pass the salt?”

An entirely ordinary sentence.

No Sanskrit.

No bells.

No elaborate mudrās.

Not even a particularly mystical tone of voice.

But within Kṣemarāja’s understanding, the speech of such a yogi is not separate from the continuous contemplation of Śiva.

The salt is still salt.

The request still means what it means.

The linguistic function remains perfectly ordinary.

But the awareness underlying the act is not limited by the ordinary identification with separate selfhood.

And thus ordinary discourse can be understood as japa.

Now, we should not flatten the teaching into:

“Everything you say is a mantra, therefore say whatever you like.”

That would be a rather spectacular misunderstanding.

The condition of recognition comes first.

The transformation of speech follows from it.

And this is why the text doesn’t simply teach a new religious ritual.

It teaches a transformation of the conscious standpoint from which activity occurs.

The body continues as a vow.

Speech becomes japa.

And now Vasugupta announces what the realised yogi gives to others.

And it’s not money.

Not supernatural powers.

Not a complicated initiation certificate.

Self-knowledge.

The highest gift.

3.28 — The Greatest Gift Isn’t Money. It’s Helping Somebody Realise What They Actually Are.
दानमात्मज्ञानम् ॥२८॥

Dānam ātmajñānam.

Literal: The gift is knowledge of the Self.

This is absolutely beautiful.

Because after all the metaphysics, after the entire cosmic theatre, after the extraordinary powers and the subtle bodies and the phonemic goddesses, Vasugupta arrives at something profoundly human.

Dāna.

Giving.

And what does the realised yogi give?

Ātmajñāna.

Knowledge of the Self.

Let’s unpack it.

Dānam — gift, giving, donation.

Ātman — Self.

Jñānam — knowledge.

Together:

The gift is knowledge of the Self.

Now think about the ordinary understanding of generosity.

You give somebody food.

Money.

Clothing.

Shelter.

Help.

All of these can be valuable.

But within the liberation-centred framework of Trika, there is something more fundamental that can be communicated.

Recognition of one’s true nature.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly interprets this sūtra through the relationship between teacher and disciple.

The realised yogi imparts knowledge of the Self to students.

Not simply information about a philosophical doctrine.

But the liberating recognition of consciousness.

And here’s where Kṣemarāja gives us a fascinating little Sanskrit discussion.

He explores the word dāna through several traditional verbal associations.

Giving.

Cutting.

Purifying.

Preserving.

Now, these shouldn’t all be treated as the ordinary historical derivation of dāna.

Rather, Kṣemarāja is using a traditional style of exegetical wordplay to disclose different dimensions of the teaching.

The gift communicates knowledge.

That knowledge cuts through the apparent separation between Śiva and the universe.

It purifies the limitations associated with Māyā.

And it helps preserve the recognition of the supreme reality.

An extraordinarily efficient gift.

It gives.

Cuts.

Cleans.

And preserves.

A complete metaphysical household appliance.

But then Kṣemarāja returns to the central meaning.

The realised yogi gives Self-knowledge to disciples.

And he explicitly uses a word associated with students dwelling near the teacher.

Antevāsin.

Somebody living in proximity to the teacher.

This is a reminder that we’re dealing with a historical tradition of instruction and transmission, not simply an abstract theory of consciousness.

And Kṣemarāja goes further.

He cites a traditional claim that accomplished yogis established in the unity of Śiva, Śakti and the individual can liberate others through their presence or touch.

This is a claim about divine grace and the extraordinary efficacy of realised teachers within the tantric tradition.

It should not be presented as an experimentally established capacity to confer liberation by physical contact. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

But philosophically, it tells us something profound about the ideal teacher.

The teacher isn’t merely someone who knows more facts than the pupil.

They’re someone whose recognition of the supreme reality can become an occasion for awakening in another.

And now think about the relationship between this sūtra and the previous one.

At 3.27:

Kathā japaḥ.

Speech is mantra-recitation.

At 3.28:

Dānam ātmajñānam.

The gift is Self-knowledge.

Speech has become rooted in recognition.

And now speech can become an instrument through which recognition is communicated.

That’s an extraordinary progression.

The very language that once contributed to bondage can become a vehicle of liberation.

Remember Mātṛkā?

The mother of the phonemes?

The same power whose differentiated activity could conceal consciousness can now communicate knowledge of its source.

And therefore the teacher’s speech participates in the liberating function of Śakti.

Now, this is where Trika’s understanding of anugraha becomes important.

Grace.

The power through which the supreme reality becomes recognised.

In the broader Śaiva system, anugraha is one of the divine functions associated with Śiva.

Manifestation.

Maintenance.

Withdrawal.

Concealment.

Grace.

And the last is especially significant.

Because the same supreme reality that freely permits contraction also possesses the power through which that contraction can be overcome.

Within the tradition, the genuine teacher can function as a vehicle of that grace.

And there’s a wonderful implication.

The highest gift is not necessarily a new object added to the recipient’s possessions.

It is the disclosure of what was already fundamental.

Imagine somebody convinced they’ve lost their home.

They spend years wandering.

Searching.

Asking for directions.

Building temporary shelters.

And eventually somebody tells them:

“You’re standing in it.”

The gift wasn’t a newly constructed house.

It was the recognition of their actual situation.

Again, that’s only an analogy.

But it captures the structure of pratyabhijñā.

Recognition.

The teacher does not manufacture the student’s Self.

They help disclose its nature.

And perhaps that is why this sūtra is so beautiful.

It takes the most extraordinary metaphysical doctrine imaginable and expresses its highest communicative function through a very ordinary human act.

Giving.

The gift is knowledge.

And the knowledge is of the Self.

But now Vasugupta makes an important distinction.

Because not everybody who speaks about Śiva is necessarily capable of awakening others.

And the next sūtra is perhaps the most linguistically peculiar in the entire section.

Even Kṣemarāja has to deal with some rather adventurous etymological interpretations.

Which is fantastic.

The man is apparently about to explain why people can get so carried away with Sanskrit wordplay that they start inventing entire philosophies out of syllables.

Finally.

Somebody said it.

3.29 — Not Everyone Who Knows Sanskrit Is Qualified to Be Your Guru
योऽविपस्थो ज्ञाहेतुश्च ॥२९॥

Yo ’vipastho jñāhetuś ca.

Interpretive gloss: The one established in sovereignty over the powers is a source of liberating knowledge.

Now this is a particularly interesting sūtra.

And it requires careful explanation.

Because the Sanskrit is unusual.

Let’s begin.

Yaḥ — he who.

Avipasthaḥ — interpreted by Kṣemarāja through a special tantric derivation.

Jñā — knowledge, or the knowing power.

Hetuḥ — cause, source, means.

Ca — and, also; Kṣemarāja understands it here in an emphatic sense.

Now, the central difficulty is avipasthaḥ.

Kṣemarāja explicitly gives an interpretive analysis involving avi, a term associated with the bound beings.

And pa, the protector.

The powers presiding over the bound beings are therefore brought into the interpretation.

Remember the goddesses from 3.19?

The Mothers.

The powers of Mātṛkā.

The divine forces associated with differentiated cognition.

Well, now the realised yogi is said to be established in sovereignty over those powers.

He is no longer merely governed by them through unconscious limitation.

Rather, he knows their true source.

And therefore possesses the capacity to communicate liberating knowledge.

Now, this is a crucial distinction.

The ordinary individual is subject to the powers of differentiated cognition.

The realised yogi recognises those powers as manifestations of Śakti.

And this recognition changes their relationship to the entire śakticakra.

The wheel of powers.

The yogi is no longer simply a dependent subject carried along by the processes of cognition.

The powers are understood through their supreme source.

And now the extraordinary implication.

Only someone established in genuine recognition can fully perform the liberating function of the teacher described in the preceding sūtra.

At 3.28, we learned:

Dānam ātmajñānam.

The gift is knowledge of the Self.

But how can someone communicate that recognition if they remain fundamentally confused about their own nature?

And that’s Kṣemarāja’s question.

A person who remains entirely subordinate to the powers of differentiated cognition cannot, simply by possessing information, exercise the same transformative authority attributed to the realised teacher.

Now, this shouldn’t be exaggerated into the claim that anyone who lacks enlightenment is incapable of explaining anything useful.

Obviously people can teach grammar.

History.

Philosophy.

Meditation techniques.

Even if they haven’t attained the supreme condition described here.

The distinction concerns the traditional ideal of the guru as an effective source of liberating recognition.

And here’s where the commentary gets unexpectedly funny.

Kṣemarāja discusses alternative interpretations of the sūtra that derive elaborate meanings from individual syllables.

For example, extracting yogīndra from yo, vijñāna from vi, and further meanings from the remaining parts.

The result becomes an intricate symbolic reading.

Very impressive.

Lots of Sanskrit.

Extremely esoteric.

Possibly the sort of thing somebody could turn into a three-hour lecture with a beautiful slideshow.

But Kṣemarāja doesn’t accept the method in this instance.

He objects that the proposed interpretation doesn’t fit the intended connection between the words sufficiently well.

And he makes a wonderful methodological point.

If you’re permitted to extract virtually any meaning from syllabic resemblance, you can generate countless interpretations of every sūtra.

Which is a rather devastating observation.

Because he’s essentially warning against unconstrained Sanskrit mysticism.

Not every attractive phonetic coincidence is a valid interpretation.

Not every elaborate symbolic explanation is faithful to the text.

And the fact that an interpretation sounds extraordinarily profound does not automatically mean it’s philologically justified.

Which is excellent advice for anybody studying Tantra.

You can construct an entire philosophical system out of a word.

But unless the derivation, context and doctrinal argument support it, you may simply be producing very sophisticated nonsense.

Kṣemarāja explicitly rejects the alternative syllable-by-syllable interpretation in favour of his explanation through the powers of knowledge and the realised yogi’s sovereignty over them. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And that makes this sūtra particularly significant.

Because it’s not merely describing the ideal spiritual teacher.

It’s also demonstrating something about how the tradition interprets its own scripture.

The words are extraordinarily compressed.

The meanings are often esoteric.

But interpretation still requires discipline.

A profound metaphysical doctrine does not excuse careless reading.

And that brings us to the final sūtra of this instalment.

One of the most extraordinary statements in the entire text.

Because after all the discussion of recognition, liberation, embodiment, mantra, and the teacher’s gift, Vasugupta returns to the fundamental nature of the universe.

And announces something absolutely enormous.

The entire world is the expansion of the yogi’s own Śakti.

Now that’s a conclusion.

3.30 — The Entire Universe Is Your Own Śakti. Try Not to Get a God Complex.
स्वशक्तिप्रचयोऽस्य विश्वम् ॥३०॥

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

Literal: The universe is the expansion or aggregate of his own power.

There it is.

Absolutely magnificent.

Because this sūtra brings together almost everything we’ve studied.

Let’s unpack it.

Sva — one’s own.

Śakti — power, divine energy, the dynamic potency of consciousness.

Pracayaḥ — accumulation, expansion, growth, unfoldment.

Asya — of him, his.

Viśvam — the universe, the whole.

And Vasugupta says:

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the expansion of his own Śakti.

Now, before the ego starts getting excited, we need to understand the standpoint.

Because this is not a claim that your ordinary personality is personally responsible for inventing every object in the cosmos.

It’s not:

“I have manifested the universe with my thoughts.”

Nor:

“Everything exists because I personally imagined it.”

That would be a rather unfortunate confusion between limited ego and supreme consciousness.

The relevant sva belongs to the realised yogi whose identity is established in the universal nature of Śiva.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly connects this sūtra with the earlier statement:

शिवतुल्यो जायते।

Śivatulyo jāyate.

One becomes like Śiva.

Because the yogi has attained this recognition, the universe is apprehended as the manifestation of their own divine power.

Not the private power of an isolated human personality.

But the universal Śakti inseparable from Śiva.

And this is why the word pracaya is so important.

The universe is not merely something observed by consciousness.

It is the unfoldment of its power.

Now think about the metaphysical implications.

In some models of consciousness, awareness is described primarily as passive.

It receives appearances.

Observes objects.

Registers experiences.

But in Trika, supreme consciousness is not merely a witness.

It possesses svātantrya.

Freedom.

And this freedom includes the power of manifestation.

Icchā.

Will.

Jñāna.

Knowledge.

Kriyā.

Action.

The entire universe is understood through these divine powers.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly associates the world’s manifestation with kriyāśakti.

The power of action.

Now, here’s the extraordinary move.

The universe isn’t an independent reality that consciousness happens to illuminate.

It is an expression of consciousness through its own manifesting power.

And Kṣemarāja quotes the Kālikākrama tradition to explain that consciousness shines in various forms both inwardly and outwardly.

Internally, through experiences such as pleasure.

Externally, through objects such as a pot.

And in both cases, the ultimate reality is consciousness.

Now let’s understand what that means.

Imagine holding a clay pot.

There’s the pot.

The visual perception.

The tactile experience.

The recognition:

“This is a pot.”

The object appears as something distinct from the perceiver.

And conventionally, that’s perfectly meaningful.

You can fill the pot with water.

You can place it on a table.

You can accidentally drop it.

And discover that nondual metaphysics doesn’t automatically make pottery shatterproof.

But Kṣemarāja is making a different claim.

The very appearance and apprehension of the object belong to consciousness.

The distinction between the object and its cognition does not establish an ultimate independence from the conscious reality through which both are manifest.

And this is where the argument becomes extraordinarily subtle.

Because prakāśa, the light of consciousness, is not merely physical illumination.

It’s the self-revealing nature through which manifestation becomes apparent.

And vimarśa is the reflexive awareness inseparable from that luminosity.

The supreme reality knows itself and manifests its powers.

Therefore the world is not fundamentally alien to consciousness.

It’s the unfoldment of Śakti.

And Kṣemarāja takes this directly back to the doctrine of contraction.

In his exposition, the one conscious reality freely manifests multiplicity and the limitations under which individuals experience themselves as separate.

The limited being is not outside Śiva.

It is Śiva’s consciousness appearing under contraction.

And liberation is the recognition of that conscious source.

He cites the Netratantra and Kālikākrama in explaining this relation between consciousness, its powers, and the apparent world of objects. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, this is where the full philosophical structure becomes extraordinarily beautiful.

At 3.1:

आत्मा चित्तम्।

Ātmā cittam.

The Self is mind.

We began with the limited individual’s identification with the internal cognitive apparatus.

At 3.2:

ज्ञानं बन्धः।

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Knowledge is bondage.

Differentiated cognition reinforces limitation.

At 3.3:

कलादीनां तत्त्वानामविवेको माया।

Kalādīnāṃ tattvānām aviveko māyā.

Māyā involves the failure to discern the true nature of the principles of manifestation.

And now, twenty-seven sūtras later:

स्वशक्तिप्रचयोऽस्य विश्वम्।

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the unfoldment of his own Śakti.

Look at the progression.

The beginning:

“I am a limited individual confronting an external universe.”

The culmination:

“The universe is the manifestation of the divine power inseparable from my true conscious nature.”

But the important thing is that the apparent limited individual has not become a separate creator.

The standpoint has changed.

The underlying reality is recognised.

And this is what makes the teaching so radical.

The universe doesn’t have to disappear for liberation to occur.

The body doesn’t necessarily have to be destroyed.

Ordinary perception doesn’t have to be abolished.

The mind doesn’t have to become incapable of thought.

The senses don’t have to cease functioning.

The entire apparatus of manifestation can continue.

But its fundamental nature is no longer misunderstood.

And that’s the great reversal.

In bondage, the individual experiences the universe as something fundamentally external.

In liberation, the universe is recognised as the expression of consciousness.

The same world.

But not the same understanding.

And this brings us back to the cosmic theatre.

Nartaka ātmā.

The Self is the dancer.

The dancer has been performing through countless forms.

Bodies.

Minds.

Worlds.

Experiences.

Emotions.

The entire drama of birth and death.

The actor appears as the king.

The servant.

The hero.

The villain.

The audience.

And the theatre itself.

And then the actor recognises the nature of the performance.

The universe hasn’t necessarily stopped.

The performance can continue.

But the actor is no longer fundamentally lost in the role.

And now, through this recognition, the entire play is understood as the expression of divine power.

Svātantrya.

Freedom.

Śakti.

Power.

Prakāśa.

Luminosity.

Vimarśa.

Reflexive awareness.

Pratyabhijñā.

Recognition.

The world is not a mistake standing outside consciousness.

It is the extraordinary manifestation of consciousness itself.

And the yogi recognises that the freedom through which it appears is inseparable from their own deepest nature.

Not the freedom of a little ego attempting to control everything.

The freedom of Śiva.

Absolutely magnificent.

The Great Reveal — What Just Happened in Sūtras 3.16–3.30?
Let’s look at this progression, because it’s far too coherent to treat as fifteen unrelated spiritual sayings.

3.16–3.18: Immersion, manifestation and liberation.

The practitioner becomes established in supreme Śakti.

The Great Lake of consciousness is entered.

Manifestation is recognised through the power of the Self.

And the continuity of innate knowledge is said to end the cycle of rebirth.

The movement is from practice towards sustained recognition.

3.19–3.21: The danger of forgetting, and the method of integration.

The phonemic goddesses can reintroduce limitation when recognition subsides.

Therefore turya must be infused into waking, dreaming and deep sleep.

The practitioner enters the supreme condition through inward, nonconceptual awareness, ultimately going beyond reliance on the grosser techniques.

The movement is from intermittent recognition towards continuity.

3.22–3.24: Returning to the world without losing Śiva.

Vital activity unfolds outward again.

Equal vision becomes possible.

But inferior mental activity can reappear in the intervening states.

Through joining the objects of experience with true I-consciousness, the lost recognition can be restored.

The movement is from withdrawal towards recognition within ordinary activity.

3.25–3.29: The transformation of the realised person’s life.

The yogi becomes like Śiva.

Bodily continuation becomes a vow.

Speech becomes mantra-recitation.

Self-knowledge becomes the supreme gift.

And the yogi established in sovereignty over the powers becomes capable of communicating liberating wisdom.

The movement is from personal liberation towards the transformation of action and instruction.

3.30: The universal recognition.

The universe is the unfoldment of Śakti.

What previously appeared as an independent external reality is understood through the freedom of supreme consciousness.

And this returns us to the most fundamental principle of the entire text.

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

Everything else has been an increasingly detailed exploration of what those two words mean.

The Final Teaching — You Don’t Have to Leave the World to Recognise Śiva
Here’s what makes this sequence so extraordinary.

The yogi first enters the Great Lake.

The supreme source.

The apparently transcendent condition.

But the teaching does not end with immersion.

The practitioner returns to manifestation.

The breath continues.

The body continues.

The senses continue.

The mind continues.

Speech continues.

And the world continues.

But the relationship to all these things has changed.

The yogi no longer regards them as independent realities fundamentally separate from consciousness.

They are recognised as expressions of Śakti.

And this is why the final sequence is so beautiful.

Śarīravṛttir vratam.

The body’s continuation is a vow.

Kathā japaḥ.

Conversation is mantra-recitation.

Dānam ātmajñānam.

The gift is Self-knowledge.

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the unfoldment of one’s own divine power.

Look at that.

The body becomes sacred.

Speech becomes sacred.

Instruction becomes sacred.

And the universe itself is recognised as Śakti.

But none of this requires pretending that ordinary empirical distinctions have disappeared.

The body still needs food.

Speech still has meaning.

Teachers still need discernment.

Other people still deserve ethical consideration.

And the universe still appears through countless differentiated forms.

The point is that these forms no longer conceal their conscious source.

In the language of Trika:

The supreme Aham is not ultimately separate from the manifested Idam.

The universal I-consciousness is inseparable from the world appearing as “this.”

And therefore the realised yogi does not necessarily need to flee manifestation.

They recognise its divine nature.

The dancer continues dancing.

The stage remains.

The spectators are watching.

And now the actor knows who he is.

Not the character.

Not the costume.

Not the story.

The conscious reality through which the entire performance becomes possible.

And the most extraordinary part?

According to Trika, that reality was never absent.

Not during bondage.

Not during confusion.

Not during suffering.

Not during forgetting.

The limitation was a contraction of its own power.

The liberation is recognition of its nature.

The entire business has been Śiva.

Playing hide-and-seek.

With Śiva.

Inside Śiva.

Using Śiva’s own Śakti to hide himself from himself.

And then writing seventy-seven sūtras explaining how he did it.

An absolutely outrageous amount of effort for a game he invented.

But apparently infinite freedom does have its hobbies.

