BOOK III — ĀṆAVOPĀYA

The Grand Finale: How Śiva Creates the Universe, Forgets He’s Doing It, Gets Upset About Absolutely Everything, and Finally Remembers That the Whole Bloody Thing Was His Idea.

Part III — Sūtras 3.31–3.45

Previously, on the Śiva Sūtras…
Ladies and gentlemen.

We’ve arrived.

The final fifteen sūtras.

Seventy-seven statements in total. Three great openings of consciousness. An entire metaphysical system compressed into what amounts to a particularly aggressive collection of Sanskrit fortune cookies.

And what a journey it’s been.

In Book I, Vasugupta informed us that we’re actually infinite consciousness.

Marvelous.

In Book II, we discovered that our own mind can become mantra, the alphabet is secretly a goddess, and the entire universe is apparently a rather elaborate manifestation of divine freedom.

Excellent.

Then came Book III.

And the situation deteriorated considerably.

It turns out that this infinite consciousness has somehow become identified with a limited mind, trapped itself in a body, developed an extraordinary number of anxieties, and forgotten the entire arrangement.

Śiva has created saṃsāra.

Entered saṃsāra.

Become confused by saṃsāra.

And apparently started buying books about how to escape saṃsāra.

Absolutely magnificent levels of self-inflicted administrative difficulty.

But then the teaching transformed.

The individual began recognising the divine powers underlying the body, mind and senses.

The world became a cosmic theatre.

The Self was the dancer.

The subtle body was the stage.

The senses were the spectators.

And at 3.30, Vasugupta gave us one of the most extraordinary statements of the entire work:

स्वशक्तिप्रचयोऽस्य विश्वम्।

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the unfoldment of his own Śakti.

The universe.

The entire bloody universe.

Not merely an illusion to be discarded.

Not an alien territory fundamentally separate from consciousness.

The manifestation of the free power of Śiva.

But this creates a philosophical problem.

A BIG one.

If the universe is consciousness manifesting its own power, what happens when the universe changes?

When something appears?

When something disappears?

When you’re happy?

When you’re miserable?

When your body dies?

If everything changes, does consciousness change with it?

And if everything is Śiva, why the hell are we still suffering?

These are precisely the problems Kṣemarāja addresses as we approach the conclusion.

And the final sūtras are more sophisticated than they initially appear.

Because we’re going to discover that the disappearance of an experience is not the disappearance of consciousness, that pleasure and pain need not define the Self, and that the very desire to grasp an object can return consciousness to its contracted standpoint.

Then come the breath, the subtle centre, the dissolution of conditioned identity, and the extraordinary final statement.

A folding back.

A closing.

A return.

Except — and this is the ultimate plot twist — the reality to which consciousness returns was never actually absent.

Let’s finish this thing.

3.31 — Congratulations, You’re Not Just Creating the Universe. You’re Also Maintaining and Deleting It.
स्थितिलयौ ॥३१॥

Sthitilayau.

Literal: Maintenance and dissolution.

That’s it.

Two words.

And Vasugupta has decided that the entire cosmic process is now your responsibility.

Fantastic.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Sthiti — standing, remaining, maintenance, preservation.

Laya — dissolution, absorption, withdrawal.

Sthitilayau is a dual compound referring to those two processes.

Now this sūtra cannot be properly understood by itself.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly makes that clear.

It continues the previous statement:

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the unfoldment of the realised yogi’s own divine power.

So 3.31 adds:

Maintenance and dissolution are also expressions of that same power.

Ah.

Now we’ve got the full cosmic operation.

Sṛṣṭi — manifestation.

Sthiti — maintenance.

Laya — withdrawal.

Creation, preservation and reabsorption.

But let’s be careful with creation.

The Trika doctrine is not necessarily proposing that Śiva manufactures a universe out of something independent of himself.

That would leave us with two fundamental realities.

Śiva.

And the material from which Śiva constructs the universe.

Which would be rather embarrassing for a nondual metaphysics.

Instead, the universe is understood as an ābhāsa.

A manifestation or appearance within and through consciousness.

And Kṣemarāja describes these processes in exactly that framework.

Manifestation is the coming forth of differentiated appearances through kriyāśakti, the divine power of action.

Maintenance is their continuing outward manifestation.

And dissolution is their repose in the conscious subject.

Now here’s the crucial thing.

When an object disappears from experience, it does not follow that consciousness has disappeared.

Imagine looking at a candle.

The flame is present.

You perceive it.

Then somebody blows it out.

The flame disappears.

Your perception of that particular flame ends.

But the consciousness through which its appearance and disappearance were known is not thereby annihilated.

Now don’t take the candle analogy too literally.

Kṣemarāja is not simply explaining ordinary visual persistence.

He’s giving a metaphysical account of the relation between manifestation and the conscious knower.

The point is that both appearance and withdrawal belong to Śakti.

The presence of an object doesn’t establish an independent reality outside consciousness.

And its disappearance doesn’t indicate that consciousness has lost something essential to itself.

The entire movement is understood through the conscious power of manifestation.

Kṣemarāja even invokes the distinction between asti and nāsti.

“It is.”

“It is not.”

The apparent presence and absence of things.

And he connects both with the operations of Śakti.

Now imagine a terrible situation.

Your favourite cup breaks.

Immediately:

“NOOOOO.”

The cup has existed in your experience.

It has persisted through time.

And now the particular arrangement of matter has been destroyed.

And your mind announces:

“A great tragedy has occurred.”

Which may be quite reasonable.

It was a lovely cup.

But from the standpoint of Trika metaphysics, the coming forth, duration and dissolution of phenomena all belong to the dynamic manifestation of consciousness.

The supreme reality is not made complete by the cup’s existence.

Nor is it diminished by its destruction.

And Kṣemarāja extends this to the whole universe.

Manifestation.

Maintenance.

Dissolution.

All are expressions of the same divine power.

He cites the Kālikākrama and a verse concerning the liberated person who recognises pure, independent consciousness as their own I-awareness. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now the extraordinary thing is that 3.30 and 3.31 have together established an entire cosmology.

The world unfolds as Śakti.

It persists through Śakti.

It is withdrawn through Śakti.

Nothing has escaped the sovereignty of the supreme consciousness.

Which sounds magnificent.

But an objection immediately arises.

And it’s a very good objection.

If consciousness is manifesting these different conditions, doesn’t it change?

Doesn’t it become one thing during creation, another during maintenance, and something else during dissolution?

And if it changes in that manner, how can it possibly be the eternal, self-revealing reality we’ve been talking about?

Well.

Vasugupta has an answer.

And it’s absolutely devastating.

3.32 — The Universe Can Disappear. You Haven’t.
तत्प्रवृत्तावप्यनिरासः संवेत्तृभावात् ॥३२॥

Tatpravṛttāv apy anirāsaḥ saṃvettṛbhāvāt.

Literal: Even when those processes occur, there is no loss or disruption, because of the condition of being the conscious knower.

Now this is one of the deepest metaphysical statements in the entire text.

And we need to take it seriously.

Because Kṣemarāja explicitly raises the objection.

Manifestation, maintenance and dissolution seem to be different states.

So if the yogi’s conscious nature is associated with all three, mustn’t that nature undergo corresponding changes?

And the answer is:

No.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Tat — those; referring to the preceding cosmic operations.

Pravṛtti — activity, occurrence, operation.

Api — even.

Anirāsaḥ — no disappearance, rejection, disruption or loss.

Saṃvettṛ — the conscious knower, experiencer.

Bhāvāt — because of the condition or nature of.

So:

Even when the processes of manifestation and withdrawal occur, the realised yogi’s fundamental conscious nature is not lost.

Why?

Because it is the reality of the knower.

And here’s Kṣemarāja’s central argument.

Changes in what is known do not entail the disappearance of the knowing principle.

Imagine a theatre.

One scene shows a mountain.

The next shows a palace.

The next shows a battlefield.

Then the stage goes dark.

All the scenes have changed.

But the fact that one scene ends does not mean the actor’s fundamental capacity to perform has ceased.

Now, with Śiva we have to go further.

Because the conscious knower isn’t a finite actor standing outside the universe.

It is the luminous reality through which all these manifestations become possible.

And Kṣemarāja describes that reality through:

Turya.

The fourth.

Camatkāra.

Astonishing delight.

Vimarśa.

Reflexive self-awareness.

These are not three independent substances.

They describe the self-revealing nature of consciousness in the tradition’s terminology.

And the crucial point is that consciousness does not cease when a particular differentiated object disappears.

Now Kṣemarāja brings in the Spandakārikā.

And this is where the explanation becomes exceptionally beautiful.

He distinguishes:

Kāryatā — the condition of being an effect or product.

Kartṛtva — the condition of agency.

The realm of manifest products is perishable.

The deeper power of agency is not understood as perishing in the same way.

Now, this requires care.

We’re not simply saying your personal ability to do things remains unchanged forever.

Obviously it doesn’t.

You can become tired.

Ill.

Old.

Your physical capacities can change.

The kartṛtva being described concerns the fundamental power of consciousness, not the abilities of the biological organism.

And Kṣemarāja cites a passage from the Spandakārikā explaining a very subtle confusion.

When an activity-oriented effort disappears, an ignorant person may conclude:

“I have disappeared.”

But the disappearance of a particular mental operation is not the disappearance of the conscious reality through which it was experienced.

Think about this.

A thought arises.

You follow it.

Then the thought ends.

What happened?

The thought disappeared.

Did consciousness therefore disappear?

From the Trika standpoint, no.

Now another thought appears.

A memory.

A sensation.

An emotion.

Each is transient.

But the very possibility of experiencing their arising and cessation belongs to the conscious reality.

And here’s the crucial metaphysical distinction.

Consciousness is not simply another object within the sequence of objects.

If it were, we’d need another consciousness to know it.

And then another to know that consciousness.

And we’d have an absolutely catastrophic metaphysical staffing problem.

An infinite sequence of observers.

All requiring supervision.

And nobody actually getting anything done.

Instead, Trika treats consciousness as svaprakāśa — self-luminous.

It is not known by a separate light.

Its very nature is revealing.

And Kṣemarāja makes precisely this point when explaining why the supreme conscious knower doesn’t undergo the disappearance associated with its manifest objects.

He cites the Kālikākrama and Spandakārikā on the imperishable inner conscious nature beneath changing activity. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now this has a very interesting consequence.

The aim of liberation cannot simply be to produce a particular object or mental state that will last forever.

Because the entire structure of objects belongs to changing manifestation.

Instead, liberation concerns recognition of the conscious reality that is not fundamentally exhausted by those changes.

And this is where the teaching becomes extraordinarily elegant.

The universe can manifest.

It can persist.

It can dissolve.

Thoughts can arise.

Thoughts can disappear.

Pleasure can arise.

Pain can arise.

The body can change.

But none of these changes independently determines the fundamental nature of consciousness.

That is Kṣemarāja’s claim.

And it brings us directly to the next sūtra.

Because if pleasure and pain are changing contents of experience, what happens when the yogi stops identifying with them as the ultimate Self?

Oh, this one’s rather important.

3.33 — You’re Experiencing Pain. You Don’t Have to Become the Entire Concept of Pain.
सुखदुःखयोर्बहिर्मननम् ॥३३॥

Sukhaduḥkhayor bahirmananam.

Literal: Regarding pleasure and pain as external.

Now, this is a genuinely subtle teaching.

And one that could be very easily misunderstood.

So let’s get the Sanskrit right.

Sukha — pleasure, happiness, ease.

Duḥkha — pain, suffering, distress.

-yoḥ — dual genitive ending, referring to both.

Bahiḥ — outside, external.

Mananam — considering, apprehending, reflecting.

So:

The yogi apprehends pleasure and pain as something external to their true conscious identity.

Now hang on.

Surely pain is experienced internally?

If you have a headache, you don’t ordinarily experience it as occurring inside a distant mountain.

It’s happening here.

And if you’re happy, that happiness is part of your lived experience.

So what on earth does Kṣemarāja mean by external?

He doesn’t mean pleasure and pain are physically located outside the body.

He’s making a distinction between:

Aham — the fundamental I-consciousness.

And:

Idam — the realm of appearing objects and experiences.

In ordinary life, we don’t merely experience pleasure or pain.

We identify with them.

“I am happy.”

“I am miserable.”

“I am suffering.”

And those statements can become deeply entangled with our sense of identity.

Now, Kṣemarāja says that the realised yogi experiences pleasure and pain differently.

They become apprehended as idantā — this-ness.

Objects or contents of experience.

Just as a blue object appears before awareness, pleasure and pain can appear without becoming the ultimate identity of the conscious subject.

And this is a magnificent distinction.

Because it doesn’t require denying the experience.

The yogi can still experience pleasure.

The yogi can still experience pain.

The body can still respond.

The mind can still register changing conditions.

But the fundamental identity is no longer exhausted by them.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly explains that pleasure and pain arise through contact with objects.

The realised yogi apprehends them as appearances within consciousness, rather than through the ordinary limited identification that makes them seem to define the Self.

Now, a very important qualification.

This does not mean that pain is imaginary, that bodily suffering should be ignored, or that the spiritual ideal is emotional numbness.

Nor is it evidence that advanced practitioners cannot experience physical pain.

The traditional claim concerns the metaphysical identity of the experiencer, not the abolition of ordinary human physiology.

And Kṣemarāja is very precise about this.

He says that the yogi has transcended identification with puryaṣṭaka as the ultimate knower.

Remember puryaṣṭaka?

The eightfold subtle constitution.

Buddhi.

Ahaṃkāra.

Manas.

And the five subtle sensory elements.

Ordinarily, the individual identifies with this mental and subtle apparatus.

And therefore the pleasure and pain occurring through it become appropriated as the defining experiences of the self.

But once that identification is overcome, pleasure and pain no longer have the same power to define the ultimate subject.

Kṣemarāja cites the Īśvarapratyabhijñāvimarśinī and the Spandakārikā to make the distinction between the supreme experiencer and changing pleasure-pain cognitions. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now consider a practical analogy.

You’re sitting quietly.

A difficult emotion arises.

Perhaps sadness.

The ordinary mental structure might immediately become:

“I am sad.”

Then:

“My life is sad.”

Then:

“I have always been sad.”

Then:

“I will always be sad.”

And within a few moments, a passing emotional condition has developed into an entire autobiography.

You’ve gone from one feeling to a forty-seven-volume historical account of your own misery.

Very impressive.

But suppose the emotion is recognised as an appearance within awareness.

The sadness remains.

It doesn’t have to be suppressed.

But the total identification with it can loosen.

There’s sadness.

There’s awareness of sadness.

And the whole experience is not necessarily reducible to the statement:

“This is the entirety of what I am.”

Now, that is a contemporary contemplative analogy, not a complete description of Kṣemarāja’s nondual realisation.

His claim is far more metaphysical.

But it illustrates the shift from identification with a changing experience to recognition of the conscious reality through which it appears.

And here’s something important.

The purpose isn’t to construct a permanently detached observer who sits coldly behind experience.

That would risk turning the Self into another subtle individual subject, separate from the objects it witnesses.

In Trika, the supreme consciousness is inseparable from Śakti.

It is the source of the entire manifest field.

Therefore the recognition is not ultimately a division between a pure observer and an impure world.

It’s the recognition that pleasure and pain are manifest within consciousness without exhausting its nature.

And now comes the extraordinary implication.

If the yogi is no longer inwardly bound by pleasure and pain, what condition have they attained?

Vasugupta has a word.

Kevalī.

The one who stands in the purity of their own conscious nature.

And that’s where we’re going next.

3.34 — Congratulations, You’re Alone. Not Lonely. Metaphysically Alone.
तद्विमुक्तस्तु केवली ॥३४॥

Tadvimuktas tu kevalī.

Literal: But the one liberated from those is kevalī — established in their own pure, independent nature.

Now this is absolutely magnificent.

Because we’ve just learned that the yogi can experience pleasure and pain without identifying their deepest nature with either.

And Vasugupta immediately announces:

Kevalī.

The one who stands alone.

Oh dear.

That sounds rather depressing.

You’ve attained the highest spiritual realization imaginable, and apparently there’s nobody else at the party.

Just you.

And Śiva.

Who is also you.

And the entire universe.

Which is also the manifestation of your own Śakti.

What an extraordinarily confusing social gathering.

But let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Tad — those; referring to pleasure and pain.

Vimuktaḥ — released, liberated, freed.

Tu — but, indeed, marking a distinction.

Kevalī — one established in aloneness, purity, or independence.

And here we need to understand kaivalya.

Because this is a term that appears in multiple Indian philosophical traditions, but it doesn’t always mean the same thing.

In classical Sāṃkhya-Yoga, kaivalya is associated with the isolation of puruṣa from prakṛti.

The conscious principle is distinguished from the material processes with which it had been confused.

But Trika doesn’t accept that ultimate metaphysical separation.

Because for Trika, the world of manifestation is not fundamentally independent of Śiva.

It’s Śiva’s own Śakti.

So Kṣemarāja’s kevalī is not an isolated consciousness hiding from an independently existing universe.

The yogi is established in the pure conscious nature of the Self, no longer bound by identification with the subtle body and its pleasure-pain experiences.

And this is what makes the doctrine so subtle.

The yogi isn’t simply detached from objects because objects are horrible.

The yogi recognises their fundamental conscious nature as not dependent upon those objects.

Now Kṣemarāja makes the point exceptionally strongly.

The liberated yogi is not internally touched even by the residual impressions of pleasure and pain.

Saṃskāra.

The latent impressions left by experience.

And that’s interesting.

Because ordinary pleasure and pain don’t simply disappear the moment the immediate experience ends.

They can leave tendencies.

Memories.

Habits.

Expectations.

A pleasant experience may produce desire for repetition.

A painful experience may produce aversion.

And the individual becomes governed by those residual dispositions.

But Kṣemarāja describes a yogi whose true conscious identity is no longer bound by even those impressions.

Their nature is cinmātra.

Pure consciousness.

Now this is a traditional description of the highest yogic condition, not a clinical claim that liberated persons become incapable of remembering painful experiences.

And it certainly shouldn’t be used to deny someone’s suffering.

The distinction is between the functioning of experience and the ultimate identity of the experiencer.

Kṣemarāja invokes the Kālikākrama, which describes the yogi breaking through the great delusion of duality constructed through the cognitions of pleasure, pain and related mental states.

The problem is not simply that an unpleasant event occurs.

It’s the metaphysical misunderstanding through which the limited cognitive subject is mistaken for the ultimate Self. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now here’s the extraordinary point.

Kevalī doesn’t mean a lonely individual.

It means the yogi is no longer fundamentally dependent on the limitations that define individual subjectivity.

They’re established in the conscious reality from which the appearance of individuality proceeds.

And that reality is not empty of manifestation.

It’s the source of manifestation.

So we’re not talking about a person who has become metaphysically stranded in an infinite void.

We’re talking about consciousness no longer mistaken for the limited forms appearing within it.

Now imagine someone obsessed with maintaining a particular state of happiness.

Every experience is evaluated according to whether it produces pleasure.

They seek pleasant things.

Avoid unpleasant things.

And organise their entire lives around protecting the conditions of satisfaction.

Which, to be fair, is a fairly accurate description of much ordinary human behaviour.

But the difficulty is that pleasure and pain are changing phenomena.

If your identity depends upon preserving pleasant experience, you’re vulnerable to every change in its conditions.

The happiness arrives.

You cling to it.

The happiness disappears.

You suffer.

Then you begin searching for another happiness.

And the entire process continues.

But the kevalī, in Kṣemarāja’s account, has recognised a reality not exhausted by this sequence.

And that changes the meaning of freedom.

Freedom is not simply obtaining permanent pleasant sensations.

It’s the recognition of the conscious reality underlying changing sensations.

And now comes the rather devastating contrast.

Because Vasugupta is about to show us the opposite condition.

The individual who has not recognised their true nature.

The one who remains absorbed in pleasure and pain.

The one whose activity is governed by ignorance.

The one who keeps generating karma.

In other words:

The rest of us.

Lovely.

3.35 — You’ve Been Doing Karma. Karma Has Been Doing You.
मोहप्रतिसंहतस्तु कर्मात्मा ॥३५॥

Mohapratisaṃhatas tu karmātmā.

Literal: But the one compacted in delusion is constituted by karmic activity.

Now this is a wonderful contrast.

And notice that little word again.

Tu.

But.

On the one hand, the liberated yogi.

Kevalī.

On the other hand, the bound individual.

Karmātmā.

The one whose identity is bound up with action.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Moha — delusion, bewilderment, ignorance.

Pratisaṃhata — compacted, gathered densely, thoroughly enveloped.

Karma — action, deed, karmic activity.

Ātmā — self, here the individual understood through involvement in karma.

Now, mohapratisaṃhata is a magnificent word.

A compact mass of delusion.

Not merely:

“I happen to be mistaken about one particular thing.”

No.

The whole structure of ordinary self-identification is saturated with misrecognition.

The individual believes themselves to be a finite subject possessing independent agency.

They experience the world as fundamentally separate.

They act through attraction and aversion.

And those actions produce karmic consequences.

Kṣemarāja explains that the bound person becomes the receptacle of pleasure and pain precisely because they are enveloped in ignorance.

And they are continually implicated in good and bad actions.

Śubha.

Auspicious, good.

Aśubha.

Inauspicious, bad.

Now, here’s something important.

Kṣemarāja isn’t saying morally good and bad actions are indistinguishable.

Quite the opposite.

He explicitly discusses their consequences.

But both belong to the condition of the individual agent acting under limited identification.

The person experiences:

“I am the doer.”

“I performed this action.”

“I must receive the result.”

And the entire karmic machinery becomes involved.

Now, remember the three malas.

Āṇavamala — the primordial sense of limitation.

Māyīyamala — the experience of differentiation.

Kārmamala — the limitation associated with agency and karmic action.

Here we’re seeing the consequences of the third.

The individual isn’t merely experiencing a separate world.

They are acting within it through the conviction of limited doership.

And therefore the consequences of action become part of their conditioned existence.

Kṣemarāja cites the Kālikākrama to explain how ignorance and conceptual construction prevent the individual from recognising the tattvas, beginning with Śiva, as manifestations of consciousness.

Then good and bad conditions arise through the person’s dependence upon ignorance.

And suffering follows from inauspicious states and deeds. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now think about the contrast with the previous sūtra.

The kevalī experiences the body and mind without treating their changing states as the ultimate Self.

The karmātmā identifies with the limited subject and its actions.

One recognises the underlying reality.

The other remains caught within its contracted manifestation.

And the important difference isn’t necessarily visible from the outside.

Two people might perform the same ordinary action.

They might both walk down a road.

Speak to another person.

Eat a meal.

But Kṣemarāja’s philosophical distinction concerns the conscious standpoint from which action occurs.

Not merely the outward movement.

One identifies with the finite agent as ultimate.

The other recognises the universal conscious source.

Of course, this doesn’t remove conventional responsibility.

A person claiming spiritual liberation cannot simply declare themselves exempt from the consequences of harming others.

The metaphysical recognition of Śiva is not an ethical loophole.

But the tradition’s point is that the ordinary structure of karmic bondage depends upon limited self-identification.

And now comes the extraordinary turn.

Because even the person compacted in delusion hasn’t ceased to be Śiva in their fundamental nature.

Which means the possibility of recognition remains.

And Kṣemarāja introduces śaktipāta.

The descent of divine grace.

The intervention of Śiva’s own power through which the individual’s innate freedom becomes disclosed.

And suddenly the whole situation changes.

The compact mass of delusion doesn’t have to remain compact forever.

The limitation can be recognised.

Difference can be transcended.

And then, according to the tradition, something rather spectacular becomes possible.

The creation of another world.

Oh, for God’s sake.

We’re back to supernatural powers.

Apparently Śiva cannot resist showing off.

3.36 — You Beat Duality and Unlock the Universe Editor
भेदतिरस्कारे सर्गान्तरकर्मत्वम् ॥३६॥

Bhedatiraskāre sargāntarakarmatvam.

Interpretive gloss: When difference is overcome, the capacity to create another manifestation arises.

Right.

This is a BIG one.

And we need to handle it properly, because Kṣemarāja is making a genuinely extraordinary claim.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Bheda — difference, division, differentiation.

Tiraskāra — removal, rejection, disappearance, overcoming.

Sarga — creation, manifestation, emanation.

Antara — another, different.

Karmatva — the condition or capacity of acting.

So:

When dualistic difference is overcome, the power of bringing forth another creation is said to arise.

Now, before anybody installs cosmic Minecraft and begins designing a replacement solar system, let’s understand the philosophical progression.

The previous sūtra described the bound individual.

The karmātmā.

Limited by ignorance.

Involved in action.

Subject to karmic consequences.

But Kṣemarāja begins his explanation of 3.36 by introducing the descent of divine grace.

Māheśaśaktipāta.

The descent of the power of the Great Lord.

And through this grace, the individual’s innate svātantrya becomes manifest.

Now, this is where the metaphysics of Trika becomes extraordinarily distinctive.

Because if liberation were simply escape from a separate material universe, the recognition of supreme consciousness would principally concern disengagement from matter.

But in Trika, Śiva is the source of manifestation.

His nature includes the power of creation.

And therefore the recognition of Śiva’s nature is inseparable, in the traditional account, from recognition of that creative freedom.

Kṣemarāja describes the overcoming of dualistic identification with the body, vital energy and other limited conditions.

And then he introduces a hierarchy of conscious subjects.

Sakala.

The ordinary embodied individual.

Pralayākala.

A subtler condition in which certain limitations remain.

And then the higher levels:

Mantra.

Mantreśvara.

Mantramaheśvara.

These are technical categories within the Trika hierarchy of experiencers, associated with progressively less contracted forms of consciousness.

They’re not merely job titles handed out to the best meditators.

Although they sound rather impressive.

“Good morning, what do you do?”

“Mantramaheśvara.”

“Ah. And what does that involve?”

“Cosmic sovereignty.”

“Right. Would you like a receipt?”

Anyway.

Kṣemarāja describes the progressive attainment of these higher conditions as the limitations of difference are overcome.

And the result is the creative capacity described by the sūtra.

Now here’s the part we mustn’t conceal.

Kṣemarāja cites the Svacchandatantra in support of extraordinary powers.

The cited passages claim that the adept can become like Svacchanda, exercise power over divine beings, bestow or remove fear, grant blessings or curses, overcome time’s power, and even bring down mountains.

Yes.

Mountains.

The actual tantric source includes that claim.

Not merely:

“One becomes more psychologically creative.”

Not merely:

“One discovers a fresh perspective on one’s life.”

The commentary really does appeal to supernatural cosmic agency. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, those claims aren’t established physical capabilities.

We shouldn’t pretend they are.

But we also shouldn’t sanitize the historical text into a comfortable modern self-help manual.

The metaphysical reasoning is important.

If universal consciousness possesses unlimited creative freedom, then a yogi identified with that consciousness is said, within the tradition, to participate in its manifesting power.

This is the logic behind the extraordinary claims.

And notice the sequence.

First, bhedatiraskāra.

The overcoming of difference.

Then, sargāntarakarmatva.

The power of another creation.

The power is not presented as simply an advanced technique available to the ordinary ego.

It’s connected with the overcoming of the very dualistic limitation that constitutes ordinary egoic identity.

Which is an important distinction.

Because otherwise the text would simply be saying:

“Remain an ordinary finite person, but acquire increasingly spectacular powers.”

And Kṣemarāja’s entire explanation of bondage would become irrelevant.

The deeper teaching concerns the relationship between knowing, agency and the supreme source.

The extraordinary creative powers are presented as consequences of that recognition in the tantric account.

And now comes a rather fascinating question.

Why should anyone believe consciousness possesses creative power at all?

Forget making mountains fall over.

Forget manifesting additional universes.

What evidence do we have that awareness can generate anything?

Well.

Kṣemarāja points towards something you’ve experienced approximately every night of your life.

Dreaming.

And suddenly the argument becomes very interesting indeed.

3.37 — You Literally Generate Entire Worlds in Dreams. Śiva Would Like a Word.
करणशक्तिः स्वतोऽनुभवात् ॥३७॥

Karaṇaśaktiḥ svato ’nubhavāt.

Literal: The power of creation is known from one’s own experience.

Oh, this is a beauty.

Because Kṣemarāja is about to support his metaphysical argument by appealing to something everyone can investigate.

Their own experience.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Karaṇa — making, producing, creating.

Śakti — power.

Svataḥ — from oneself, from one’s own standpoint.

Anubhavāt — from direct experience.

And the interpretation is:

The creative power of consciousness is already evident through experience.

Now, Kṣemarāja gives two examples.

Saṅkalpa.

Mental construction, imaginative determination.

And:

Svapna.

Dreaming.

And this is where the whole thing becomes extraordinary.

Consider a dream.

You’re asleep.

And suddenly there’s a world.

Buildings.

People.

Mountains.

An entire environment.

Perhaps you’re standing in a city you’ve never visited.

Perhaps you’re speaking to somebody who died years ago.

Perhaps you’re being chased by a six-foot goose wearing a police uniform.

Absolutely terrifying.

But for the duration of the dream, the world appears.

You experience it.

You respond to it.

You may even believe it’s entirely independent of you.

And then you wake up.

And discover that the dream world was not an independently existing physical environment of the same kind as your waking surroundings.

It arose through mental activity.

Now, modern neuroscience and Trika metaphysics would explain this in very different ways.

But Kṣemarāja’s immediate point is about the experience of manifestation.

The mind can present forms, objects and situations that are experienced as a world.

And this shows, within his argument, that consciousness possesses a power of presentation or manifestation.

He connects this with the Īśvarapratyabhijñā, whose account of knowledge and agency appeals to the capacity of living beings to manifest desired forms through cognition.

And this is one of the most fascinating connections in the entire work.

Because the argument isn’t merely:

“Imagine something, therefore you can physically create anything.”

That would be a logical leap.

And a rather dangerous one if you were planning to manifest a helicopter.

The first step is more subtle.

Experience already displays a power of conscious manifestation.

In dreaming, the conscious field presents an elaborate world.

In imagination, objects can be mentally represented without their corresponding external presence.

And these examples become important for the Pratyabhijñā theory of agency.

Now Kṣemarāja takes the argument further.

He says that when this creative power is recognised with intense determination, it can, according to the tantric account, become capable of manifesting things beyond the private domain of imagination.

He even cites the Tattvagarbha on the yogi whose will becomes like a kalpavṛkṣa.

The wish-fulfilling tree.

A tree that grants whatever you desire.

Quite an improvement over ordinary gardening.

But again, distinguish the levels.

The fact that dreams contain generated experiences is observable.

The conclusion that a yogi can therefore manifest public physical objects at will is an additional traditional claim, not something established by the dream example alone.

Kṣemarāja makes both moves, and we should preserve both without conflating them. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now let’s explore the philosophical point more deeply.

Why does the dream matter so much to nondual Śaivism?

Because it destabilises an assumption we make constantly.

That whatever appears to consciousness must be entirely independent of the conscious process through which it is experienced.

In a dream, that assumption becomes questionable.

A whole environment can appear as though it exists independently of the dreamer.

And yet its manifestation is tied to the dreaming condition.

Now, Kṣemarāja uses this to illustrate the larger doctrine of manifestation.

The waking universe, from the supreme standpoint of Trika, is also understood as an expression of consciousness.

But not simply a private dream produced by your personal human mind.

That distinction is absolutely essential.

Universal consciousness is not identical to the ordinary individual imagination.

The supreme Aham is not the contracted ego.

And therefore Trika’s account of cosmic manifestation is not the claim that the world exists merely because your private personality imagines it.

That would be a very crude form of solipsism.

The doctrine is more radical.

The individual mind itself is a manifestation within universal consciousness.

And the world of waking experience is also understood through that universal conscious reality.

The individual doesn’t create Śiva.

Śiva manifests the individual.

And the world.

And their relationship.

Now consider the beauty of that.

Ordinarily you ask:

“How does my consciousness relate to the world?”

But Trika changes the question.

It asks:

How does universal consciousness manifest the distinction between a limited subject and its world?

The first question assumes two fundamental realities and tries to connect them.

The second begins from one reality and explains the appearance of differentiation.

And that is precisely what Kṣemarāja’s interpretation of karaṇaśakti is doing.

The creative power of consciousness is understood as intrinsic to the Self.

And dreaming becomes one accessible illustration of manifestation.

Now, this brings us to an enormous philosophical development.

Because the final sūtras are about to move away from the special question of extraordinary creative powers.

And return to something much more immediate.

The three fundamental movements of manifestation.

Arising.

Remaining.

Subsiding.

And the fourth state of consciousness that underlies them.

But this time, Kṣemarāja is going to connect them to everyday experiences of joy, delight, wonder, and even sensual pleasure.

Which is very tantric indeed.

Apparently the highest metaphysical reality can become apparent while listening to beautiful music.

Excellent.

Finally, a spiritual path with decent acoustics.

3.38 — You Can Recognise Bhairava While Listening to Music. Finally, Some Good News.
त्रिपदाद्यनुप्राणनम् ॥३८॥

Tripadādyanuprāṇanam.

Interpretive gloss: The three states are to be enlivened by their primary source, the fourth.

Now this is a very beautiful sūtra.

And it deliberately returns us to an earlier teaching.

Remember 3.20?

त्रिषु चतुर्थं तैलवदासेच्यम्।

Triṣu caturthaṃ tailavad āsecyam.

The fourth should be poured into the three like oil.

And now:

Tripadādyanuprāṇanam.

The enlivening of the three by their source.

But Kṣemarāja makes an important distinction.

At 3.20, the discussion centred on the pervasion of waking, dreaming and deep sleep by turya.

Here he interprets the three through the dynamics of:

Sṛṣṭi — manifestation.

Sthiti — maintenance.

Laya — withdrawal.

And the primary condition underlying all three is:

Turya.

The fourth.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Tri — three.

Pada — state, position, condition.

Ādi — first, primary, fundamental.

Anuprāṇana — enlivening, vitalising, animating.

The instruction concerns the infusion of the three conditions with the awareness of their supreme source.

Now, here’s the truly fascinating part.

Kṣemarāja associates the three with distinct orientations of experience.

The outward movement towards objects.

The involvement with those objects.

And their inward withdrawal.

Manifestation.

Maintenance.

Dissolution.

Consider what happens when you encounter something beautiful.

A piece of music, for example.

First, the music begins.

A sound emerges.

Your attention turns towards it.

Sṛṣṭi.

Manifestation.

Then the music continues.

You become absorbed in it.

Perhaps the melody becomes extraordinarily moving.

Sthiti.

Maintenance.

Then the phrase ends.

The final note fades.

The experience subsides.

Laya.

Withdrawal.

Now here’s the extraordinary claim.

These three movements can become occasions for recognising the fourth.

Turya.

The underlying conscious reality.

And Kṣemarāja says that even when this reality is concealed by Māyā, it can flash forth for an instant.

Like lightning.

Vidyutvat.

Like a flash of lightning.

Now this is a gorgeous image.

Because sometimes an experience produces a sudden opening.

A moment of extraordinary joy.

A feeling of fullness.

A profound sense of wonder.

And for an instant, the ordinary structures of mental limitation can seem to relax.

Kṣemarāja says the practitioner can use such moments to enliven their recognition of the supreme state.

But the aim is not merely to chase increasingly intense pleasures.

That would simply return us to rāga.

Wanting.

Clinging.

Searching for completeness in another object.

Rather, the yogi attends to the underlying conscious reality disclosed through the experience.

And now Kṣemarāja cites the Vijñānabhairava.

This is particularly important because the examples are surprisingly diverse.

He invokes practices involving intense delight, the joy of meeting a beloved person after a long separation, the pleasure of eating and drinking, the experience of music, and even the heightened states associated with sexual enjoyment.

Yes.

The traditional text really does include sensual and erotic examples.

This is Tantra.

We’re not going to pretend it exclusively concerns sitting quietly with a nice cup of herbal tea.

But the philosophical point isn’t simply indulgence.

It’s the recognition of the conscious fullness that can become apparent through powerful experiences.

And Kṣemarāja also invokes the Spandakārikā on intense states such as anger, joy, uncertainty and rapid movement.

The common principle is that an unusual intensity of experience may disclose a deeper conscious power when approached through the appropriate contemplative understanding. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now let’s be precise.

This is not a claim that every intense emotion automatically reveals enlightenment.

Nor that sexual pleasure, anger or excitement are inherently liberating.

The relevant point is the conscious standpoint of the practitioner and the possibility of recognising the source of experience.

Here’s the beauty of it.

Imagine listening to a piece of music that moves you profoundly.

For a moment, the usual sense of being a separate subject evaluating an external object may become less prominent.

The experience becomes extraordinarily full.

Now, you can chase that fullness as something produced exclusively by the external music.

Or you can investigate the conscious reality through which the fullness is experienced.

And Kṣemarāja’s argument points towards the latter.

The delight becomes an occasion for recognising the source.

But it must not remain merely an isolated flash.

Remember the lightning.

The momentary opening.

The practitioner is instructed to cultivate the continuity of recognition.

To enliven the successive phases of experience with the conscious reality of turya.

This is why the word anuprāṇana is so beautiful.

It’s not simply suppressing experience.

It’s enlivening it.

The ordinary processes of manifestation, maintenance and withdrawal become pervaded by recognition.

And now we reach the next sūtra.

Which extends the method even further.

Not merely to particular mental states.

Not merely to extraordinary moments of joy.

But to the body.

The senses.

And the external world.

Because apparently no part of experience is permitted to remain outside this recognition.

The entire universe is getting spiritually saturated.

3.39 — Don’t Leave Your Enlightenment on the Meditation Cushion, You Absolute Muppet.
चित्तस्थितिवच्छरीरकरणबाह्येषु ॥३९॥

Cittasthitivac charīrakaraṇabāhyeṣu.

Interpretive gloss: Just as in the states of mind, so too in the body, the senses and external objects.

Now this is a beautiful continuation.

And the Sanskrit is intentionally dependent upon the previous sūtra.

In other words, the phrase:

Tripadādyanuprāṇanam

continues to govern the meaning.

The fourth should enliven the three states.

And that enlivening should extend beyond the mind into the body, senses and external manifestations.

Let’s unpack the compound.

Citta — mind.

Sthiti — condition, state.

Vat — like, as in.

Śarīra — body.

Karaṇa — instrument; here, the sense faculties.

Bāhya — external objects.

So:

As with mental states, so with the body, senses and the external world.

And here’s Kṣemarāja’s point.

It is not enough for the yogi to become established in recognition only during inward contemplation.

The same recognition must gradually extend throughout outward experience.

Kramāt kramam.

Step by step.

Now that phrase is important.

Because it tells us we’re dealing with a progressive integration.

The practitioner doesn’t simply announce:

“I have understood nonduality, therefore my entire life is now permanently liberated.”

That would be a rather bold claim.

Especially if they immediately become furious about a delayed cup of tea.

Instead, the recognition cultivated inwardly is extended into ordinary embodied activity.

The body.

The senses.

The appearances of the world.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly says this occurs through the power of sustained inward vimarśa.

The reflexive awareness of consciousness.

Now, think about the philosophical significance.

At the beginning of Book III, the individual was bound through identification with the mind.

Then the tattvas were contemplated.

The body became a field of practice.

The senses became spectators of the cosmic drama.

And gradually the practitioner recognised the supreme conscious source.

But now that recognition must become pervasive.

The body isn’t to be regarded as a fundamentally alien obstacle.

The senses aren’t fundamentally outside Śakti.

And the external world isn’t an independent reality standing beyond the power of consciousness.

All become objects of contemplation within the same nondual understanding.

Kṣemarāja cites a verse from the Vijñānabhairava in which the practitioner is instructed to contemplate the entire universe, or their own body, as filled with the delight of the Self.

And from that contemplative fullness, supreme bliss is said to arise. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now imagine this in ordinary life.

You’re walking down a road.

Your legs are moving.

Your body is balancing.

Your senses are registering the environment.

You hear footsteps.

Feel the ground.

See the changing light.

Ordinarily, you might be entirely absorbed in these activities or in the thoughts accompanying them.

But the practitioner is invited to recognise the conscious reality through which the entire field of experience appears.

Not merely the thoughts.

Not merely the inner state.

The whole embodied manifestation.

And here’s the important distinction.

This doesn’t require pretending that the body and world are physically identical.

Nor does it mean a practitioner loses the ability to distinguish between themselves and other people.

The nondual claim concerns the ultimate conscious source of experience, not the elimination of every conventional distinction.

And the process is gradual.

The recognition first becomes clear inwardly.

Then extends outward.

And eventually, within the traditional ideal, the difference between inward absorption and outward engagement no longer obscures the supreme reality.

This is what makes the entire progression so extraordinary.

The yogi begins by withdrawing attention from objects.

Then recognises the conscious source.

Then returns to objects.

But now the objects are understood differently.

The world hasn’t necessarily changed its outward appearance.

The standpoint has changed.

And the experience of manifestation becomes inseparable from awareness of its source.

Now, after all this, you’d imagine the final six sūtras would simply conclude with congratulations.

Perhaps:

“You’ve done it.”

“Well done.”

“Please collect your liberation certificate at reception.”

But no.

Vasugupta is about to return to the bound individual.

Again.

Because one final problem remains.

Desire.

The sense of incompleteness.

The movement of attention outward towards something imagined to provide fulfilment.

And this is where the text becomes absolutely devastating.

Because after discussing the highest states of recognition, it identifies a wonderfully ordinary mechanism through which consciousness becomes contracted again.

Wanting.

The little voice that says:

“I’ll be complete when I get that.”

Oh dear.

Śiva has opened a shopping account.

3.40 — Congratulations, You’ve Reinstalled Wanting. Welcome Back to Saṃsāra.
अभिलाषाद्बहिर्गतिः संवाह्यस्य ॥४०॥

Abhilāṣād bahirgatiḥ saṃvāhyasya.

Literal: Through desire, the one carried along moves outward.

Oh, this is absolutely devastating.

Because after everything we’ve learned about the extraordinary nature of consciousness, Vasugupta brings us back to a very simple human predicament.

I want something.

That’s it.

The entire cosmic drama begins again.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Abhilāṣa — desire, longing, wanting.

-āt — because of, arising from.

Bahis — outward.

Gatiḥ — movement, going.

Saṃvāhya — one who is carried along, conveyed or driven onward.

Now that last word is particularly important.

Saṃvāhya.

The one who is carried.

Kṣemarāja interprets this as the bound individual who is carried from one womb or embodied condition to another through the powers constituting conditioned existence.

The paśu.

The bound being.

The karmātmā we encountered in 3.35.

And the powers carrying this individual include the kañcukas, mental faculties, senses, subtle elements and gross elements.

An entire metaphysical conveyor belt.

Absolutely extraordinary.

You’ve got the limited individual standing on the conveyor belt, looking around, asking:

“Where are we going?”

And the machinery replies:

“Another embodied existence.”

“Why?”

“Desire.”

“Can I get off?”

“Have you recognised your true nature?”

“Not really.”

“NEXT WOMB, PLEASE.”

Now that’s the traditional saṃsāric framework, not an empirically demonstrated mechanism of rebirth.

But Kṣemarāja’s philosophical explanation is very precise.

He connects abhilāṣa with āṇavamala.

The primordial limitation of the individual.

And specifically with the feeling:

Apūrṇammanyatā.

The conviction of incompleteness.

Let’s unpack that.

A-pūrṇa — not full, incomplete.

Manyatā — considering, regarding, thinking oneself to be.

The individual experiences:

“I am incomplete.”

And from that fundamental orientation arises desire.

“I need this.”

“I must obtain that.”

“I cannot be fulfilled until something changes.”

And therefore consciousness becomes outwardly directed.

Bahirgati.

The movement towards objects.

Now here’s the extraordinary subtlety.

Trika doesn’t simply say that every ordinary preference is morally evil.

Wanting a meal when you’re hungry is part of embodied life.

The deeper issue is the metaphysical identification through which the subject experiences itself as fundamentally deficient.

The limited individual mistakes the acquisition of objects for the ultimate recovery of completeness.

And this is where the whole system becomes remarkably coherent.

Remember rāga among the five kañcukas?

The limitation associated with desire.

The supreme fullness of consciousness is experienced through the condition of lack.

And that apparent lack drives the individual outward.

The person searches among objects for a completeness that, according to the doctrine, belongs to their own supreme conscious nature.

Now imagine somebody convinced that happiness lies in obtaining one particular thing.

A relationship.

A possession.

A professional achievement.

A certain status.

And perhaps they obtain it.

Wonderful.

For a moment, satisfaction arises.

But then another desire appears.

Or anxiety about losing what they’ve acquired.

Or comparison with somebody who has something better.

And the cycle begins again.

The problem isn’t necessarily the object.

It’s the structure of seeking completeness from something conceived as ultimately external.

Kṣemarāja is talking about this structure at a much deeper metaphysical level.

He describes the limited individual as becoming outwardly oriented precisely because they fail to recognise the inward reality of turya as their own Self.

He cites the Svacchandatantra and Kālikākrama on ignorance, desire, differentiated mental construction and the continuation of suffering. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

And here’s the beautiful philosophical reversal.

The object may be perfectly ordinary.

A cup of tea.

A beautiful place.

A person you love.

But the underlying mode of experience can differ radically.

One person encounters the object through the conviction:

“I am incomplete, and this thing must complete me.”

Another encounters it without mistaking the object for the ultimate source of fulfilment.

The difference is not necessarily in the outward action.

It’s in the fundamental identification of the subject.

And now Vasugupta gives us the solution.

Not simply suppressing all desire by force.

Not constructing a new ego whose proudest achievement is having no desires.

But the disclosure of fullness through stable recognition of consciousness.

And when that recognition becomes established, the condition of being the limited individual is said to dissolve.

Which sounds alarming.

Because we’re about to have a sūtra involving the destruction of the jīva.

But don’t worry.

Nobody needs to die.

This is about the end of a particular mode of self-identification.

3.41 — Your Limited Self Has Been Cancelled. Nobody Actually Died.
तदारूढप्रमितेस्तत्क्षयाज्जीवसङ्क्षयः ॥४१॥

Tadārūḍhapramites tatkṣayāj jīvasaṅkṣayaḥ.

Interpretive gloss: For one whose awareness is established in that supreme state, the ending of desire brings the dissolution of conditioned individuality.

Now this is absolutely central to the ending of the entire Śiva Sūtras.

Let’s unpack it carefully.

Tad — that; referring to the supreme condition of turya.

Ārūḍha — ascended to, established in.

Pramiti — knowledge, conscious apprehension.

Tat-kṣayāt — through the destruction of that; referring back to desire.

Jīva — the living, conditioned individual.

Saṅkṣayaḥ — complete dissolution, cessation, exhaustion.

Now, Kṣemarāja explicitly explains both instances of tad.

The first refers to the supreme conscious state.

The second refers to the desire described in 3.40.

So the sequence is:

Awareness becomes established in the supreme reality.

The fundamental craving arising from incompleteness subsides.

And the limited condition of the jīva comes to an end.

But here’s the crucial question.

What exactly is being destroyed?

Not the physical body.

Not the existence of consciousness.

Not the possibility of ordinary experience.

What ceases is the identification of the Self with the limited puryaṣṭaka as the ultimate knowing subject.

That is Kṣemarāja’s explanation.

The jīva as the contracted, desire-driven individual ceases to define the Self.

And now we encounter an absolutely beautiful expression.

Ātmārāmatā.

Delight in one’s own Self.

Resting in the fullness of the Self.

The person no longer requires an external object to establish their fundamental completeness.

And Kṣemarāja contrasts this with the continual outward movement of the bound individual.

Now, something fascinating happens.

He cites a tantric passage comparing liberation to waking from a dream.

When you awaken, the objects of the dream are no longer experienced as the actual environment in which you’re situated.

The dream city.

The dream characters.

The dream catastrophe.

Their former apparent authority is withdrawn.

And similarly, the yogi who recognises the supreme reality no longer apprehends saṃsāra through the same fundamental delusion.

But this doesn’t mean that the physical universe disappears for everyone else.

Nor that the enlightened person ceases to perceive ordinary objects.

The analogy concerns the transformation of their conscious standpoint.

The world is no longer experienced as a realm fundamentally separate from Śiva.

And here’s a remarkable detail.

Kṣemarāja’s cited material actually uses the term nirvāṇa.

Yes.

Nirvāṇapada.

The state of nirvāṇa.

Now, we should not automatically equate this with the Buddhist understanding of Nibbāna.

The metaphysical frameworks are substantially different.

In the Buddhist traditions, nirvāṇa is associated with the cessation of greed, hatred and delusion, without positing an eternal universal Śiva-self.

In Trika, Kṣemarāja interprets liberation through recognition of the supreme conscious Self, inseparable from divine freedom and Śakti.

The resemblance in vocabulary is real.

But so is the philosophical difference.

And this is an excellent example of why we need to read the actual Sanskrit rather than assuming a word has exactly the same meaning everywhere.

Kṣemarāja describes the yogi as established in kaivalya because they are no longer carried along by the senses and subtle elements.

The individual no longer remains a helpless passenger within the machinery of conditioned existence. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now here’s the extraordinary result.

The body may continue.

The senses may function.

The world may appear.

But the fundamental sense of being an incomplete individual driven outward by desire has been overcome.

And this takes us to one of the most beautiful metaphysical corrections in the entire work.

Because if the jīva has been dissolved, why is the liberated person still walking around?

Surely their body should vanish?

Shouldn’t they immediately ascend into some luminous cosmic dimension?

Well.

Vasugupta has thought of that.

And the answer is wonderful.

The body remains like a cloak.

3.42 — You’re Free, but You’ve Still Got to Wear the Meat Suit.
भूतकञ्चुकी तदा विमुक्तो भूयः पतिसमः परः ॥४२॥

Bhūtakañcukī tadā vimukto bhūyaḥ patisamaḥ paraḥ.

Interpretive gloss: Then, though still clothed in the elements, one is liberated, supremely complete, and like the Lord.

Oh, this is absolutely magnificent.

Because Kṣemarāja explicitly raises the objection.

If the conditioned individual has been dissolved, shouldn’t the body disappear as well?

And yet liberated yogis are described as continuing to live.

How is that possible?

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Bhūta — element.

Kañcuka — covering, cloak, sheath.

Bhūtakañcukī — one clothed in the elements.

Tadā — then.

Vimuktaḥ — liberated.

Bhūyaḥ — abundantly, fully, further.

Patisamaḥ — equal to the Lord.

Paraḥ — supreme, transcendent, complete.

Now, bhūtakañcukī is a magnificent word.

The one wearing the elements as a cloak.

And that’s precisely the image Kṣemarāja uses.

The body is constituted by the elements.

It remains present.

But the realised yogi no longer identifies those elements as the ultimate Self.

The body is like clothing.

Not in the sense that it should be neglected or physically disregarded.

But in the sense that the Self is not fundamentally reducible to the garment it wears.

Imagine an actor wearing a costume.

A king’s robe.

A crown.

A sword.

The costume helps manifest the role.

But the actor isn’t fundamentally identical with the costume.

They can continue wearing it while knowing perfectly well that it isn’t their ultimate identity.

And here we return, once again, to the cosmic theatre.

The dancer.

The stage.

The spectators.

The costume.

The entire performance.

But now the actor has recognised the conscious reality underlying the role.

And therefore the costume no longer conceals their identity.

This is Kṣemarāja’s understanding of liberated embodiment.

Jīvanmukti.

Liberation while living.

Now here’s an important detail.

Kṣemarāja explicitly connects 3.42 back to 3.26.

Remember:

शरीरवृत्तिर्व्रतम्।

Śarīravṛttir vratam.

The continuation of the body is the vow.

The realised yogi’s body continues to function.

And that continuation is no longer understood as evidence of fundamental bondage.

The body may remain as a residual condition of manifestation.

But the yogi is not governed by identification with it as the supreme subject.

Kṣemarāja invokes several tantric sources, including the Kularatnamālā, Netratantra and Kulasāra, in support of this liberated condition.

Some of those sources also claim that genuine recognition can bring liberation immediately and that the realised adept may communicate liberation to others.

These are traditional theological and yogic claims, not empirically established powers. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now consider the philosophical beauty.

The limited individual has not been transformed into a second, independently existing god.

The universal conscious nature has been recognised.

And therefore the body is no longer the fundamental boundary of the Self.

But the body can continue.

And the universe can continue.

The problem wasn’t simply the presence of embodiment.

It was the misidentification of the Self with the limited embodied subject.

And here’s the remarkable consequence.

Liberation doesn’t necessarily require physical disappearance.

Which is excellent news.

Because otherwise the only proof of enlightenment would be that somebody had vanished.

And there’d be a very awkward administrative problem at every monastery.

“Where’s the teacher?”

“Enlightened.”

“Right. When will he be back?”

“Never.”

“Well, who’s teaching Sanskrit on Tuesday?”

But Kṣemarāja’s liberated yogi remains capable of living.

Teaching.

Speaking.

Acting.

The body persists.

The consciousness is recognised.

The play continues.

And now we reach the final three sūtras.

We’re almost finished.

But Vasugupta has one last technical problem to resolve.

Why does the body remain connected to life and breath after liberation?

And the answer brings us back to the deepest relationship between consciousness and prāṇa.

Because apparently Śiva has been breathing through the entire performance.

3.43 — Even When You’re Liberated, Your Body Keeps Breathing. Shocking, I Know.
नैसर्गिकः प्राणसम्बन्धः ॥४३॥

Naisargikaḥ prāṇasambandhaḥ.

Literal: The connection with vital energy is natural.

Now this is an extraordinarily subtle continuation.

And we need to understand why Vasugupta places it here.

The previous sūtra described the liberated yogi as still clothed in the elements.

The body remains.

And now the question arises:

Why?

If the fundamental conditions of bondage have been overcome, why does the embodied condition continue?

Kṣemarāja answers:

Naisargikaḥ prāṇasambandhaḥ.

The connection with prāṇa is natural.

Let’s unpack it.

Naisargika — natural, belonging to nature, arising spontaneously.

Prāṇa — vital energy, life-breath.

Sambandha — connection, relationship.

But here’s the important point.

Kṣemarāja isn’t merely making the ordinary biological observation that living organisms breathe.

He’s interpreting prāṇa through the metaphysics of Śakti.

And this becomes absolutely fascinating.

The supreme consciousness possesses svātantrya.

Freedom.

Through that freedom, it manifests the universe.

And part of this manifestation involves adopting the standpoint of a contracted subject.

A perceiver.

A living individual.

And prāṇa, within the tantric framework, belongs to the manifestation of that living subjectivity.

Now, Kṣemarāja describes the Goddess of consciousness as wishing to manifest the wonderful diversity of the universe.

And in doing so, she assumes a contracted aspect associated with vital activity.

The subject and its objects become manifest through this process.

The relation between consciousness and vital energy therefore belongs to the divine freedom of manifestation.

It’s not necessarily an alien imprisonment imposed upon Śiva from outside.

And this is where the doctrine becomes extremely elegant.

The same power that makes embodied life possible can continue to operate after the yogi has recognised its source.

The body remains alive.

Breathing continues.

The faculties function.

But the yogi is no longer fundamentally identified with them.

Now Kṣemarāja brings in a very interesting tantric correspondence.

The three principal subtle channels.

Iḍā.

Piṅgalā.

Suṣumṇā.

And these are connected with symbolic structures involving:

Moon.

Sun.

Fire.

The known.

The knower.

Knowledge.

And the three temporal dimensions:

Past.

Present.

Future.

Now, we have to keep two levels distinct.

These are traditional tantric correspondences concerning the subtle body and cosmic manifestation.

They should not be mistaken for anatomical structures verified by modern physiology.

But within the tradition, their metaphysical significance is substantial.

They express how the power of consciousness becomes articulated through vitality, temporality and differentiation.

And Kṣemarāja goes even deeper.

He relates prāṇa to the processes of emission and return.

Visarga.

Emission.

Āpūraṇa.

Filling, inward replenishment.

And he connects these with the continuous movement of life.

Now imagine breathing.

An outgoing movement.

An incoming movement.

An apparent alternation.

A rhythm.

Within the tantric model, this rhythm symbolises the larger movement of manifestation and withdrawal.

The same structure encountered throughout the text.

Sṛṣṭi.

Manifestation.

Sthiti.

Maintenance.

Laya.

Withdrawal.

Now the cosmic process is understood through the rhythm of vitality.

And Kṣemarāja cites the Svacchandatantra on the relationship between prāṇa and apāna, as well as the association of the phoneme ha with spontaneous vital movement.

He also cites Bhaṭṭa Kallaṭa’s Tattvārthacintāmaṇi, where consciousness is described as becoming vital energy. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now, here’s why this matters.

The body’s continued vitality doesn’t invalidate liberation.

The natural connection with prāṇa can remain.

But the yogi understands that connection through the supreme conscious reality.

And this is a very different perspective from assuming that liberation requires the abolition of ordinary biological life.

The body can breathe.

The senses can function.

The world can appear.

And recognition can remain.

The important distinction is not whether the vital processes continue.

It’s whether consciousness is still fundamentally misidentified with them.

Which brings us to the penultimate sūtra.

And this one is exceptionally technical.

Because Vasugupta is going to discuss the subtle channels, the central reality, and the highest form of contemplative awareness.

And Kṣemarāja is going to make an extraordinary claim.

That the yogi established in the innermost conscious reality no longer depends upon the distinctions between the left, right and central channels.

In other words:

After an entire book discussing the metaphysical plumbing system, we’re finally going beyond the pipes.

Thank God.

3.44 — Stop Obsessing Over Your Nostril Channels. The Real Centre Is Consciousness.
नासिकान्तर्मध्यसंयमात्किमत्र सव्यापसव्यसौषुम्नेषु ॥४४॥

Nāsikāntarmadhyasaṃyamāt kim atra savyāpasavyasauṣumneṣu.

Interpretive gloss: Through intense contemplation of the innermost centre of the vital power, what more need be said of the right, left and central channels?

Now.

Look at that Sanskrit.

Vasugupta has clearly decided that if we’re finishing the text, we’re going to make the reader work for it.

He’s gone from:

Caitanyam ātmā.

Two words.

To this magnificent phonemic railway accident.

But the meaning is beautiful.

Let’s break it down.

Nāsikā — ordinarily, the nose or nostril; but Kṣemarāja gives it a specialised interpretation here.

Antar — inner.

Madhya — middle, centre.

Saṃyama — disciplined concentration or restraint; here, intense inner contemplative awareness.

Kim atra — what here? What more is to be said?

Savya — right-side, as Kṣemarāja interprets it in this context.

Apasavya — left-side.

Sauṣumṇa — relating to Suṣumṇā, the central subtle channel.

Now, the first thing to understand is that Kṣemarāja doesn’t simply treat nāsikā as the physical nose.

This is absolutely crucial.

He interprets it as the vital power moving in a winding or curved manner through the subtle channels.

Prāṇaśakti.

And the antar of that vital power refers to its inner conscious reality.

Then the madhya is the innermost centre.

The supreme vimarśa.

The reflexive I-consciousness of Śiva.

Now we’re getting somewhere.

Because the entire system of subtle channels has been associated with the differentiation of vitality.

Right.

Left.

Centre.

The movements of prāṇa.

The rhythms of embodied experience.

But Kṣemarāja is directing attention towards something more fundamental.

The conscious power underlying that entire system.

And he quotes the Kālikākrama on vimarśa as the supreme Śakti of the highest conscious Lord.

The crucial distinction is between working with the differentiated movements of vital energy and recognising the conscious source underlying them.

Now, to be clear, this is a traditional contemplative interpretation, not a reason to force your breathing, hold your breath for dangerous lengths of time, or manipulate the nostrils in some elaborate improvised practice.

Kṣemarāja’s point here is not that physiological strain produces liberation.

It’s the intensity of inner awareness.

And he glosses saṃyama through:

Antarnibhālanaprakarṣa.

The intensification of inward contemplation or awareness.

What a magnificent expression.

The practitioner turns towards the innermost conscious reality.

Not merely the movement of breath.

Not merely the sensation of energy.

Not merely the subtle channels.

But the self-revealing awareness through which those phenomena become manifest.

And here’s where the entire metaphysics comes together.

The conscious reality is not merely another thing located inside the body.

It’s not a tiny glowing object hidden somewhere between your eyebrows.

Nor is it a mysterious anatomical organ discovered through sufficiently advanced breathing exercises.

It’s the underlying self-aware power of consciousness.

And Kṣemarāja says that through continuous awareness of that reality, the yogi attains the supreme samādhi that shines throughout all states.

Nirvyutthāna.

Without return to the ordinary distracted standpoint.

And now we arrive at a particularly beautiful citation.

Kṣemarāja invokes the Vijñānabhairava on the relationship between knower and known.

All embodied beings participate in cognition.

But the yogi’s distinction lies in their attentiveness to the relationship between the conscious subject and its objects.

Not merely the objects.

Not merely an abstract theory of a subject.

But the fundamental relation through which manifestation occurs. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now consider the philosophical significance.

At the beginning of Book III, the individual experienced:

“I am this mind.”

And therefore the world appeared as a separate domain of objects.

But now the yogi has traced the entire structure of embodiment back towards the innermost conscious reality.

The body.

The senses.

The breath.

The subtle channels.

The mental apparatus.

All have been examined as manifestations of Śakti.

And now the supreme source becomes the object — or, more accurately, the reality — of recognition.

The movement is from the most differentiated structures of individuality to the conscious power that makes their manifestation possible.

And here’s the most beautiful part.

The yogi doesn’t necessarily have to abolish all the differentiated processes.

Breathing can continue.

The body can remain.

The senses can operate.

But the ultimate reality is no longer concealed by them.

And this is why Kṣemarāja concludes that the yogi’s supreme samādhi can shine throughout every state.

Not merely in the absence of ordinary experience.

But through its manifestation.

And now.

Ladies and gentlemen.

We’ve arrived.

The final sūtra.

Number seventy-seven.

And Vasugupta has chosen an ending so beautiful, so compressed, and so perfectly aligned with the entire work that it almost makes you want to start reading the whole bloody thing again.

Which, as it turns out, is rather appropriate.

Because the final word is about returning.

Folding back.

The coming together of inward and outward awareness.

The universe returning to its conscious source.

Except that source never ceased to be its reality.

Here we go.

3.45 — The Greatest Plot Twist: You Were Śiva the Entire Bloody Time.
भूयः स्यात्प्रतिमीलनम् ॥४५॥

Bhūyaḥ syāt pratimīlanam.

Interpretive gloss: Again and again, there is the complete reunion of inward and outward awareness in the supreme Self.

And that’s it.

The final sūtra.

The seventy-seventh statement.

Vasugupta has spent the entire text explaining the nature of consciousness, the structure of bondage, the powers of Śakti, and the means of liberation.

And he ends with:

Bhūyaḥ syāt pratimīlanam.

Now we need to be exceptionally careful with this one.

Because the word pratimīlana is doing enormous philosophical work.

Let’s unpack the Sanskrit.

Bhūyaḥ — again, repeatedly, abundantly.

Syāt — may be, should be, comes to be.

Pratimīlanam — closing together, folding together, reunion or complete integration.

And Kṣemarāja gives it a very particular interpretation.

He connects pratimīlana with two complementary movements.

Nimīlana.

Closing inward.

The withdrawal of consciousness from differentiated manifestation into its own nature.

And:

Unmīlana.

Opening outward.

The manifestation of the universe through that same consciousness.

Now think about what that means.

Ordinarily, spiritual practice can appear to involve a movement away from the world.

You withdraw.

You close your eyes.

You turn attention inward.

You seek the source.

And in some traditions, that inward withdrawal becomes the principal image of liberation.

But Trika gives us something more comprehensive.

Because the supreme reality isn’t merely a state of withdrawal.

Śiva possesses the freedom of manifestation.

And therefore the outward opening is not ultimately separate from the inward source.

Nimīlana and unmīlana are not two different absolute realities.

They’re complementary movements of one consciousness.

And pratimīlana is the integration of both.

Now, Kṣemarāja’s explanation is extraordinary.

He says the universe arises from the essential nature of consciousness.

Then, for the yogi established in supreme yoga, the universe is folded back into that conscious reality.

But this doesn’t necessarily mean the physical world disappears.

The crucial movement is the disappearance of the residual impression of duality.

Bhedasaṃskāra.

The latent impression of difference.

That impression falls away.

And the yogi recognises the universe as not fundamentally separate from consciousness.

Now here’s the beautiful thing.

Kṣemarāja explains that the inward closing and outward opening become aspects of the same supreme recognition.

When awareness is directed inward, the yogi recognises consciousness.

When the universe appears outwardly, the yogi recognises that appearance as the manifestation of consciousness.

The distinction between inward and outward no longer conceals their fundamental unity.

And this is precisely why pratimīlana is such a perfect ending.

Because the entire text has been concerned with the relation between the Self and manifestation.

At the beginning:

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

Then:

ज्ञानं बन्धः।

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Limited knowledge is bondage.

And now, at the end:

भूयः स्यात्प्रतिमीलनम्।

Bhūyaḥ syāt pratimīlanam.

Repeated integration into the supreme conscious reality.

The apparent separation between subject and object has been overcome.

But not through the permanent annihilation of the world.

Rather, through recognition of the world as the manifestation of the Self.

And Kṣemarāja makes the central point absolutely explicit.

He says the yogi’s Śiva-nature is not something new.

Nāpūrvam.

Not newly produced.

Rather:

Svabhāva eva.

It is their own essential nature.

And this is the ultimate revelation.

The yogi doesn’t become Śiva by creating an entirely new metaphysical identity.

The yogi recognises the Śiva-nature that was always fundamental.

Māyā had obscured it through the power of differentiated mental construction.

The practitioner had experienced themselves as limited.

The body appeared to define the Self.

The senses appeared to encounter a separate world.

The mind became absorbed in differentiated cognition.

Desire produced the movement outward.

And the individual wandered through conditioned existence.

But the supreme nature of consciousness had never ceased to be the reality underlying the entire process.

And Kṣemarāja explicitly says that the methods described throughout the work serve to reveal this already-existing Śiva-nature.

Not manufacture it.

Reveal it.

And now comes a magnificent metaphor from the Svacchandatantra, quoted by Kṣemarāja.

The image is of fire.

Fire emerging from wood.

Once kindled, the fire becomes brilliantly luminous.

And the liberated Self is compared to this emergence.

The fire isn’t created as an entirely alien substance imposed from outside.

The analogy is used to explain the disclosure of a nature previously concealed by limiting conditions.

And Kṣemarāja cites the Svacchandatantra on the yogi who has become free from the impurities and limiting powers.

Even while remaining within manifestation, the liberated person is no longer bound by it in the former manner.

Then he concludes the entire commentary by explaining the final sūtra’s deepest point.

The Śiva-nature of the yogi was not previously absent.

It was obscured through the self-concealing power of Māyā.

And the practices have brought it to manifestation in recognition. Sanskrit & Trika Shaivism

Now stop and think about the structure of the whole journey.

The first sūtra:

Consciousness is the Self.

The final sūtra:

The inward and outward movements of manifestation are reunited in consciousness.

We’ve gone from the declaration of the supreme reality to its recognition throughout the entire universe.

And that is the full movement of the Śiva Sūtras.

Not away from existence.

Not towards an entirely new substance.

Not from an independent human being into a separate divine being.

But from misrecognition to recognition.

From contraction to the disclosure of freedom.

From the limited aṇu to the recognition of the universal Self.

And the final instruction isn’t:

“Destroy the world.”

It’s not:

“Suppress every thought.”

It’s not:

“Abandon every relationship.”

It’s not:

“Become an exceptionally impressive individual.”

It’s something far more radical.

Recognise the conscious reality through both the inward withdrawal and outward manifestation of the universe.

And there’s an exquisite irony here.

Because the whole text has been explaining how to become what, according to the tradition, you never actually ceased to be.

You were Śiva.

You experienced limitation.

You searched for Śiva.

You studied Śiva.

You meditated upon Śiva.

You worshipped Śiva.

You asked teachers about Śiva.

And eventually, the distinction between the seeker and the sought was overcome.

The ultimate metaphysical hide-and-seek.

With one player.

And an infinite number of disguises.

THE GRAND FINALE — ALL SEVENTY-SEVEN ŚIVA SŪTRAS
What Vasugupta Actually Just Told Us
Right.

The book is finished.

Seventy-seven sūtras.

Three sections.

And an astonishingly unified philosophical argument.

But now that we’ve followed everything, we can finally stand back and see the whole structure.

Because there’s something extraordinarily elegant about it.

The Śiva Sūtras don’t simply begin with a human being and tell them how to become divine.

They begin by declaring the nature of the divine Self.

Then they explain why that nature appears concealed.

And finally they show how recognition can occur through several distinct modes of practice.

The movement is not from one independent substance to another.

It’s from contracted awareness to recognition of its universal nature.

And the three upāyas approach this from different standpoints.

BOOK I — ŚĀMBHAVOPĀYA
Twenty-two sūtras. The way of Śiva, associated with the supreme power of will.

The book opens with the most extraordinary declaration in the entire text.

चैतन्यमात्मा।

Caitanyam ātmā.

Consciousness is the Self.

That’s the foundation.

Everything that follows depends upon it.

But immediately comes the problem.

ज्ञानं बन्धः।

Jñānaṃ bandhaḥ.

Limited knowledge is bondage.

Why?

Because the universal consciousness experiences itself through a contracted standpoint.

The individual comes to identify with a limited body and mind.

The powers of Śakti manifest differentiation.

And the infinite experiences itself as finite.

But the supreme reality has not actually been replaced by a second substance.

Contraction is itself a possibility of divine freedom.

Then comes the great breakthrough.

उद्यमो भैरवः।

Udyamo bhairavaḥ.

The sudden upsurge of awareness is Bhairava.

Not the manufacture of a new Self.

But the immediate disclosure of the supreme consciousness.

And the universe is withdrawn into its source.

The three ordinary states become transparent to the fourth.

Waking.

Dreaming.

Deep sleep.

Turya.

The conscious reality underlying their changes.

And then the astonishing transformation begins.

The yogi recognises divine will as Umā.

The entire visible universe becomes the body of the Self.

The mind is brought into union with the Heart.

Limited individuality loses its governing power.

And the yogi recognises:

I am Śiva.

Not the inflated personal ego.

But the universal I-consciousness that is the source of manifestation.

And then comes the magnificent statement:

विस्मयो योगभूमिकाः।

Vismayo yogabhūmikāḥ.

Wonder is the ground of yoga.

The universe becomes astonishing.

Not because the yogi has discovered a new collection of exotic objects.

But because the nature of manifestation is recognised.

And finally the book culminates in the Great Lake.

Mahāhrada.

The supreme reservoir of consciousness.

The source of mantra’s potency.

The entire first section is concerned with the direct recognition of what consciousness fundamentally is.

And the method is associated with the immediacy of divine will.

Śāmbhavopāya.

The way of Śiva.

The cosmic equivalent of realising you’ve been looking for your spectacles while wearing them.

Except the spectacles are infinite consciousness.

And the person searching for them is also infinite consciousness.

A rather difficult optometry appointment.

BOOK II — ŚĀKTOPĀYA
Ten sūtras. The way of Śakti, associated with the transformative power of knowledge.

Now we encounter something extraordinary.

The mind itself becomes a means of liberation.

चित्तं मन्त्रः।

Cittaṃ mantraḥ.

The mind is mantra.

Not the ordinary wandering mind simply because it happens to think.

But mental activity transformed through contemplation of its supreme source.

The practitioner directs attention towards the conscious power underlying thought.

Effort becomes effective through the living movement of contemplation.

And the secret of mantra is disclosed.

Not merely the phonemes.

Not merely mechanical recitation.

But the living power of consciousness.

Then comes the warning.

Extraordinary mental experiences can remain within Māyā.

Visionary phenomena do not necessarily constitute supreme knowledge.

And the yogi must not mistake the accumulation of siddhis for liberation.

Then supreme knowledge arises naturally.

Khecarī.

The power moving freely in the space of consciousness.

And the guru becomes the means of instruction.

Not merely through transmitting information, but through revealing the nature of the conscious source.

Then comes the great awakening to Mātṛkā.

The mother of the phonemes.

The entire Sanskrit alphabet understood through the powers of Śakti.

And the great mantra of universal I-consciousness.

Aham.

I.

Not the little ego.

But the fullness of consciousness in which the universe appears.

Then the body becomes a sacrificial offering.

Knowledge becomes food.

The entire apparatus of differentiated cognition is reabsorbed into the recognition of the supreme.

But the final sūtra warns that knowledge may subside.

Recognition can be interrupted.

The dreamlike world of limitation can return.

And that brings us naturally to the third book.

Because not everyone can remain established in the immediate or knowledge-centred modes of recognition.

Some practitioners require more articulated supports.

And therefore we enter the world of the aṇu.

The limited individual.

BOOK III — ĀṆAVOPĀYA
Forty-five sūtras. The way of the limited individual, employing definite supports and practices.

And this is where the entire philosophical system becomes embodied.

The book opens:

आत्मा चित्तम्।

Ātmā cittam.

The Self is mind.

Not ultimately.

But from the contracted standpoint of ordinary individual experience.

The universal consciousness appears identified with the mental apparatus.

Buddhi.

Ahaṃkāra.

Manas.

And so knowledge becomes bondage.

Māyā manifests differentiation through the tattvas.

The limitations of agency, knowledge, desire, time and necessity structure the individual condition.

But the practitioner begins working with those very structures.

The body.

The breath.

The senses.

The elements.

The mind.

And through contemplative practice, their relation to consciousness becomes increasingly apparent.

But the warning remains:

Supernatural powers are not identical with liberation.

The yogi must overcome delusion.

Recognise innate knowledge.

And discover the universe as the radiance of consciousness.

Then comes the theatrical masterpiece.

नर्तक आत्मा।

Nartaka ātmā.

The Self is the dancer.

रङ्गोऽन्तरात्मा।

Raṅgo ’ntarātmā.

The inner Self is the stage.

प्रेक्षकाणीन्द्रियाणि।

Prekṣakāṇīndriyāṇi.

The senses are the spectators.

The entire world becomes the performance of consciousness.

And through refined intelligence, the yogi recognises the underlying reality.

सिद्धः स्वतन्त्रभावः।

Siddhaḥ svatantrabhāvaḥ.

Freedom is attained.

But the recognition must be sustained.

Attention turns towards the seed.

Bījāvadhānam.

The supreme Śakti.

The Great Lake is entered.

Innate knowledge becomes continuous.

The powers of Mātṛkā are recognised.

Turya is infused throughout the three ordinary states.

The practitioner returns to the world.

And the world becomes a means of recovering recognition.

Then the transformation of life itself begins.

शरीरवृत्तिर्व्रतम्।

Śarīravṛttir vratam.

The body’s continuation is the vow.

कथा जपः।

Kathā japaḥ.

Speech is mantra-recitation.

दानमात्मज्ञानम्।

Dānam ātmajñānam.

The gift is Self-knowledge.

And then the great universal statement:

स्वशक्तिप्रचयोऽस्य विश्वम्।

Svaśaktipracayo ’sya viśvam.

The universe is the unfoldment of the yogi’s own divine power.

The world is not fundamentally separate from consciousness.

Its manifestation, maintenance and withdrawal all belong to Śakti.

The conscious knower is not annihilated by the disappearance of its objects.

Pleasure and pain become appearances rather than ultimate definitions of the Self.

The yogi transcends limited identification.

And the jīva, as the bound subject, ceases to determine the nature of awareness.

Yet the body can remain.

Vitality can continue.

The senses can function.

And the entire manifest world can still appear.

Finally, the yogi recognises the innermost conscious reality underlying the movements of prāṇa.

And the text concludes with:

भूयः स्यात्प्रतिमीलनम्।

Bhūyaḥ syāt pratimīlanam.

Repeated integration of inward and outward consciousness.

The universe folds back into its source.

And opens forth from that same source.

The dancer has recognised the dancer.

The performance continues.

But the fundamental misunderstanding has ended.

The Actual Heart of the Entire Teaching
And now, after all seventy-seven sūtras, we can finally articulate the central philosophical structure.

Prakāśa.

The luminous, self-revealing nature of consciousness.

Vimarśa.

Consciousness’s reflexive awareness of itself.

Svātantrya.

The intrinsic freedom of that consciousness.

Śakti.

The manifesting power inseparable from Śiva.

Saṃkoca.

The contraction through which universal consciousness appears as limited individuality.

Mala.

The limiting impurities associated with that contracted condition.

Māyā.

The power through which differentiation is experienced without recognition of its underlying unity.

Upāya.

The means through which recognition becomes possible.

Pratyabhijñā.

Recognition.

And ultimately:

Aham.

The supreme I-consciousness.

Not the little individual personality.

But the universal reality in which the entire manifestation of Idam, the world appearing as “this,” is included.

And this is why Trika isn’t simply a doctrine of escaping the world.

The world is not fundamentally something other than Śiva.

It’s the expression of Śakti.

The problem is not manifestation itself.

The problem is misrecognition.

And therefore liberation is not the production of a second reality beyond the universe.

It is the recognition of the supreme conscious nature through which the universe becomes manifest.

That is the profound difference.

And that is why the final sūtra could hardly have been more perfect.

Because the inward and outward movements are reunited.

The source and manifestation are recognised through their nondual relationship.

The apparently finite self is no longer mistaken for the entirety of what one is.

And the ultimate reality has not become something new.

It has been recognised.

The Last Scene
Imagine the Himalayas.

Kashmir.

A quiet valley.

A man sitting beside a great lake.

The mountains reflected in its surface.

The water perfectly still.

For a moment there is no wind.

No sound.

No movement.

Only the extraordinary stillness of the landscape.

And then a breeze passes across the water.

Ripples form.

The reflections move.

The mountains appear broken into a thousand fragments.

The whole surface becomes an intricate pattern.

Now imagine somebody looking at the lake.

They become fascinated by the reflections.

One fragment appears.

Another disappears.

They follow each movement.

They distinguish one form from another.

They forget the lake.

And eventually begin arguing with the reflections.

“This mountain is mine.”

“That mountain is better.”

“This reflection is beautiful.”

“That reflection is terrible.”

“I must obtain the perfect reflection.”

And the whole drama of individual existence begins.

Now, of course, the water has never ceased being water.

The ripples have not created a second substance.

The reflections haven’t become independent of the surface.

But the observer has become absorbed in the differentiated appearances.

And then something changes.

The observer recognises the lake.

The ripples remain.

The reflections remain.

The mountains appear.

The breeze continues.

But the fundamental nature of the scene has become apparent.

And this is the final movement of the Śiva Sūtras.

Not the permanent destruction of the ripples.

Not the rejection of the reflected mountains.

Not the impossible task of keeping the water perfectly still forever.

But the recognition of the reality through which the entire display appears.

And there, Kṣemarāja brings us back to the beginning.

The nature of consciousness was never absent.

The limitation belonged to its apparent self-concealment.

The practices have not manufactured a new Śiva.

They have disclosed the Śiva-nature that the tradition regards as fundamental.

And now the final statement returns.

भूयः स्यात्प्रतिमीलनम्।

Bhūyaḥ syāt pratimīlanam.

Again and again, the inward and outward movements of consciousness are united in recognition.

The universe opens.

The universe closes.

The dance continues.

But the dancer knows.

And there’s a rather beautiful irony.

We’ve spent seventy-seven sūtras investigating the nature of the Self.

We’ve travelled through the entire hierarchy of manifestation.

We’ve encountered divine powers, subtle bodies, supernatural claims, philosophical arguments, the alphabet, the breath, and the workings of bondage and liberation.

And the final conclusion is that the supreme conscious nature was never missing.

Which means that the entire book has been an extraordinarily elaborate attempt to explain something that, in the tradition’s own account, is already the fundamental reality of the one doing the reading.

Seventy-seven sūtras.

An entire metaphysical universe.

And Śiva’s final revelation is essentially:

“Mate. It’s been you the whole bloody time.”

॥ शिवम् ॥
Iti śivam.

Thus, Śiva. Thus, the auspicious conclusion.

End of the Śiva Sūtras — all 77 sūtras completed.

